internal val videoCatalogPart0: List<String> = listOf(
    "[{\"vid\":\"fNk_zzaMoSs\",\"title\":\"Vectors | Chapter 1, Essence of linear algebra\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"The first chapter of 3Blue1Brown's Essence of Linear Algebra series, introducing vectors with animated geometric visuals. It begins a series that builds intuition for linear algebra from the basics.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN's go-to recommendation for learning linear algebra intuition, repeatedly credited with making concepts click and complementing textbooks.\",\"content_type\":\"course\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"linear algebra\",\"categories\":[\"Pure mathematics\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"vectors\",\"linear algebra\",\"visual intuition\",\"3blue1brown\",\"machine learning math\"],\"quote\":\"the most welcoming and informative introduction to the topic I've ever seen\",\"recommenders\":17,\"score\":25.0},{\"vid\":\"FITJMJjASUs\",\"title\":\"Ruby Conf 12 - Y Not- Adventures in Functional Programming by Jim Weirich\",\"channel\":\"Confreaks\",\"summary\":\"Jim Weirich's RubyConf 2012 talk derives the Y combinator step by step, live-coding in Ruby lambdas. It builds up an understanding of functional programming and lambda calculus from first principles.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it repeatedly as one of the best and most entertaining programming talks, and as the talk that makes the Y combinator click, even for people who do not use Ruby.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"y combinator\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Computability & complexity theory\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"functional programming\",\"lambda calculus\",\"ruby\",\"live coding\",\"jim weirich\"],\"quote\":\"perhaps the most entertaining and interesting technical talk I've ever seen.\",\"recommenders\":17,\"score\":22.5},{\"vid\":\"NGFhc8R_uO4\",\"title\":\"Indistinguishable From Magic: Manufacturing Modern Computer Chips\",\"channel\":\"Andor Gafotas\",\"summary\":\"A HOPE09 talk on how modern computer chips are manufactured, from 22nm transistors to ultra-clean fabs and the tricks used to pattern tiny features with light. It frames chipmaking as hacking electrical engineering and physics.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly calls it the best in-depth introduction to IC manufacturing, a sense-of-awe talk by an independent engineer, while wishing for an updated version.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"semiconductor manufacturing\",\"categories\":[\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\",\"Hacker culture & free software\"],\"tags\":[\"chip fabrication\",\"lithography\",\"semiconductors\",\"hope conference\",\"transistors\"],\"quote\":\"This talk on YouTube is the most in depth explanation of IC manufacuring I have seen. I highly recommend it to anyone, no matter their background.\",\"recommenders\":19,\"score\":22.0},{\"vid\":\"Unzc731iCUY\",\"title\":\"How to Speak\",\"channel\":\"MIT OpenCourseWare\",\"summary\":\"Patrick Winston's long-running MIT lecture on public speaking teaches heuristic rules for talks. It covers how to start, use time and place, boards, props and slides, and how to inform, persuade and finish.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the canonical, near-universally recommended lecture on giving talks and presentations.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"public speaking\",\"categories\":[\"University lectures & courses\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\",\"Education & learning\"],\"tags\":[\"presentations\",\"communication\",\"mit\",\"patrick winston\",\"slides\",\"storytelling\"],\"quote\":\"drop what you're doing and watch it now\",\"recommenders\":17,\"score\":21.0},{\"vid\":\"VMj-3S1tku0\",\"title\":\"The spelled-out intro to neural networks and backpropagation: building micrograd\",\"channel\":\"Andrej Karpathy\",\"summary\":\"Andrej Karpathy's first Neural Networks: Zero to Hero lecture, which builds micrograd, a tiny autograd engine, from scratch in Python. It explains backpropagation and neural network training step by step, assuming only basic Python and some high-school calculus.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the best starting point for really understanding backprop and neural nets, because Karpathy explains the why with intuition and builds everything in code.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"backpropagation and neural networks\",\"categories\":[\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"micrograd\",\"autograd\",\"backpropagation\",\"neural networks\",\"python\",\"andrej karpathy\"],\"quote\":\"It's especially useful because he explains the why and provides intuitive explanations instead of just talking about the what and how.\",\"recommenders\":17,\"score\":21.0},{\"vid\":\"aMcJ1Jvtef0\",\"title\":\"8 Bit & '8 Bitish' Graphics-Outside the Box\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"Mark Ferrari, the LucasArts background artist, explains in a GDC 2016 talk how he draws 8-bit graphics. He covers color cycling and palette shifting to create complex animated scenery without frame animation.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as a master class in limited-palette pixel art and palette cycling, from an artist behind Loom and Monkey Island.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"8-bit pixel art\",\"categories\":[\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Visual arts, photography & architecture\",\"Creative coding & generative art\"],\"tags\":[\"palette cycling\",\"pixel art\",\"mark ferrari\",\"lucasarts\",\"deluxe paint\",\"gdc\"],\"quote\":\"I recommend this talk to anyone who is interested in the details and history of working on limited colour palette games!\",\"recommenders\":13,\"score\":20.0},{\"vid\":\"qCGofLIzX6g\",\"title\":\"How Python was Shaped by leaky Internals, Armin Ronacher, Flask framework\",\"channel\":\"Видео с мероприятий {speach!\",\"summary\":\"Armin Ronacher, creator of Flask, explains how CPython's internals and C API leak into the language's semantics. Using examples like the complexity of the add operation, he shows why Python is hard to optimize or reimplement.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is HN's standard answer to why Python cannot easily get much faster, and is recommended by people who work on JITs and alternative runtimes.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"python internals\",\"categories\":[\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Software performance & optimization\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"cpython\",\"performance\",\"jit\",\"c api\",\"language semantics\",\"armin ronacher\"],\"quote\":\"This excellent talk describes how the semantics of the \\\"add\\\" operation are much more complex than generally described.\",\"recommenders\":15,\"score\":19.5},{\"vid\":\"DovunOxlY1k\",\"title\":\"AT&T Archives: Similiarities of Wave Behavior (Bonus Edition)\",\"channel\":\"AT&T Tech Channel\",\"summary\":\"A Bell Labs film in which Dr. J.N. Shive uses a mechanical wave machine to demonstrate reflection, standing waves, resonance and impedance matching. He includes some equations but relies mostly on real-world examples a layperson can follow.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly calls it the clearest introduction to waves and transmission-line behavior, better than modern explainers.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"wave behavior and transmission lines\",\"categories\":[\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"wave machine\",\"standing waves\",\"impedance matching\",\"bell labs\",\"signal reflection\"],\"quote\":\"By far the best introduction to the subject of waves of any sort.\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":19.0},{\"vid\":\"JTfhYyTuT44\",\"title\":\"In Search Of A Flat Earth\",\"channel\":\"Folding Ideas\",\"summary\":\"Dan Olson's 76-minute essay on flat earth belief, built around a hike to a lake to test earth's curvature. It examines the community, how YouTube promoted it, and how it connects to wider conspiracy thinking and QAnon.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the definitive, compassionate documentary on why people fall into conspiracy theories.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"conspiracy theories\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Gaming culture & analysis\",\"Religion, belief & skepticism\"],\"tags\":[\"flat earth\",\"folding ideas\",\"qanon\",\"youtube algorithm\",\"misinformation\",\"dan olson\"],\"quote\":\"probably the best documentary I have seen on conspiracy theories.\",\"recommenders\":17,\"score\":19.0},{\"vid\":\"NNnIGh9g6fA\",\"title\":\"1. Introduction to Human Behavioral Biology\",\"channel\":\"Stanford\",\"summary\":\"The opening lecture of Robert Sapolsky's Stanford course on Human Behavioral Biology, explaining the course premise and how to avoid categorical thinking. It starts a roughly 25-lecture series on how biology, genes, hormones and environment shape behavior.\",\"why_hn\":\"One of the most consistently recommended lecture series on HN, praised as fascinating, funny and eye-opening about human behavior across many scales.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"behavioral biology\",\"categories\":[\"University lectures & courses\",\"Neuroscience & the brain\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"sapolsky\",\"neuroscience\",\"genetics\",\"evolution\",\"stanford\",\"hormones\"],\"quote\":\"Best course I've ever done.\",\"recommenders\":15,\"score\":19.0},{\"vid\":\"PHe0bXAIuk0\",\"title\":\"How The Economic Machine Works by Ray Dalio\",\"channel\":\"Principles by Ray Dalio\",\"summary\":\"Ray Dalio's 30-minute animated video explains how the economy works through credit, debt, interest rates, and short- and long-term cycles. It is based on his template for understanding the economy.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as the best layperson overview of debt, credit and economic cycles.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"macroeconomics\",\"categories\":[\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\"],\"tags\":[\"ray dalio\",\"debt cycles\",\"credit\",\"interest rates\",\"economy\"],\"quote\":\"it’s the best layperson explanation of debt I’ve ever seen\",\"recommenders\":18,\"score\":19.0},{\"vid\":\"8bZh5LMaSmE\",\"title\":\"RailsConf 2014 - All the Little Things by Sandi Metz\",\"channel\":\"Confreaks\",\"summary\":\"Sandi Metz takes an ugly section of conditional code and refactors it into a few simple objects, bridging object-oriented theory and practice. The talk includes her take on the Gilded Rose refactoring kata and the idea that duplication is cheaper than the wrong abstraction.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as a top-tier programming talk for learning refactoring and for the wrong-abstraction principle, recommended repeatedly over a decade.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"refactoring object-oriented code\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"refactoring\",\"sandi metz\",\"wrong abstraction\",\"gilded rose\",\"ruby\",\"object-oriented design\"],\"quote\":\"this talk captures all the important values in a single example.\",\"recommenders\":15,\"score\":18.5},{\"vid\":\"spUNpyF58BY\",\"title\":\"But what is the Fourier Transform?  A visual introduction.\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"3Blue1Brown's animated visual introduction to the Fourier Transform, showing how a signal breaks into frequency components.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN's default recommendation for understanding the Fourier transform intuitively; many say it was the explanation that made it click.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"fourier transform\",\"categories\":[\"Information theory & signal processing\",\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"fourier transform\",\"3blue1brown\",\"signal processing\",\"visual math\",\"frequencies\"],\"quote\":\"this was the first thing that ever really made sense to me\",\"recommenders\":16,\"score\":18.5},{\"vid\":\"yYAw79386WI\",\"title\":\"Around The Corner - How Differential Steering Works (1937)\",\"channel\":\"US Auto Industry\",\"summary\":\"A 1937 GM-produced film explaining how the automobile differential lets wheels turn at different speeds around a corner. It uses clear animations and cutaway demonstrations to build the idea from first principles.\",\"why_hn\":\"Held up as a marvel of pedagogy and the best explanation of differentials, recommended again and again.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"automotive differentials\",\"categories\":[\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\"],\"tags\":[\"differential\",\"gears\",\"vintage film\",\"mechanisms\",\"cars\"],\"quote\":\"this video explaining differentials is from 1937 and is a marvel of pedagogy\",\"recommenders\":17,\"score\":18.5},{\"vid\":\"JvBT4XBdoUE\",\"title\":\"The Soul of Erlang and Elixir • Sasa Juric • GOTO 2019\",\"channel\":\"GOTO Conferences\",\"summary\":\"Sasa Juric explains what makes Erlang and Elixir suited to server-side systems, focusing on the BEAM concurrency model rather than syntax or ecosystem. He demonstrates processes, supervision, and fault tolerance.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is HN's favorite pitch for Elixir and the BEAM, credited with convincing many people to adopt them.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"erlang and elixir concurrency\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Distributed systems\"],\"tags\":[\"elixir\",\"erlang\",\"beam\",\"fault tolerance\",\"supervision trees\",\"actor model\"],\"quote\":\"Watch this if you want to understand why so many people fall in love with it.\",\"recommenders\":16,\"score\":17.5},{\"vid\":\"SCAGc-rkIfo\",\"title\":\"Essential Ideas for Parents\",\"channel\":\"Jason Salotti\",\"summary\":\"Dr. Russell Barkley's roughly three-hour lecture on ADHD for parents of affected children. It explains what ADHD is and how the ADHD brain differs.\",\"why_hn\":\"Repeatedly called the best way to understand ADHD, and recommended to anyone before commenting on it.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"adhd\",\"categories\":[\"Mental health & neurodiversity\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"adhd\",\"russell barkley\",\"parenting\",\"executive function\",\"neurodevelopment\"],\"quote\":\"Here's the best explanation I've ever seen of the subject\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":17.0},{\"vid\":\"SxdOUGdseq4\",\"title\":\"\\\"Simple Made Easy\\\" - Rich Hickey (2011)\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Rich Hickey's Strange Loop 2011 keynote distinguishing 'simple' (not intertwined) from 'easy' (familiar or near at hand). It argues that software complexity comes from complecting things and that we should pick simple constructs.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the canonical talk on software complexity, shared constantly and recommended to every programmer regardless of language.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software design complexity\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"rich hickey\",\"simplicity\",\"complecting\",\"clojure\",\"software design\",\"strange loop\"],\"quote\":\"is a must-watch (even if you never touch Clojure)\",\"recommenders\":14,\"score\":17.0},{\"vid\":\"dEaecUuEqfc\",\"title\":\"The Quantum Conspiracy: What Popularizers of QM Don't Want You to Know\",\"channel\":\"Google TechTalks\",\"summary\":\"Ron Garret's Google Tech Talk argues that quantum mechanics is no harder to understand than relativity and that popular accounts wrongly rely on the Copenhagen interpretation. He presents measurement as entanglement and explains phenomena like the quantum eraser.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters repeatedly recommend it as the talk that finally made quantum mechanics click, especially for programmers.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"quantum mechanics interpretation\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum physics\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"quantum mechanics\",\"entanglement\",\"measurement\",\"copenhagen interpretation\",\"quantum eraser\"],\"quote\":\"This is the video that finally made it click for me[1].\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":17.0},{\"vid\":\"rStL7niR7gs\",\"title\":\"Try to Rule Fairly and You'll Be Overthrown\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey's animated adaptation of The Dictator's Handbook, explaining how rulers stay in power by keeping a small group of key supporters happy rather than ruling fairly. It lays out selectorate theory and how resources shape regimes.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the standard short summary of how power and political incentives work, applied to governments, companies and workplaces.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"political power and selectorate theory\",\"categories\":[\"Politics & history (other)\",\"World history\",\"Management, leadership & organizations\"],\"tags\":[\"rules for rulers\",\"dictators handbook\",\"selectorate theory\",\"political science\",\"cgp grey\"],\"quote\":\"CGP Grey's excellent Rules for Rulers video\",\"recommenders\":15,\"score\":17.0},{\"vid\":\"vtIzMaLkCaM\",\"title\":\"LEADERSHIP LAB: The Craft of Writing Effectively\",\"channel\":\"UChicago Social Sciences\",\"summary\":\"Larry McEnerney of the University of Chicago Writing Program gives a graduate-student session on writing effectively. His core idea is that readers care about value to them, not the writer's thinking process.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN users repeatedly say it changed how they think about writing, calling it a masterclass useful far beyond academia.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"effective writing\",\"categories\":[\"Literature & writing craft\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"writing\",\"larry mcenerney\",\"academic writing\",\"communication\",\"readers\"],\"quote\":\"it's a masterclass in understanding what writing actually is, and what it does for you\",\"recommenders\":15,\"score\":17.0},{\"vid\":\"8aGhZQkoFbQ\",\"title\":\"What the heck is the event loop anyway? | Philip Roberts | JSConf EU\",\"channel\":\"JSConf\",\"summary\":\"Philip Roberts explains the JavaScript event loop, call stack, callbacks and asynchronous behavior with live visualizations using a tool he built. It demystifies terms like non-blocking and single-threaded.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters repeatedly name it as the best explanation of the JS event loop and the first thing to watch before tackling async code.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"javascript event loop\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"event loop\",\"async javascript\",\"call stack\",\"callbacks\",\"visualization\"],\"quote\":\"The tool he created to display the event loop in real time is fantastic.\",\"recommenders\":16,\"score\":16.5},{\"vid\":\"aircAruvnKk\",\"title\":\"But what is a neural network? | Deep learning chapter 1\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"3Blue1Brown's first chapter on deep learning explains what neurons and layers are and the math underlying a basic neural network, using handwritten digit recognition with visual animations.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the standard first stop for learning neural networks, praised for making the math intuitive.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"neural networks\",\"categories\":[\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"deep learning\",\"3blue1brown\",\"gradient descent\",\"visualization\",\"machine learning basics\"],\"quote\":\"the visualisations and explanations of the math/concepts are so amazing\",\"recommenders\":16,\"score\":16.5},{\"vid\":\"xAoljeRJ3lU\",\"title\":\"A Better Default Colormap for Matplotlib | SciPy 2015 | Nathaniel Smith and Stéfan van der Walt\",\"channel\":\"Enthought\",\"summary\":\"SciPy 2015 talk by Nathaniel Smith and Stefan van der Walt on choosing a new default colormap for Matplotlib. It covers why Jet misleads, how perceptual color spaces work, and how viridis was designed.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN's go-to recommendation for colormap and color-perception questions; commenters call it funny, brief, clear and a favorite talk.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"perceptual colormaps\",\"categories\":[\"Data visualization & information design\",\"Data science & statistics\"],\"tags\":[\"viridis\",\"matplotlib\",\"colormaps\",\"color perception\",\"data visualization\",\"colorblindness\"],\"quote\":\"This hilarious SciPy2015 talk[1] convinced me to switch to the Parula color scheme.\",\"recommenders\":15,\"score\":16.5},{\"vid\":\"5f9R9MtkpqM\",\"title\":\"George Lakoff: Moral Politics\",\"channel\":\"University of California Television (UCTV)\",\"summary\":\"Cognitive scientist George Lakoff lectures on how political debates are framed through language and metaphors tied to values, and on the moral worldviews behind progressive and conservative politics. It was given in 2005 at UC San Diego.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters repeatedly say it changed how they hear political speech and recommend it for understanding why opponents are not simply stupid.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"political framing and metaphor\",\"categories\":[\"US politics & elections\",\"Language & linguistics\",\"Consciousness & cognition\"],\"tags\":[\"george lakoff\",\"framing\",\"political language\",\"metaphor\",\"moral politics\"],\"quote\":\"Changed how I listen - to anyone - forever.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":16.0},{\"vid\":\"7J52mDjZzto\",\"title\":\"Heat Pumps: the Future of Home Heating\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"Technology Connections explains how heat pumps work as reversed air conditioners and why they can deliver several times the heat of resistive heating per unit of energy. It also tackles common myths such as poor cold-weather performance.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN's default recommendation for a friendly, thorough introduction to heat pumps and why they are so efficient.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"heat pumps\",\"categories\":[\"Power grid, heating & buildings\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"home heating\",\"hvac\",\"efficiency\",\"refrigeration cycle\",\"technology connections\"],\"quote\":\"Start here for a good introduction to heat pumps and how they are so efficient\",\"recommenders\":17,\"score\":16.0},{\"vid\":\"XXBxV6-zamM\",\"title\":\"Micro Men - 720p (2009)\",\"channel\":\"gimpymoo\",\"summary\":\"Micro Men is a 2009 BBC comic drama about the early 1980s British home computer boom. It follows the rivalry between Clive Sinclair and Acorn founder Chris Curry over the BBC Micro contract and the ZX Spectrum.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it often as an entertaining and authentic dramatization of Sinclair versus Acorn, the story from which ARM grew.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"british home computing history\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Tech company stories & documentaries\"],\"tags\":[\"clive sinclair\",\"acorn\",\"bbc micro\",\"zx spectrum\",\"docudrama\"],\"quote\":\"'Micro Men' is an excellent TV drama\",\"recommenders\":15,\"score\":16.0},{\"vid\":\"aVwxzDHniEw\",\"title\":\"The Beauty of Bézier Curves\",\"channel\":\"Freya Holmér\",\"summary\":\"Freya Holmer explains how Bezier curves work, from the helper-line construction to their uses in animation, text rendering and shapes. It uses visuals from her Unity Shapes plugin and was a SoME1 contest winner.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly calls it one of the best math and graphics explainers on YouTube, praised for bridging game dev and math.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"bezier curves\",\"categories\":[\"Computer graphics & rendering\",\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Animation & visual effects\"],\"tags\":[\"bezier\",\"splines\",\"computer graphics\",\"animation\",\"game dev\",\"math visualization\"],\"quote\":\"it's genuinely one of the best math/graphics/explainer videos ever made\",\"recommenders\":16,\"score\":16.0},{\"vid\":\"eJ3RzGoQC4s\",\"title\":\"The Century of the Self (Full Adam Curtis Documentary)\",\"channel\":\"David Lessig\",\"summary\":\"Adam Curtis' multi-part documentary on how Freud's ideas and Edward Bernays' public relations shaped consumer culture and political control. It argues that business and government learned to manufacture desires.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it often and strongly as an eye-opening account of marketing psychology and the engineering of consumer society.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"propaganda and consumer culture\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"adam curtis\",\"edward bernays\",\"freud\",\"advertising\",\"psychology\"],\"quote\":\"For the first time in my life I feel like I understood the “why” of how things work in American society.\",\"recommenders\":13,\"score\":16.0},{\"vid\":\"o_AIw9bGogo\",\"title\":\"The Tragedy of systemd\",\"channel\":\"linux.conf.au\",\"summary\":\"FreeBSD developer Benno Rice traces the history of init and bootstrap systems at linux.conf.au 2019 to explain why systemd was seen as necessary and why it provokes such strong reactions. He also discusses why change is scary.\",\"why_hn\":\"The most-recommended balanced take on systemd, from a BSD perspective, handed to anyone caught up in the systemd debate.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"systemd\",\"categories\":[\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\",\"Operating systems & kernels\"],\"tags\":[\"systemd\",\"init systems\",\"freebsd\",\"unix history\",\"linux\"],\"quote\":\"is an interesting talk on this topic from the view of someone (Benno Rice) in BSD land. Highly recommended.\",\"recommenders\":16,\"score\":16.0},{\"vid\":\"AdNJ3fydeao\",\"title\":\"Rich Harris - Rethinking reactivity\",\"channel\":\"You Gotta Love Frontend\",\"summary\":\"Svelte creator Rich Harris explains moving reactivity into the compiler and language rather than tracking state at runtime, with a history of spreadsheets as reactive systems and comparisons to React.\",\"why_hn\":\"The most recommended introduction to Svelte, credited with sparking its adoption.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"frontend reactivity\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\"],\"tags\":[\"svelte\",\"reactivity\",\"compilers\",\"react\",\"rich harris\",\"spreadsheets\"],\"quote\":\"you can (and I heartily recommend doing so) watch the \\\"Rethinking reactivity\\\" video here\",\"recommenders\":15,\"score\":15.5},{\"vid\":\"IcgmSRJHu_8\",\"title\":\"\\\"Making Impossible States Impossible\\\" by Richard Feldman\",\"channel\":\"elm-conf\",\"summary\":\"Richard Feldman's elm-conf talk on designing models so that bugs from impossible application states cannot occur, using Elm's compiler and types. It demonstrates techniques for making invalid states unrepresentable.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as a talk that changed how people model data, with ideas that apply well beyond Elm to any typed language.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"type-driven data modeling\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"elm\",\"type systems\",\"make illegal states unrepresentable\",\"functional programming\",\"data modeling\"],\"quote\":\"the talk is Elm lang focused, but the concepts still apply to other languages.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":15.5},{\"vid\":\"LQRAfJyEsko\",\"title\":\"Dominion (2018) - full documentary [Official]\",\"channel\":\"Farm Transparency Project\",\"summary\":\"Dominion is a two-hour documentary using drones and hidden cameras to expose conditions in animal agriculture, including food, clothing, entertainment and research. It questions humanity's dominion over animals.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters push it, often forcefully, as the video to watch to see what factory farming really involves, and some say it changed their diet.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"animal agriculture cruelty\",\"categories\":[\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\",\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Animals & nature\"],\"tags\":[\"factory farming\",\"animal rights\",\"veganism\",\"slaughterhouse\",\"ethics\"],\"quote\":\"this documentary made me re-evaluate my diet.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":15.5},{\"vid\":\"cIQ9IXSUzuM\",\"title\":\"D/A and A/D | Digital Show and Tell (Monty Montgomery @ xiph.org)\",\"channel\":\"FL Studio\",\"summary\":\"Monty Montgomery of Xiph.org gives real-time demonstrations of sampling, quantization, bit depth and dither using real audio equipment, including both digital analysis tools and vintage analog bench gear. It shows that sampled audio is not stair-stepped and corrects common misconceptions about digital audio.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN calls it the gold-standard debunking of audiophile myths and the stair-step misconception, and recommends it as a primer on digital audio.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"digital audio sampling\",\"categories\":[\"Audio & video engineering\",\"Information theory & signal processing\"],\"tags\":[\"sampling\",\"nyquist\",\"bit depth\",\"dither\",\"audiophile myths\",\"xiph\"],\"quote\":\"might be the best explanatory video I have ever witnessed\",\"recommenders\":15,\"score\":15.5},{\"vid\":\"kpk2tdsPh0A\",\"title\":\"SM64 - Watch for Rolling Rocks - 0.5x A Presses (Commentated) [OUTDATED]\",\"channel\":\"pannenkoek2012\",\"summary\":\"Pannenkoek2012's commentated tool-assisted Super Mario 64 run collecting the Watch for Rolling Rocks star with half an A press, using parallel universes and QPU movement. The video is marked outdated in favor of a newer version.\",\"why_hn\":\"Held up as a legendary deep dive into game internals, explained lucidly and with humor, and a gateway to the channel.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"super mario 64 internals\",\"categories\":[\"Speedrunning, glitches & game hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"super mario 64\",\"speedrunning\",\"tas\",\"parallel universes\",\"game internals\",\"pannenkoek2012\"],\"quote\":\"This guy literally has a PhD in SM64. It's insanely complicated, and surprisingly lucid.\",\"recommenders\":13,\"score\":15.5},{\"vid\":\"HyznrdDSSGM\",\"title\":\"8-bit computer update\",\"channel\":\"Ben Eater\",\"summary\":\"Ben Eater's short update announcing his plan to build another 8-bit computer from scratch on camera. It kicks off the breadboard computer series that became widely known.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends the series it launched as the best way to understand how a CPU works, praised for Eater's clear teaching.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"building an 8-bit computer\",\"categories\":[\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\"],\"tags\":[\"ben eater\",\"breadboard computer\",\"cpu design\",\"digital logic\",\"electronics\"],\"quote\":\"I think I learned more from Ben Eater’s “Building an 8-bit breadboard computer” series [0] than my intro to computer architecture course at university.\",\"recommenders\":14,\"score\":15.0},{\"vid\":\"L0KuAx1COEk\",\"title\":\"MIT's Pathway to Fusion Energy (IAP 2017) - Zach Hartwig\",\"channel\":\"MIT Plasma Science and Fusion Center\",\"summary\":\"Zach Hartwig of MIT introduces the key concepts of magnetic fusion energy and the state of fusion research. He then presents MIT's proposed pathway of using high-field superconducting magnets to build smaller, cheaper tokamaks on a climate-relevant timescale.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended again and again as the best accessible overview of fusion physics, useful for judging fusion announcements and understanding why high-temperature superconducting magnets matter.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"fusion energy\",\"categories\":[\"Nuclear power & fusion\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"tokamak\",\"high-temperature superconductors\",\"sparc\",\"magnetic confinement\",\"iter\"],\"quote\":\"This has been by far the most informative source on Fusion I've seen and gives a clear picture on how to evaluate claims like this.\",\"recommenders\":16,\"score\":15.0},{\"vid\":\"NOAgplgTxfc\",\"title\":\"Stanford's Sapolsky On Depression in U.S. (Full Lecture)\",\"channel\":\"Stanford\",\"summary\":\"Stanford professor Robert Sapolsky lectures on depression as a real biological disease. He covers its biological and psychological causes, how it differs from ordinary sadness, and ideas such as learned helplessness. He ties these together into one account.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it repeatedly as the best single introduction to understanding clinical depression.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"clinical depression\",\"categories\":[\"Mental health & neurodiversity\",\"Neuroscience & the brain\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"sapolsky\",\"depression\",\"neuroscience\",\"learned helplessness\",\"mental health\"],\"quote\":\"This is the quintessential video for understanding clinical depression.\",\"recommenders\":14,\"score\":15.0},{\"vid\":\"jvPPXbo87ds\",\"title\":\"The Continuity of Splines\",\"channel\":\"Freya Holmér\",\"summary\":\"Freya Holmer's 74-minute video essay on splines, covering Bezier curves, B-splines, derivatives and the different levels of continuity (C0, C1, C2), built with custom animation tools. She notes she did not cover non-uniform splines.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the go-to recommendation for anyone who wants to genuinely understand Bezier curves and splines, and commenters repeatedly call it one of the best programming-related educational videos.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"splines and bezier curves\",\"categories\":[\"Computer graphics & rendering\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"bezier\",\"b-splines\",\"continuity\",\"interpolation\",\"computer graphics\",\"math visualization\"],\"quote\":\"is (IMHO) one of the best educational videos on a programming topic, full stop.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":15.0},{\"vid\":\"kBdfcR-8hEY\",\"title\":\"Justice: What's The Right Thing To Do? Episode 01 \\\"THE MORAL SIDE OF MURDER\\\"\",\"channel\":\"Harvard University\",\"summary\":\"The first episode of Michael Sandel's Harvard course on moral reasoning, opening with the trolley problem and the choice between killing one to save five. Sandel draws out student arguments and introduces utilitarian and other approaches to justice.\",\"why_hn\":\"One of the most frequently recommended online lecture series on HN, praised for giving people vocabulary to examine ethical positions and for Sandel's skilled teaching.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"moral philosophy\",\"categories\":[\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"trolley problem\",\"ethics\",\"michael sandel\",\"justice\",\"utilitarianism\",\"harvard\"],\"quote\":\"Two lecture series I always recommend, and feel like everyone should watch\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":15.0},{\"vid\":\"5SaFTm2bcac\",\"title\":\"Video explains the world's most important 6-sec drum loop\",\"channel\":\"Landon Proctor\",\"summary\":\"Nate Harrison's 2004 video history of the Amen Break, a six-second drum sample from a 1969 b-side that became the basis of hip-hop sampling, jungle and drum and bass. It is also a meditation on cultural ownership and creativity.\",\"why_hn\":\"A perennial HN recommendation, brought up whenever sampling, copyright or music history comes up, often described as a must-watch.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"amen break and sampling\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"amen break\",\"sampling\",\"copyright\",\"jungle\",\"hip hop\",\"music history\"],\"quote\":\"this is an excellent 20-minute video about the history of the Amen Break\",\"recommenders\":15,\"score\":14.5},{\"vid\":\"LIb3L4vKZ7U\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2015: Andrei Alexandrescu “std::allocator...”\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Andrei Alexandrescu's CppCon 2015 talk argues that std::allocator is badly designed. He proposes building high-performance allocators from small composable, templated pieces.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as an entertaining, mind-opening talk on allocator design and composable generic programming.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"memory allocators\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"c++\",\"std::allocator\",\"composable allocators\",\"andrei alexandrescu\",\"templates\",\"cppcon\"],\"quote\":\"It really opened my eyes to how one could very elegantly make a very complex and high-performance heap allocator\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":14.5},{\"vid\":\"QNznD9hMEh0\",\"title\":\"Jim Simons (full length interview) - Numberphile\",\"channel\":\"Numberphile2\",\"summary\":\"The full-length Numberphile interview with mathematician and hedge fund founder Jim Simons. He discusses his path from mathematics and codebreaking to founding Renaissance Technologies, his use of algorithms in markets, and his philanthropy.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the best readily available interview with Simons, notable for his emphasis on luck and a humble approach to success.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"quantitative finance\",\"categories\":[\"Investing & personal finance\",\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"jim simons\",\"renaissance technologies\",\"numberphile\",\"hedge funds\",\"mathematics\"],\"quote\":\"the full length one is very good. it gives an understanding as to why simons has been so successful.\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":14.5},{\"vid\":\"ar9WRwCiSr0\",\"title\":\"Reverse emulating the NES to give it SUPER POWERS!\",\"channel\":\"suckerpinch\",\"summary\":\"Tom Murphy (suckerpinch) shows 'reverse emulation', using a Raspberry Pi in an NES cartridge to give an unmodified NES new abilities such as playing SNES games. The video starts in a strange way and gets stranger.\",\"why_hn\":\"Praised as a brilliant, deadpan and inventive hardware hack, and a good starting point for Tom7's videos.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nes hardware hacking\",\"categories\":[\"Speedrunning, glitches & game hacking\",\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\"],\"tags\":[\"reverse emulation\",\"nes\",\"raspberry pi\",\"retro gaming\",\"tom7\"],\"quote\":\"are a must watch.\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":14.5},{\"vid\":\"dBnniua6-oM\",\"title\":\"Sugar: THE BITTER TRUTH\",\"channel\":\"University of California Television (UCTV)\",\"summary\":\"Robert Lustig of UCSF lectures on the biochemistry of sugar, arguing that fructose and lack of fiber drive the obesity epidemic via insulin. It is the widely viewed 90-minute talk that popularized the anti-sugar case.\",\"why_hn\":\"The most-linked YouTube video on HN, recommended as an in-depth explanation of fructose metabolism.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"sugar and metabolism\",\"categories\":[\"Nutrition & diet science\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"robert lustig\",\"fructose\",\"obesity\",\"insulin\",\"nutrition\",\"liver\"],\"quote\":\"This is one of the best nutritional videos I've ever seen.\",\"recommenders\":13,\"score\":14.5},{\"vid\":\"otbnC2zE2rw\",\"title\":\"Designing the Ideal Bootstrapped Business with Jason Cohen\",\"channel\":\"Rob Walling\",\"summary\":\"Jason Cohen's MicroConf talk on designing a bootstrapped business by starting from your own strengths, goals and constraints. He works backward to the business model, pricing, market and product that fit, with practical points like annual prepay and pricing tiers.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the go-to talk HN commenters recommend to anyone asking about bootstrapping, SaaS pricing, or choosing bootstrapping over VC.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"bootstrapped business design\",\"categories\":[\"Startups & entrepreneurship\",\"Product, marketing & sales\"],\"tags\":[\"bootstrapping\",\"saas pricing\",\"annual prepay\",\"microconf\",\"startups\"],\"quote\":\"This talk is the holy grail on this topic\",\"recommenders\":13,\"score\":14.5},{\"vid\":\"040ejWnFkj0\",\"title\":\"How Self-Driving Cars will Destroy Cities (and what to do about it)\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes argues that self-driving cars will worsen sprawl and car dependence and harm cities. The video compares car-centered and people-centered urban design and weighs what cities should do instead.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently recommended in autonomous vehicle and Waymo threads as the main counterargument, a long but worthwhile video essay on how self-driving cars could affect cities.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"self-driving cars and cities\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Self-driving & autonomous vehicles\"],\"tags\":[\"urban planning\",\"autonomous vehicles\",\"car dependence\",\"public transport\",\"sprawl\"],\"quote\":\"It's long, but well worth an hour of your time.\",\"recommenders\":17,\"score\":14.0},{\"vid\":\"B1J2RMorJXM\",\"title\":\"Light Years Ahead | The 1969 Apollo Guidance Computer\",\"channel\":\"TNMoC\",\"summary\":\"Robert Wills of TNMoC explains the Apollo Guidance Computer hardware and software, then walks through the Apollo 11 landing procedure step by step, including the 1202 alarms.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN calls it mandatory viewing on the AGC, praising the speaker's enthusiasm and the clear account of restartable, robust software design.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"apollo guidance computer\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Space exploration & spaceflight\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\"],\"tags\":[\"apollo 11\",\"agc\",\"1202 alarm\",\"spaceflight software\",\"computer history\"],\"quote\":\"The guy is really, really into it and it just makes the presentation amazing.\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":14.0},{\"vid\":\"D1R-jKKp3NA\",\"title\":\"Steve Jobs Stanford Commencement Speech 2005\",\"channel\":\"JoshuaG\",\"summary\":\"Steve Jobs's 2005 Stanford commencement address, built around three stories: connecting the dots, love and loss including being fired from Apple, and death. It is a 15-minute talk on finding work you love.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it for years as an inspirational talk to send to graduates, founders and anyone at a career crossroads.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"career and life advice\",\"categories\":[\"Productivity & self-improvement\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"steve jobs\",\"commencement speech\",\"motivation\",\"stanford\",\"follow your passion\"],\"quote\":\"This is no doubt one of the best speech i ever heard.\",\"recommenders\":13,\"score\":14.0},{\"vid\":\"F_Riqjdh2oM\",\"title\":\"Quantum Computing for Computer Scientists\",\"channel\":\"Microsoft Research\",\"summary\":\"An 88-minute Microsoft Research talk that explains how quantum computers beat classical ones using basic linear algebra, qubits, superposition and gates. It works through the Deutsch oracle problem and ends with entanglement, teleportation and a live demo.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is HN's most frequently recommended starting point for quantum computing because it avoids pop-science hand-waving and needs only basic linear algebra.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"quantum computing\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum computing\"],\"tags\":[\"qubits\",\"linear algebra\",\"deutsch oracle\",\"quantum gates\",\"entanglement\",\"q#\"],\"quote\":\"This video is the simplest explanation that I have found for Quantum Computing\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":14.0},{\"vid\":\"Fy0aCDmgnxg\",\"title\":\"Juice it or lose it - a talk by Martin Jonasson & Petri Purho\",\"channel\":\"grapefrukt\",\"summary\":\"Martin Jonasson and Petri Purho take a bland Breakout clone and add layer after layer of 'juice' such as easing, particles, screen shake and sound until it feels alive. A live demo makes the effect of small feedback details obvious.\",\"why_hn\":\"The go-to recommendation on game feel and polish, cited by HN as one of the best talks for anyone making interactive things feel good.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"game feel\",\"categories\":[\"Game design\",\"Indie game development\"],\"tags\":[\"juice\",\"game feel\",\"polish\",\"game design\",\"breakout\",\"ux\"],\"quote\":\"Gotta be one of the best talks for anyone trying to make things interactive that also feel good.\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":14.0},{\"vid\":\"HxaD_trXwRE\",\"title\":\"Lexical Scanning in Go - Rob Pike\",\"channel\":\"GoogleFOSSSydney\",\"summary\":\"Rob Pike shows how the Go text/template lexer is built, using state functions, goroutines and channels. The talk was given at a Google Technology User Group in Sydney in 2011.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it for years as an elegant introduction to writing a lexer that is easy to follow even without a parsing background.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"lexical scanning\",\"categories\":[\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"go\",\"lexer\",\"rob pike\",\"state machines\",\"concurrency\",\"parsing\"],\"quote\":\"It's far more interesting than it sounds, and even if you don't know much about lexing/parsing it's easy to follow.\",\"recommenders\":13,\"score\":14.0},{\"vid\":\"LnzuMJLZRdU\",\"title\":\"“Hello, world” from scratch on a 6502 — Part 1\",\"channel\":\"Ben Eater\",\"summary\":\"Ben Eater starts a series building a working computer on breadboards around the 6502 microprocessor. In this first part he gets a 'hello world' running from scratch.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as the best hands-on way to understand how a CPU and a computer work from first principles.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"6502 computer hardware\",\"categories\":[\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\"],\"tags\":[\"6502\",\"breadboard computer\",\"digital electronics\",\"assembly\",\"ben eater\"],\"quote\":\"It is highly recommended if you want to dive down into computers and digital logic on first principles.\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":14.0}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"OyfBQmvr2Hc\",\"title\":\"William Byrd on \\\"The Most Beautiful Program Ever Written\\\" [PWL NYC]\",\"channel\":\"PapersWeLove\",\"summary\":\"William Byrd presents at Papers We Love NYC on what he calls the most beautiful program ever written, a Lisp interpreter written in Lisp. He walks through the metacircular interpreter and related ideas, and demos relational programming and program synthesis near the end.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as an amazing talk that derives the essence of Lisp and shows miniKanren-style program synthesis.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"lisp metacircular interpreters\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\"],\"tags\":[\"lisp\",\"scheme\",\"minikanren\",\"program synthesis\",\"relational programming\",\"interpreters\"],\"quote\":\"I can't recommend the talk Dr Byrd did on this enough\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":14.0},{\"vid\":\"RjD1aLm4Thg\",\"title\":\"What Bodies Think About: Bioelectric Computation Outside the Nervous System - NeurIPS 2018\",\"channel\":\"The Artificial Intelligence Channel\",\"summary\":\"Michael Levin's NeurIPS 2018 talk on bioelectric computation outside the nervous system, showing how cell electrical networks shape development and regeneration. It covers morphological information processing in living tissue.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN regularly calls it mind-blowing and recommends it as an introduction to Levin's work on bioelectricity and regeneration.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"bioelectricity and regeneration\",\"categories\":[\"Biology & biotechnology\",\"Neuroscience & the brain\",\"Consciousness & cognition\"],\"tags\":[\"michael levin\",\"bioelectricity\",\"morphogenesis\",\"regeneration\",\"cognition\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend this video from Levin. It was my introduction to his work.\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":14.0},{\"vid\":\"ZK3O402wf1c\",\"title\":\"Lec 1 | MIT 18.06 Linear Algebra, Spring 2005\",\"channel\":\"MIT OpenCourseWare\",\"summary\":\"The first lecture of Gilbert Strang's MIT 18.06 Linear Algebra course, covering the geometry of linear equations. It opens a full 35-lecture university course.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is HN's default recommendation for learning linear algebra, praised for Strang's teaching and for building real understanding useful in machine learning.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"linear algebra\",\"categories\":[\"University lectures & courses\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"gilbert strang\",\"mit ocw\",\"matrices\",\"machine learning math\",\"systems of equations\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend the MIT 18.06 open course on linear algebra - the lectures are first rate.\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":14.0},{\"vid\":\"_rBO8neWw04\",\"title\":\"Detergent packs are kinda wishy-washy (Dishwashers Explained)\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"Technology Connections explains how dishwashers work, why detergent pods underperform powder, and how to use the pre-wash and main-wash compartments. It argues you don't need to pre-rinse dishes.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as the definitive, mind-changing explanation of dishwasher use, even though it runs over 30 minutes.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"dishwashers\",\"categories\":[\"Home, DIY & practical skills\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"Consumer devices & gadgets\"],\"tags\":[\"dishwasher\",\"detergent\",\"appliances\",\"pre-rinse\",\"household tech\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend you watch this video by Technology connections[1], I too used to think I had to rinse my dishes before putting them in the machine.\",\"recommenders\":15,\"score\":14.0},{\"vid\":\"4fFDFbi3toc\",\"title\":\"\\\"Testing Distributed Systems w/ Deterministic Simulation\\\" by Will Wilson\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Will Wilson of FoundationDB explains how the team tested their distributed database using deterministic simulation. By running the whole system in a simulator with injected faults and reproducible seeds, they could find rare concurrency and network bugs.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the canonical talk on simulation testing and a mind-blowing example of how far rigorous testing can go.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"distributed systems testing\",\"categories\":[\"Distributed systems\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Databases & storage\"],\"tags\":[\"deterministic simulation\",\"foundationdb\",\"fault injection\",\"concurrency bugs\",\"strange loop\"],\"quote\":\"this is one of the most amazing talks on distributed systems\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":13.5},{\"vid\":\"7Pq-S557XQU\",\"title\":\"What Happened to Horses Is Happening to Us\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey's video \\\"Humans Need Not Apply\\\" argues that automation will make a large part of the workforce economically obsolete, much as engines did for horses. It surveys robots and software replacing both physical and mental labor.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN has recommended it for a decade as the standard explainer on automation and unemployment, though skeptics later cite it as an example of overconfident predictions.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"automation and unemployment\",\"categories\":[\"Labor, work & inequality\",\"AI safety, AGI & impact\",\"Robotics\"],\"tags\":[\"automation\",\"jobs\",\"ai\",\"economics\",\"robotics\"],\"quote\":\"Excellent video on the subject: CGPGrey - Humans Need Not Apply\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":13.5},{\"vid\":\"BLGFriOKz6U\",\"title\":\"Behind the Scenes of iOS Security\",\"channel\":\"Black Hat\",\"summary\":\"Apple security head Ivan Krstic gives a Black Hat 2016 talk on iOS security, covering HomeKit, Auto Unlock and iCloud Keychain. He explains key hierarchies and how data can stay safe even in adversarial clouds.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as the deepest public look at how iCloud Keychain and Apple's device and cloud security are designed.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"ios and icloud security\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Cryptography\",\"Consumer devices & gadgets\"],\"tags\":[\"ios security\",\"icloud keychain\",\"secure enclave\",\"cloud key vault\",\"black hat\"],\"quote\":\"if you haven't watched the talk at Black Hat I'd highly recommend it\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":13.5},{\"vid\":\"EZ05e7EMOLM\",\"title\":\"🚀  TDD, Where Did It All Go Wrong (Ian Cooper)\",\"channel\":\"DevTernity Conference\",\"summary\":\"Ian Cooper's DevTernity talk revisits Kent Beck's original TDD ideas and shows where misunderstandings crept in. It argues tests should target behaviors through public interfaces rather than implementation details, so suites support refactoring instead of resisting it.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly calls it the best talk on testing, and many say it changed how they approach tests, mocks and the meaning of a unit.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"test-driven development\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"tdd\",\"unit testing\",\"mocking\",\"refactoring\",\"kent beck\",\"bdd\"],\"quote\":\"This excellent talk completely changed the way I approached testing. Every developer who writes tests needs to watch this now!\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":13.5},{\"vid\":\"PUv66718DII\",\"title\":\"Bret Victor - Inventing on Principle\",\"channel\":\"Rui Oliveira\",\"summary\":\"Bret Victor's talk at CUSEC 2012 in which he argues that creators need immediate feedback and should organize their work around a guiding principle. He demonstrates live-coding and animation tools that show results