"mechanical analog computers\",\"categories\":[\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"History of computing\",\"Military history & warfare\"],\"tags\":[\"fire control computers\",\"analog computing\",\"us navy\",\"mechanisms\",\"gears\"],\"quote\":\"It illustrates the problems and solutions near perfectly, and I've watched it way too many times.\",\"recommenders\":13,\"score\":13.5},{\"vid\":\"-5wpm-gesOY\",\"title\":\"The Problem with Time & Timezones - Computerphile\",\"channel\":\"Computerphile\",\"summary\":\"Tom Scott explains for Computerphile why handling time and timezones in code is so hard, walking through a simple web app that computes how long ago something happened. It covers the quirks of timezones and calendars and ends in an exasperated rant.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the go-to funny, accessible explanation of why you should never implement datetime handling yourself, linked in nearly every timezone thread.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"time and timezones in software\",\"categories\":[\"Software (other)\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"timezones\",\"datetime\",\"tom scott\",\"computerphile\",\"programming pitfalls\"],\"quote\":\"a must watch for developers just starting out with Timezones\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":13.0},{\"vid\":\"-6BsiVyC1kM\",\"title\":\"The Value of Values with Rich Hickey\",\"channel\":\"InfoQ\",\"summary\":\"Rich Hickey's JaxConf 2012 keynote examines what values are and why immutable values matter as information systems grow more complex and data gets bigger. It contrasts places with values and builds the case for immutability.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it as the talk that convinced them of immutability and functional programming.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"immutability and values\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Databases & storage\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"rich hickey\",\"clojure\",\"immutable data\",\"functional programming\",\"datomic\"],\"quote\":\"is what finally sold me on the benefits of pure functional programming and immutable data structures.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":13.0},{\"vid\":\"4Q7FTjhvZ7Y\",\"title\":\"Project Code Rush - The Beginnings of Netscape / Mozilla Documentary\",\"channel\":\"The Documentary Network\",\"summary\":\"Code Rush is a one-hour documentary following Netscape engineers in Silicon Valley through the company's last year as an independent firm. It covers the Mozilla open-source announcement, the frantic push to ready the source for release by March 31, 1998, and the toll on engineers' lives before the AOL acquisition.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as an intimate, embedded look at late-90s web history and the spirit of building something important under pressure.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"netscape and mozilla history\",\"categories\":[\"Tech company stories & documentaries\",\"Hacker culture & free software\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"netscape\",\"mozilla\",\"dot-com era\",\"open source\",\"jwz\",\"web history\"],\"quote\":\"Code Rush, which is an awesome documentary for anyone interested in the history of the Web\",\"recommenders\":13,\"score\":13.0},{\"vid\":\"8pTEmbeENF4\",\"title\":\"Bret Victor   The Future of Programming\",\"channel\":\"Joey Reid\",\"summary\":\"Bret Victor's DBX 2013 talk, delivered in the guise of a 1973 IBM engineer with an overhead projector. It surveys forgotten ideas from 1960s and early 70s computing and warns against assuming you know what you're doing.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as a classic, near-mandatory talk on how programming tools and paradigms stagnated and which older ideas deserve revival.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"history and future of programming\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"bret victor\",\"programming paradigms\",\"computing history\",\"tooling\",\"software stagnation\"],\"quote\":\"Take a look and you will be amazed of how good (or revolutionary by our standards) some old technologies were.\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":13.0},{\"vid\":\"AJdEqssNZ-U\",\"title\":\"Jan Willem Nijman - Vlambeer - \\\"The art of screenshake\\\" at INDIGO Classes 2013\",\"channel\":\"Dutch Game Garden\",\"summary\":\"Vlambeer's Jan Willem Nijman shows how to make an action game feel great by stacking small effects like screenshake, recoil and particles onto a basic shooter. It was presented at INDIGO Classes 2013.\",\"why_hn\":\"A perennial recommendation for anyone learning game feel and juice, praised for showing concrete steps that turn a bland prototype into a satisfying game.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"game feel\",\"categories\":[\"Game design\",\"Indie game development\"],\"tags\":[\"screenshake\",\"juice\",\"vlambeer\",\"game polish\",\"indie games\"],\"quote\":\"\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":13.0},{\"vid\":\"FACK2knC08E\",\"title\":\"DON'T PANIC — Hans Rosling showing the facts about population\",\"channel\":\"Gapminder Foundation\",\"summary\":\"Hans Rosling uses data and animated statistics to show how world population is changing, why birth rates fall as child survival and wealth rise, and why population is expected to peak around 11 billion. It also tackles common misconceptions about Africa and family planning.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it to counter overpopulation alarm with data on falling birth rates.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"world population\",\"categories\":[\"Data visualization & information design\",\"Social issues & culture\",\"Data science & statistics\"],\"tags\":[\"hans rosling\",\"birth rates\",\"demography\",\"gapminder\",\"statistics\"],\"quote\":\"I can highly recomme