ce in complex adaptive systems, arguing systems operate near the edge of failure and that the surprise is how rarely they fail. He presents a model of how pressures push systems toward failure boundaries.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people cite it constantly as the clearest explanation of normalization of deviance and how complex systems drift toward failure.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"complex systems safety\",\"categories\":[\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\",\"Management, leadership & organizations\"],\"tags\":[\"richard cook\",\"resilience\",\"normalization of deviance\",\"operations\",\"failure modes\"],\"quote\":\"One of the most important talks I've ever seen is Richard Cook's\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":13.0},{\"vid\":\"QyJZzq0v7Z4\",\"title\":\"Why Isn't Functional Programming the Norm? – Richard Feldman\",\"channel\":\"Metosin\",\"summary\":\"Richard Feldman argues that functional programming is not the norm largely because of historical accidents, such as C++ popularity, platform exclusivity, marketing spend and network effects, rather than merit. He traces the history of how languages become mainstream.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the standard answer to why FP isn't mainstream and why languages succeed.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming language adoption\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"functional programming\",\"oop\",\"language adoption\",\"network effects\",\"elm\"],\"quote\":\"This talk by Richard Feldman is an excellent discussion about why Functional Programming is not the norm\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":13.0},{\"vid\":\"W3aieHjyNvw\",\"title\":\"Overwatch Gameplay Architecture and Netcode\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"Blizzard's Timothy Ford explains in this 2017 GDC session how Overwatch uses an Entity Component System architecture for layered gameplay. He also covers its server-authoritative netcode with client-side prediction and how they keep the game responsive and fair.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the best real-world AAA example of ECS and game netcode, and cites it constantly in discussions of data-oriented design.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"game architecture and netcode\",\"categories\":[\"Game programming & engines\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"entity component system\",\"netcode\",\"overwatch\",\"client prediction\",\"gdc\",\"data-oriented design\"],\"quote\":\"This Overwatch GDC talk is the best breakdown/example of data-oriented ECS in a AAA game that I know of\",\"recommenders\":16,\"score\":13.0},{\"vid\":\"WXuK6gekU1Y\",\"title\":\"AlphaGo - The Movie | Full award-winning documentary\",\"channel\":\"Google DeepMind\",\"summary\":\"Feature-length documentary following DeepMind's AlphaGo program as it prepares for and plays the 2016 match against Go champion Lee Sedol. It covers the team, the games, and the emotional reaction of the Go world to an AI beating a top human.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it a moving, well-made human story about AI and Go that works for non-technical viewers and does not date.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"alphago and go\",\"categories\":[\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"Chess, board games & puzzles\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"alphago\",\"lee sedol\",\"deepmind\",\"go\",\"reinforcement learning\"],\"quote\":\"I consider AlphaGo - The Movie [1] to be a timeless classic that will never feel outdated.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":13.0},{\"vid\":\"YR5WdGrpoug\",\"title\":\"Maybe Not - Rich Hickey\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"Rich Hickey's Clojure/Conj 2018 keynote argues that Maybe/Option types and rigid type signatures are a poor way to model data that changes over time. He builds the case around optionality, schema growth and compatibility, 