001 (SICP) with Abelson and Sussman, giving an overview of the course and an introduction to Lisp. It argues that computer science is about managing complexity and abstraction, not computers.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend the SICP lectures as a deeper, livelier route into the book and a formative course even for seasoned programmers.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming fundamentals with Lisp\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"University lectures & courses\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"sicp\",\"scheme\",\"lisp\",\"abstraction\",\"mit\",\"recursion\"],\"quote\":\"I watched and cannot recommend it enough, even for seasoned programmers\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":12.5},{\"vid\":\"1Jwo5qc78QU\",\"title\":\"YouTube's copyright system isn't broken. The world's is.\",\"channel\":\"Tom Scott\",\"summary\":\"Tom Scott's long explainer on YouTube's copyright system, Content ID and the DMCA. He argues the platform's system is a workaround for a broader copyright system that does not fit the modern world.\",\"why_hn\":\"The default link in copyright and takedown threads, recommended as an excellent, comprehensive explanation of why automated enforcement and the law work as they do.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"youtube copyright and content id\",\"categories\":[\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Internet culture & memes\",\"Tech industry & business models\"],\"tags\":[\"copyright\",\"dmca\",\"content id\",\"fair use\",\"youtube\"],\"quote\":\"Tom Scott explains the situation and the solution best.\",\"recommenders\":13,\"score\":12.5},{\"vid\":\"30jNsCVLpAE\",\"title\":\"Debugging Under Fire: Keep your Head when Systems have Lost their Mind • Bryan Cantrill • GOTO 2017\",\"channel\":\"GOTO Conferences\",\"summary\":\"Bryan Cantrill's GOTO Chicago 2017 talk on debugging production systems under pressure, centered on the incident where an operator's mistyped command rebooted an entire Joyent datacenter. It covers human factors, automation and architecture.\",\"why_hn\":\"A perennial HN recommendation for outage and postmortem threads, praised as funny, educational and one of the best postmortems anyone has seen.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"incident response and postmortems\",\"categories\":[\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"bryan cantrill\",\"outages\",\"postmortem\",\"debugging\",\"joyent\",\"devops\"],\"quote\":\"The best postmortem I've seen was actually a conference talk\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":12.5},{\"vid\":\"5ESJH1NLMLs\",\"title\":\"Children Of The Magenta Line\",\"channel\":\"Mossie Fly\",\"summary\":\"A re-upload of American Airlines captain Warren Vanderburgh's 1997 presentation warning that pilots who over-rely on cockpit automation lose manual flying skills and awareness. The uploader adds context from his own flying.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN brings it up in any thread about autopilot, Tesla FSD, self-driving cars and crashes as the classic lecture on automation dependency.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"automation dependency in aviation\",\"categories\":[\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"autopilot\",\"pilot training\",\"human factors\",\"automation\",\"flight safety\"],\"quote\":\"if you haven't seen this amazing talk, you should definitely give it a watch\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":12.5},{\"vid\":\"Ik1AK56FtVc\",\"title\":\"Republic, Lost | Lawrence Lessig | Talks at Google\",\"channel\":\"Talks at Google\",\"summary\":\"Lawrence Lessig's Talks at Google presentation based on his book Republic, Lost, arguing that money in politics corrupts Congress and eroded public trust, and outlining what could be done about it. It supports the argument with data on political donations.\",\"why_hn\":\"A perennial HN recommendation whenever lobbying, SOPA, taxes or political corruption come up, with several commenters saying it changed their views.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"money in politics\",\"categories\":[\"US politics & elections\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\"],\"tags\":[\"lawrence lessig\",\"campaign finance\",\"corruption\",\"citizens united\",\"democracy\"],\"quote\":\"That video has changed my point of view on the entire subject and has allowed me to start striking at the root.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":12.5},{\"vid\":\"LrObZ_HZZUc\",\"title\":\"London Swallowed a City but Couldn't Digest It.\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey explains that the City of London is a tiny, separate entity with its own governance, history and privileges, distinct from the city called London. He covers its financial role and how it is run.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN's go-to short explainer for the City of London versus London, cited in many finance and UK threads.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"city of london\",\"categories\":[\"World history\",\"Politics & history (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"city of london\",\"uk governance\",\"finance\",\"history\",\"cgp grey\"],\"quote\":\"CGP Grey has a delightful video explaining this\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":12.5},{\"vid\":\"ZSRHeXYDLko\",\"title\":\"Preventing the Collapse of Civilization / Jonathan Blow (Thekla, Inc)\",\"channel\":\"DevGAMM\",\"summary\":\"A DevGAMM talk by Jonathan Blow arguing that civilizations can lose technological capability and that software quality is already deteriorating. He discusses how knowledge fails to transfer between generations.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the standard link in HN threads about software decay, lost knowledge and fragile technological civilization.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"software quality collapse\",\"categories\":[\"Software performance & optimization\",\"Futurism & technology and society\",\"Game design\"],\"tags\":[\"jonathan blow\",\"software decay\",\"lost knowledge\",\"technology regression\",\"complexity\"],\"quote\":\"Anyone who finds this concept interesting to think about and hasn't seen this video may enjoy it\",\"recommenders\":14,\"score\":12.5},{\"vid\":\"a9xAKttWgP4\",\"title\":\"Conway's Game Of Life in APL\",\"channel\":\"Dyalog\",\"summary\":\"A short Dyalog demonstration building Conway's Game of Life in a single line of APL, step by step. It shows how array operations replace loops.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended whenever APL or array languages come up as the quickest way to see why the paradigm is so striking.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"apl array programming\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Functional programming\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"apl\",\"game of life\",\"array languages\",\"dyalog\",\"code golf\"],\"quote\":\"Try watching this video; it will only take you 8 minutes\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":12.5},{\"vid\":\"bKGhvKyjgLY\",\"title\":\"Finding the Missing Memristor - R. Stanley Williams\",\"channel\":\"The Qualcomm Institute\",\"summary\":\"R. Stanley Williams of HP Labs gives a keynote on the memristor, covering the physics, crossbar computation, and potential for memory and new computer architectures.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended for years as the best technical introduction to memristors from the person who found the physical device.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"memristors\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Materials science\"],\"tags\":[\"memristor\",\"hp labs\",\"nonvolatile memory\",\"crossbar\",\"computer architecture\"],\"quote\":\"I encourage folks to watch this tech talk by HP Research Labs on the discovery of the memristor and implementation details.\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":12.5},{\"vid\":\"d_zt3kGW1NM\",\"title\":\"Winners Take All | Anand Giridharadas | Talks at Google\",\"channel\":\"Talks at Google\",\"summary\":\"Anand Giridharadas presents his book Winners Take All at Google, arguing that the global elite's philanthropy and 'win-win' framing preserve the systems that create inequity. He challenges the audience, including Google employees, to examine their own role in that system.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a pointed critique of philanthropy and win-win thinking, delivered inside Google itself, and cite it across threads on wealth, tax and tech power.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"elite philanthropy\",\"categories\":[\"Labor, work & inequality\",\"Tech industry & business models\"],\"tags\":[\"philanthropy\",\"inequality\",\"win-win\",\"tech elite\",\"political economy\"],\"quote\":\"I strongly encourage everyone - especialy anyone employed at Google - to watch it.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":12.5},{\"vid\":\"k2h2lvhzMDc\",\"title\":\"Ed Catmull, Pixar: Keep Your Crises Small\",\"channel\":\"Stanford Graduate School of Business\",\"summary\":\"Ed Catmull of Pixar speaks at Stanford's 2007 Entrepreneurship Conference on running a creative company. He argues for surfacing small problems early and building good teams, with stories like the Toy Story 2 crunch.\",\"why_hn\":\"Repeatedly called one of the most useful talks on building organizations and managing creative teams.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"creative leadership and organizations\",\"categories\":[\"Management, leadership & organizations\",\"Animation & visual effects\",\"Tech company stories & documentaries\"],\"tags\":[\"pixar\",\"ed catmull\",\"management\",\"team building\",\"creativity\"],\"quote\":\"one of the most useful talks of all time for building organizations is by Ed Catmull (of Pixar)\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":12.5},{\"vid\":\"r-TLSBdHe1A\",\"title\":\"\\\"Performance Matters\\\" by Emery Berger\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Emery Berger explains why performance is hard to measure and deliver on modern hardware, showing how code layout and alignment can swamp optimization effects. He presents Stabilizer for statistically sound evaluation and Coz, a causal profiler.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the standard recommendation for anyone puzzled by unexplained benchmark changes from tiny code edits, and for learning about causal profiling.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"performance measurement\",\"categories\":[\"Software performance & optimization\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"causal profiling\",\"coz\",\"benchmarking\",\"memory layout\",\"emery berger\",\"optimization\"],\"quote\":\"this is the most amazing optimisation information I have seen\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":12.5},{\"vid\":\"t1MqJPHxy6g\",\"title\":\"Alain de Botton: Status Anxiety\",\"channel\":\"Alain de Botton\",\"summary\":\"Alain de Botton's 2004 documentary based on his book Status Anxiety, running over two hours. It asks why people in rich societies are still unhappy, and examines how worries about success, snobbery and meritocracy drive that anxiety.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an illuminating, eye-opening long-form take on status, luck and how we judge the successful and unsuccessful.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"status anxiety\",\"categories\":[\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Social issues & culture\"],\"tags\":[\"status\",\"philosophy\",\"meritocracy\",\"happiness\",\"alain de botton\"],\"quote\":\"This doc is eye-opening\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":12.5}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"ySuUZEjARPY\",\"title\":\"[LIVE] How to Achieve Proper Grounding - Rick Hartley - Expert Live Training (US)\",\"channel\":\"Altium\",\"summary\":\"Rick Hartley gives a roughly two-hour live training on proper grounding in PCB design. He explains how energy actually flows in the space between conductors, and how that governs noise, EMI, return paths and signal reflections.\",\"why_hn\":\"Hailed as the best video on electrical engineering and an essential, eye-opening watch for anyone designing high-speed boards.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"pcb grounding and emi\",\"categories\":[\"Electronics & circuit design\"],\"tags\":[\"grounding\",\"pcb design\",\"emi\",\"signal integrity\",\"return paths\",\"high-speed design\"],\"quote\":\"the best video I've ever seen on electrical engineering\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":12.5},{\"vid\":\"1OfxlSG6q5Y\",\"title\":\"The Antique Toaster that's Better than Yours\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"Technology Connections takes apart a 1940s Sunbeam Radiant Control toaster. It senses the toast's own browning and lowers and raises it automatically using purely mechanical parts and no electronics.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it again and again as a favorite example of elegant, chip-free mechanical engineering that beats modern toasters.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"mechanical engineering of appliances\",\"categories\":[\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"Consumer devices & gadgets\"],\"tags\":[\"sunbeam toaster\",\"vintage appliances\",\"bimetallic\",\"mechanical design\",\"technology connections\"],\"quote\":\"In case you haven't seen this video of a toaster from the 60s before, you're in for a treat\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"2Op3QLzMgSY\",\"title\":\"Lecture 1A | MIT 6.001 Structure and Interpretation, 1986\",\"channel\":\"MIT OpenCourseWare\",\"summary\":\"The first lecture of MIT's 1986 6.001 Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs, given by Harold Abelson, introducing Lisp and the idea of computer science as the study of process.\",\"why_hn\":\"Seen as a classic and often the moment programming 'clicks'; commenters praise Abelson's opening that computer science is not about computers.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming fundamentals\",\"categories\":[\"University lectures & courses\",\"Functional programming\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"sicp\",\"scheme\",\"lisp\",\"abstraction\",\"computer science\",\"mit\"],\"quote\":\"blew my mind and made me switch from being a UX designer dabbling in Rails to a full blown programmer.\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"4Z2uzEM0ugY\",\"title\":\"Christopher Hitchens - Free Speech\",\"channel\":\"hitch archive\",\"summary\":\"Christopher Hitchens delivers a speech defending freedom of speech, including the right of audiences to hear offensive speakers and a take on the fire-in-a-crowded-theatre line. It is framed around the Charlie Hebdo attack.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN invokes it in nearly every content moderation and censorship thread as the most eloquent case for free speech.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"free speech\",\"categories\":[\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\"],\"tags\":[\"free speech\",\"christopher hitchens\",\"censorship\",\"debate\",\"civil liberties\"],\"quote\":\"There is a brilliant speech by the late Christopher Hitchens [0] on free speech that I must have seen a dozen times.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"8--5LwHRhjk\",\"title\":\"Painting a Character with Maths\",\"channel\":\"Inigo Quilez\",\"summary\":\"Inigo Quilez live-codes a cartoon character painting entirely from math, using signed distance fields in a Shadertoy shader. He explains each step of building the image from formulas.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly calls it a mind-blowing and accessible introduction to SDFs and procedural art from a master explainer.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"signed distance fields\",\"categories\":[\"Creative coding & generative art\",\"Computer graphics & rendering\"],\"tags\":[\"sdf\",\"shaders\",\"shadertoy\",\"procedural art\",\"raymarching\",\"glsl\"],\"quote\":\"he's a master of this stuff and a great explainer.\",\"recommenders\":13,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"F-QA2rkpBSY\",\"title\":\"The Most IMPORTANT Video You'll Ever See (part 1 of 8)\",\"channel\":\"wonderingmind42\",\"summary\":\"Part 1 of a lecture by physicist Albert Bartlett on the exponential function and its consequences for population growth, resource use and energy. It argues that steady growth rates quickly lead to enormous quantities.\",\"why_hn\":\"A perennial HN recommendation for building intuition about exponential growth, cited whenever endless growth, energy or population come up.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"exponential growth\",\"categories\":[\"University lectures & courses\",\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"albert bartlett\",\"exponential function\",\"population growth\",\"sustainability\",\"energy\"],\"quote\":\"stick with it; it's really captivating and interesting\",\"recommenders\":13,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"FvmTSpJU-Xc\",\"title\":\"Alan Kay - Normal Considered Harmful\",\"channel\":\"powerfuloutlet\",\"summary\":\"Alan Kay's 2009 UIUC talk arguing that computing is more a pop culture than a science and that ignoring history and fundamentals leads to inferior solutions. He touches on the history of interactive environments and what the web should have been.\",\"why_hn\":\"A staple on must-watch lists, recommended as a starting point for Kay's thinking and for why software is often poor and ahistorical.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"computing history and philosophy\",\"categories\":[\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Software architecture & design\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"alan kay\",\"software engineering\",\"computing history\",\"web design\",\"pop culture\"],\"quote\":\"his talk 'Normal Considered Harmful' in particular is worth a look as it pretty much goes straight to the heart of the issue\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"Ggz_gd--UO0\",\"title\":\"Antonin Scalia - On American Exceptionalism\",\"channel\":\"AmericanRhetoric.com\",\"summary\":\"Justice Antonin Scalia's opening statement at a 2011 Senate Judiciary hearing on the role of judges. He argues that the Constitution's separation of powers is what protects liberty, and that gridlock is by design.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended often as a clear explanation of why the US system is built to be slow and why gridlock protects minorities.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"us constitutional design\",\"categories\":[\"Law, policing & justice\",\"US politics & elections\"],\"tags\":[\"scalia\",\"separation of powers\",\"gridlock\",\"constitution\",\"senate hearing\"],\"quote\":\"one of the best explanations for why founding fathers built the system the way they did is this[1], by late Justice Scalia.\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"HyzD8pNlpwI\",\"title\":\"The Ultimate Game Boy Talk (33c3)\",\"channel\":\"media.ccc.de\",\"summary\":\"Michael Steil's 33c3 talk explains essentially everything about the Game Boy: CPU, video hardware, peripherals, and the tricks used by games and demos. It revives the era of counting clock cycles around hardware limits.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the standard starting point for Game Boy emulator writers and homebrew developers, with several calling it one of their favorite talks.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"game boy hardware\",\"categories\":[\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\"],\"tags\":[\"game boy\",\"emulation\",\"retro hardware\",\"ppu\",\"33c3\"],\"quote\":\"I'd recommend watching The Ultimate Game Boy Talk [1] before trying to dive into any code.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"JxAXlJEmNMg\",\"title\":\"Crockford on JavaScript - Volume 1: The Early Years\",\"channel\":\"YUI Library\",\"summary\":\"Douglas Crockford's first Yahoo lecture puts JavaScript in historical context. He traces the language's structure and quirks back through the early decades of computer science and programming language history.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends the Crockford series as a must-watch history of computing and programming languages, and as a way to learn to think in JavaScript.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of javascript and programming languages\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"douglas crockford\",\"javascript\",\"computing history\",\"programming languages\",\"web history\"],\"quote\":\"I urge you to watch this. It really is worth it!\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"ORzNZUeUHAM\",\"title\":\"Stroads are Ugly, Expensive, and Dangerous (and they're everywhere) [ST05]\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes introduces the \\\"stroad\\\", a street-road hybrid common in the US and Canada that fails at both jobs. He contrasts it with Dutch design, where each road has a single purpose, and covers cost, danger and ugliness.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it constantly as the standard introduction to stroads and Strong Towns-style arguments about car-centric design.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"urban planning and road design\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Civil engineering & construction\"],\"tags\":[\"stroads\",\"strong towns\",\"netherlands\",\"road safety\",\"car-centric design\"],\"quote\":\"This video from the Not Just Bikes channel shows the stark difference between a street and a road\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"QmOF0crdyRU\",\"title\":\"Controlling Your Dopamine For Motivation, Focus & Satisfaction\",\"channel\":\"Andrew Huberman\",\"summary\":\"Andrew Huberman's long Huberman Lab episode is a masterclass on dopamine. It covers the neural circuits, peaks and baselines, dopamine depletion, and 14 tools for motivation, focus and avoiding addiction.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it repeatedly to people struggling with motivation, distraction or phone and internet addiction, as a science-based guide to dopamine.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"dopamine and motivation\",\"categories\":[\"Neuroscience & the brain\",\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"dopamine\",\"motivation\",\"focus\",\"addiction\",\"neuroscience\"],\"quote\":\"I'm obsessed with this podcast by Andrew Huberman (Stanford Neuroscientist) because he's talking about a scientific basis for stuff that has \\\"felt\\\" true to me for a while.\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"SWEts0rlezA\",\"title\":\"RailsConf 2016 -  Turbolinks 5: I Can’t Believe It’s Not Native! by Sam Stephenson\",\"channel\":\"Confreaks\",\"summary\":\"Sam Stephenson's RailsConf 2016 talk on Turbolinks 5, showing how small teams can ship fast Rails apps in the browser and hybrid iOS and Android apps sharing one set of web views.\",\"why_hn\":\"Repeatedly urged on people choosing a stack, as a case against heavy SPA complexity and for shared web and native code.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"rails turbolinks hybrid apps\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"turbolinks\",\"rails\",\"basecamp\",\"hybrid mobile apps\",\"spa alternatives\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend this talk on managing the complexity of basecamp and sharing a codebase between web and native apps.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"YSfCdBBqNXY\",\"title\":\"ADHD: Essential Ideas for Parents - Dr. Russell Barkely\",\"channel\":\"ADHDtips\",\"summary\":\"Dr. Russell Barkley gives a nearly three-hour lecture of essential ideas about ADHD for parents, covering what ADHD is, its effects on executive function, motivation and emotion, and how it affects children.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN readers recommend it as the best primer for understanding ADHD at a neurological level, often suggesting that even the first 10 to 20 minutes are enlightening.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"ADHD\",\"categories\":[\"Mental health & neurodiversity\"],\"tags\":[\"adhd\",\"russell barkley\",\"executive function\",\"parenting\",\"emotional regulation\"],\"quote\":\"You don't need to watch the entire 3 hours. The first 10 minutes should be fairly enlightening.\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"_ahvzDzKdB0\",\"title\":\"Growing a Language, by Guy Steele\",\"channel\":\"Bill Pugh\",\"summary\":\"Guy Steele's 1998 OOPSLA keynote on designing a programming language that its users can grow. He builds the talk using only one-syllable words and terms he has defined, to demonstrate the idea.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN considers it one of the best technical talks ever and cites it in discussions of language design, small cores and extensibility.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming language design\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"guy steele\",\"growing a language\",\"oopsla\",\"language extensibility\",\"lisp\",\"java\"],\"quote\":\"This is probably the best talk ever on the general topic\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"ajGX7odA87k\",\"title\":\"USENIX Security '18-Q: Why Do Keynote Speakers Keep Suggesting That Improving Security Is Possible?\",\"channel\":\"USENIX\",\"summary\":\"James Mickens's humorous USENIX Security keynote argues that improving security and trusting opaque technology like machine learning is far harder than the industry admits.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN considers it a classic, hilarious and sobering talk on ML inscrutability and tech hubris, often rewatched.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"security and ml skepticism\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Satire & parody\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\"],\"tags\":[\"james mickens\",\"security\",\"machine learning\",\"humor\",\"keynote\"],\"quote\":\"His keynote at USENIX Security 2018 is by far the best summation of the dangers of ML that I've ever seen.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"bmSAYlu0NcY\",\"title\":\"A Philosophy of Software Design | John Ousterhout | Talks at Google\",\"channel\":\"Talks at Google\",\"summary\":\"John Ousterhout, Stanford professor and creator of Tcl, presents the ideas of his book A Philosophy of Software Design in a Talks at Google session. He covers managing complexity, deep modules and interfaces, and what he teaches in his Stanford software design course.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the standard one-hour teaser for the book and recommends it for its deep-modules thinking before buying or reading the book.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software design\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"deep modules\",\"complexity\",\"abstraction\",\"john ousterhout\",\"api design\"],\"quote\":\"This google talk is a condensed version of the (already small - 150 pags IIRC) book. I highly recommend both.\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"eG0KrT6pBPk\",\"title\":\"Defcon 18   Changing threats to privacy   Moxie Marlinspike   Part \",\"channel\":\"killab66661\",\"summary\":\"Moxie Marlinspike's DEF CON 18 talk on changing threats to privacy argues that surveillance arrived through convenient services and network effects, so opting out is not a real solution. It also includes his framing of getting social democracy rather than the fascism people feared.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited repeatedly to rebut 'just don't use Facebook' arguments and to explain why tracking becomes hard to escape at critical mass.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"surveillance and privacy\",\"categories\":[\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Security & hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"privacy\",\"surveillance\",\"network effects\",\"moxie marlinspike\",\"defcon\",\"tracking\"],\"quote\":\"I would highly encourage anyone who thinks that the solution is as simple as \\\"don't use XYZ\\\" to see what Moxie Marlinspike had to say\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"ecIWPzGEbFc\",\"title\":\"\\\"Uncle\\\" Bob Martin - \\\"The Future of Programming\\\"\",\"channel\":\"Paul Stringer's Mobile Tech\",\"summary\":\"Robert C. Martin (Uncle Bob) traces the history of software from 1948 to today, looking at the problems programmers faced, how they were solved, and what mistakes the profession made. He then projects where the field is going, including professionalism and regulation.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an engaging overview of programming history and an argument for professional standards, citing the agile manifesto section and the claim that software has advanced little since the 1950s.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of programming\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"software history\",\"professionalism\",\"agile\",\"regulation\",\"programming languages\"],\"quote\":\"It's a fascinating introduction to the evolution of hardware and programming languages.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"kZRE7HIO3vk\",\"title\":\"The Thirty Million Line Problem\",\"channel\":\"Molly Rocket\",\"summary\":\"Casey Muratori's long talk arguing that modern software has ballooned to tens of millions of lines because of hardware and OS diversity. He proposes a stable ISA for entire system-on-a-chip packages and recalls when games shipped with their own boot disk.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it, long as it is, as an eye-opening rant on software complexity and why systems became so complicated.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software complexity\",\"categories\":[\"Software performance & optimization\",\"Operating systems & kernels\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\"],\"tags\":[\"casey muratori\",\"operating systems\",\"instruction set architecture\",\"software bloat\",\"hardware\"],\"quote\":\"The video is long, but it's worth watching all of it.\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"ncHmEUmJZf4\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2017: Matt Kulukundis “Designing a Fast, Efficient, Cache-friendly Hash Table, Step by Step”\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Matt Kulukundis explains how Google designed SwissTable, a fast, cache-friendly hash table, step by step from std::unordered_map. The talk covers open addressing, SIMD probing and the tradeoffs of changing a fundamental data structure.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly cites it as the definitive explanation of SwissTable, the design behind Abseil, Rust's hash map and Go's maps.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"hash table design\",\"categories\":[\"Algorithms & data structures\",\"Software performance & optimization\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"swisstable\",\"hash tables\",\"c++\",\"simd\",\"abseil\",\"cache efficiency\"],\"quote\":\"Excellent talk which explains the concepts really clearly and concisely.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":12.0},{\"vid\":\"1gEz__sMVaY\",\"title\":\"The Cardboard Box Reform - Nixon's Ghost Bill & A Crucial Flaw in Democracy\",\"channel\":\"CRI\",\"summary\":\"James D'Angelo argues that the 1970 Legislative Reorganization Act made congressional votes public, opening the door to lobbyists and special interests. He proposes restoring secret voting as a cheap fix for corruption and many social ills.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a long, evidence-backed theory for what changed in the 1970s and why Congress is broken, even when they are unsure they agree.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"congressional transparency and lobbying\",\"categories\":[\"US politics & elections\",\"Law, policing & justice\"],\"tags\":[\"legislative reorganization act\",\"secret ballot\",\"lobbying\",\"congress\",\"political reform\"],\"quote\":\"It is long, but well-reasoned and supported by a lot of evidence. Definitely worth a watch...\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"2V1FtfBDsLU\",\"title\":\"Effective Programs - 10 Years of Clojure - Rich Hickey\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"Rich Hickey's talk at Clojure's tenth anniversary on the kind of programs Clojure is designed for. He argues for situated programs that deal with changing requirements, and for dynamic typing and data over static types.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN's go-to link in typing debates and for talk lists, and several call it their favorite programming talk even for non-Clojure users.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software design philosophy\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Programming language design\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"rich hickey\",\"clojure\",\"dynamic typing\",\"situated programs\",\"data-oriented programming\"],\"quote\":\"Whole video is gold, is my fav programming talk of all time.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"2y5Pv4yN0b0\",\"title\":\"Brian Goetz - Stewardship: the Sobering Parts\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"Java Language Architect Brian Goetz talks at a Clojure conference about the challenges of evolving a mature language like Java while preserving compatibility with existing code and developer expectations. It also gives a look at where the platform is headed.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as the definitive explanation of why Java evolves cautiously and why compatibility matters in language design.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"language evolution and compatibility\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"java\",\"language design\",\"backward compatibility\",\"brian goetz\",\"jvm\"],\"quote\":\"Brian Goetz's talk nails it on the head why the languages in my day-to-day are sub-optimal\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"ERbvKrH-GC4\",\"title\":\"Music and Life - Alan Watts\",\"channel\":\"seandulac\",\"summary\":\"A two-minute animation of an Alan Watts talk arguing that life is like music: it is meant to be danced to in the moment, not rushed toward a final goal. It is a short, accessible take on living in the present.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters post it in threads about purpose, ambition, burnout and career anxiety as a reframing that life is a dance, not a march toward eventual happiness.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"philosophy of life\",\"categories\":[\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"alan watts\",\"present moment\",\"purpose\",\"happiness\",\"animation\"],\"quote\":\"You haven't been failing, you've been dancing.\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"EY6q5dv_B-o\",\"title\":\"VCF East: Ken Thompson interviewed by Brian Kernighan\",\"channel\":\"Vintage Computer Federation\",\"summary\":\"Brian Kernighan interviews Unix co-creator Ken Thompson at VCF East 2019. Thompson recounts how he was hired at Bell Labs, writing the Unix kernel in about three weeks, the origins of B and TMG, pipes, and other anecdotes.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it for Thompson's funny, first-hand Unix history stories, some of which even Kernighan had not heard.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"unix history\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Operating systems & kernels\"],\"tags\":[\"ken thompson\",\"brian kernighan\",\"unix\",\"bell labs\",\"computing history\",\"tmg\"],\"quote\":\"Also I found these fascinating\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"HaEPXoXVf2k\",\"title\":\"AWS re:Invent 2018: Amazon DynamoDB Deep Dive: Advanced Design Patterns for DynamoDB (DAT401)\",\"channel\":\"Amazon Web Services\",\"summary\":\"Rick Houlihan's AWS re:Invent 2018 deep dive on advanced DynamoDB design patterns. It covers index overloading, GSI sharding, composite keys, graph processing and transactions.\",\"why_hn\":\"The go-to recommendation for learning DynamoDB data modeling, with many saying it made access-pattern-driven design click.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"dynamodb data modeling\",\"categories\":[\"Databases & storage\",\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\"],\"tags\":[\"dynamodb\",\"nosql\",\"single-table design\",\"aws\",\"reinvent\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend every engineer to watch it: it's a great overview of SQL vs NoSQL\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"JcJSW7Rprio\",\"title\":\"Harder Drive: Hard drives we didn't want or need\",\"channel\":\"suckerpinch\",\"summary\":\"Tom7 (suckerpinch) builds and evaluates a series of absurd hard drives, including storage made from ping packets across the internet. The video accompanies his paper and mixes deep technical hacking with comedic storytelling.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as a favorite example of silly but educational computer science, recommended often when threads touch odd storage hacks.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"esoteric data storage\",\"categories\":[\"Hardware (other)\",\"Networking & internet infrastructure\",\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\"],\"tags\":[\"tom7\",\"ping storage\",\"hilbert curve\",\"hacks\",\"humor\"],\"quote\":\"recommend this video to anyone here.\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"KkpqA8yG9T4\",\"title\":\"Breakthrough in  Nuclear Fusion? - Prof. Dennis Whyte\",\"channel\":\"MIT Club of Northern California\",\"summary\":\"MIT's Prof. Dennis Whyte explains the ARC/SPARC compact tokamak approach, in which new high-temperature superconducting magnets allow smaller and cheaper fusion reactors. He covers fusion physics, performance trends and the engineering path to net energy.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the grounded, approachable explanation of why REBCO magnets changed the outlook for fusion.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"nuclear fusion\",\"categories\":[\"Nuclear power & fusion\"],\"tags\":[\"tokamak\",\"sparc\",\"superconducting magnets\",\"rebco\",\"mit\",\"plasma\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend watching Dr. Whyte's talks on YouTube, he discusses the challenges, design, and performance in an approachable way.\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"NQFqDKRAROI\",\"title\":\"The Solar Gravitational Lens will Map Exoplanets. Seriously.\",\"channel\":\"Launch Pad Astronomy\",\"summary\":\"Explains the proposed NASA JPL plan to use the Sun as a gravitational lens, sending solar sail craft to its focal region to image an exoplanet's surface. It covers the distances involved, how imaging with the Sun would work and the mission design.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN strongly recommends it as the clearest, best-illustrated explanation of the solar gravitational lens idea, and links it whenever exoplanet imaging comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"solar gravitational lens\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"Space exploration & spaceflight\"],\"tags\":[\"exoplanet imaging\",\"gravitational lensing\",\"solar sails\",\"nasa jpl\",\"space telescope\"],\"quote\":\"It's got some great graphics and visualisations, and is accessibly narrated.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"RIctzAQOe44\",\"title\":\"The Real Story Behind Wayland and X - Daniel Stone (linux.conf.au 2013)\",\"channel\":\"Mladen Mijatov\",\"summary\":\"Daniel Stone, a longtime X and Wayland developer, explains at linux.conf.au 2013 why X11's design became untenable and what motivated Wayland. The talk walks through X's history and accumulated cruft.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the definitive, funny and educational answer to why X could not be fixed, and links it in almost every Wayland vs X argument.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"wayland vs x11\",\"categories\":[\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\",\"Computer graphics & rendering\"],\"tags\":[\"wayland\",\"x11\",\"linux graphics\",\"display servers\",\"daniel stone\"],\"quote\":\"incredibly funny and educational talk[1] from 2013\",\"recommenders\":13,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"UzxYlbK2c7E\",\"title\":\"Lecture 1 | Machine Learning (Stanford)\",\"channel\":\"Stanford\",\"summary\":\"The first lecture of Andrew Ng's Stanford CS229 Machine Learning course, giving an overview of the course. It introduces supervised and unsupervised learning, learning theory and reinforcement learning.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the rigorous, math-heavy original course, deeper than the Coursera version, for learning machine learning.\",\"content_type\":\"course\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"machine learning fundamentals\",\"categories\":[\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"andrew ng\",\"cs229\",\"supervised learning\",\"stanford\",\"machine learning course\"],\"quote\":\"I'd suggest going back to the original youtube[1] and course materials[2].\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"WpkDN78P884\",\"title\":\"Ruby Midwest 2011 - Keynote: Architecture the Lost Years by Robert Martin\",\"channel\":\"Confreaks\",\"summary\":\"Robert C. Martin's Ruby Midwest 2011 keynote argues that software architecture should express the application's intent rather than its framework. He covers separating entities, interactors and boundaries so the web, database and framework are details.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently recommended as an influential talk on decoupled, testable architecture, though some commenters push back on Martin's TDD advocacy.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software architecture\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"uncle bob\",\"clean architecture\",\"interactors\",\"testing\",\"frameworks\"],\"quote\":\"The talk that had the most influence on how I write software is Robert 'Uncle Bob' Martin's \\\"Architecture: The Lost Years\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"WwslBPj8GgI\",\"title\":\"Confessions of a Converted Lecturer: Eric Mazur\",\"channel\":\"UMBCtube\",\"summary\":\"Harvard physicist Eric Mazur explains how he realized his students were memorizing rather than understanding, and how he changed his teaching. He presents peer instruction and data showing improved student performance.\",\"why_hn\":\"Repeatedly recommended in education threads as the definitive talk on why lectures fail and what works better.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"peer instruction teaching\",\"categories\":[\"Education & learning\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"eric mazur\",\"peer instruction\",\"physics education\",\"active learning\",\"lectures\"],\"quote\":\"Great video on how a pretty good teacher (as conventionally measured) learned how to really teach.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"a1zDuOPkMSw\",\"title\":\"Hamming, \\\"You and Your Research\\\" (June 6, 1995)\",\"channel\":\"securitylectures\",\"summary\":\"Richard Hamming's 1995 recording of his talk 'You and Your Research', drawn from his graduate engineering course. He discusses how great scientists pick important problems and build careers, drawing on his years at Bell Labs.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as career and research advice on choosing important problems and doing first-class work.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"research career advice\",\"categories\":[\"Productivity & self-improvement\",\"Scientific method & science communication\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"richard hamming\",\"bell labs\",\"important problems\",\"career\",\"learning to learn\"],\"quote\":\"So much sage advice as compiled over a very long and fruitful career.\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"aKLntZcp27M\",\"title\":\"RustConf 2018 - Closing Keynote - Using Rust For Game Development by Catherine West\",\"channel\":\"Rust Programming Language\",\"summary\":\"Catherine West's RustConf 2018 closing keynote on building a game in Rust and organizing a large codebase. It builds up an entity component system and generational indices step by step.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the go-to explanation of ECS, valuable even for people who don't use Rust.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"entity component systems\",\"categories\":[\"Game programming & engines\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"ecs\",\"rust\",\"game development\",\"generational indices\",\"architecture\"],\"quote\":\"is still my favorite ECS explanation, because she builds the concept piece by piece starting from megaclass-hell to full entities-as-IDs with reasons for each step.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"c12cYAUTXXs\",\"title\":\"Erlang Factory 2014 - That's 'Billion' with a 'B': Scaling to the Next Level at WhatsApp\",\"channel\":\"Erlang Solutions\",\"summary\":\"Rick Reed of WhatsApp explains at Erlang Factory 2014 how the service scaled to billions of messages with a very small engineering team. He covers the FreeBSD and Erlang stack, Mnesia usage, server counts and bottleneck work.\",\"why_hn\":\"The go-to talk showing how Erlang let a tiny team run a massive messaging system.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"scaling erlang systems\",\"categories\":[\"Distributed systems\",\"Functional programming\",\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"erlang\",\"whatsapp\",\"scalability\",\"mnesia\",\"freebsd\",\"performance\"],\"quote\":\"watch this talk and imagine trying to recreate that system with only a handful of server engineers\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"d0nERTFo-Sk\",\"title\":\"Fear the Boom and Bust: Keynes vs. Hayek - The Original Economics Rap Battle!\",\"channel\":\"Radical Discourse\",\"summary\":\"A rap battle in which John Maynard Keynes and F. A. Hayek debate the causes of boom and bust, stimulus, and bailouts. It was produced by Econstories to teach the two schools of thought.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a funny, accurate and easy primer on Keynesian versus Austrian views of the business cycle.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"keynes versus hayek\",\"categories\":[\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\",\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\",\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"economics\",\"keynes\",\"hayek\",\"business cycle\",\"rap\"],\"quote\":\"the Keynes vs. Hayek rap is incredibly well informed about both positions, and it's pretty easy to follow and entertaining\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"dP1xVpMPn8M\",\"title\":\"A Tour of the Acme Editor\",\"channel\":\"Russ Cox\",\"summary\":\"Russ Cox gives a 23-minute tour of the Acme text editor from Plan 9, showing its mouse-driven interface, executing commands from text, and its filesystem-based interface to other programs.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points people to it as the best introduction to Acme and its very different take on editors, shells, and GUIs.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"acme text editor\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"GUI, HCI & interface history\"],\"tags\":[\"acme\",\"plan 9\",\"text editors\",\"russ cox\",\"mouse interface\"],\"quote\":\"Russ Cox has a great video tour of Acme's features here\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"eviSykqSUUw\",\"title\":\"A Deep Dive into Nanite Virtualized Geometry\",\"channel\":\"SIGGRAPH Advances in Real-Time Rendering\",\"summary\":\"Brian Karis's SIGGRAPH 2021 talk explains how Unreal Engine 5's Nanite virtualized geometry works. It covers mesh import, the data structures, streaming, decompression, culling, software and hardware rasterization, and shading.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the definitive deep dive on Nanite, admired for the engineering ambition behind pixel-sized triangles and compute-driven rendering.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"virtualized geometry rendering\",\"categories\":[\"Computer graphics & rendering\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"nanite\",\"unreal engine 5\",\"software rasterizer\",\"level of detail\",\"gpu rendering\",\"siggraph\"],\"quote\":\"This siggraph session will expand your mind\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"f84n5oFoZBc\",\"title\":\"Hammock Driven Development - Rich Hickey\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"Rich Hickey's philosophical talk from the first Clojure Conj in 2010. He argues that good design comes from deliberate, away-from-keyboard thinking, and describes how to feed the background mind so it solves problems.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it constantly as permission and method for thinking deeply before coding, and as an antidote to always-working anxiety.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"problem solving and design thinking\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Software architecture & design\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"rich hickey\",\"clojure\",\"design\",\"deep thinking\",\"software craftsmanship\",\"productivity\"],\"quote\":\"Both of these talks are in my top 5.\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"gwf5mAlI7Ug\",\"title\":\"U.S. NAVY  BASIC MECHANISMS OF FIRE CONTROL COMPUTERS  MECHANICAL COMPUTER INSTRUCTIONAL FILM  27794\",\"channel\":\"PeriscopeFilm\",\"summary\":\"A 1953 US Navy training film on the basic mechanisms in fire control computers: shafts, gears, cams and differentials. It shows how these mechanical parts compute ship and target firing solutions.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the classic demonstration of how analog mechanical computation works, especially the differential gear section.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"mechanical analog computers\",\"categories\":[\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"History of computing\",\"Military history & warfare\"],\"tags\":[\"fire control computer\",\"differential gears\",\"cams\",\"navy training film\",\"analog computing\"],\"quote\":\"I especially like the section on differential gears, demonstrating their use as computing units\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"hQVTIJBZook\",\"title\":\"JavaScript: The Good Parts\",\"channel\":\"Google TechTalks\",\"summary\":\"Douglas Crockford's 2009 Google tech talk on the good and bad parts of JavaScript, based on his book. He argues for using a reliable subset of the language and avoiding its flawed features.\",\"why_hn\":\"A classic recommended to people learning JavaScript, even though some find it dogmatic and dated.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"javascript language design\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Programming language design\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"javascript\",\"douglas crockford\",\"good parts\",\"best practices\",\"web\"],\"quote\":\"This video changed my mind\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"hUhisi2FBuw\",\"title\":\"The Ingenious Design of the Aluminum Beverage Can\",\"channel\":\"engineerguy\",\"summary\":\"Bill Hammack of Engineer Guy explains the engineering behind the aluminum beverage can: why it is cylindrical, how it is manufactured, why it narrows at the top, the double seam, and how the pull tab works.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is a go-to recommendation whenever cans, good design or mass-produced objects come up, and many also recommend the whole channel.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"manufacturing and product design\",\"categories\":[\"Manufacturing & industrial processes\",\"Product & industrial design\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"aluminum can\",\"engineering\",\"pull tab\",\"industrial design\",\"engineerguy\"],\"quote\":\"Whenever it’s about cans I can only recommend this video from the engineer guy\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"izxXGuVL21o\",\"title\":\"How Crash Bandicoot Hacked The Original Playstation | War Stories | Ars Technica\",\"channel\":\"Ars Technica\",\"summary\":\"In Ars Technica's War Stories, Naughty Dog co-founder Andy Gavin explains how the team squeezed Crash Bandicoot onto the original PlayStation. They overwrote Sony library code in RAM, built custom animation compression, and streamed assets around slow seek times.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is one of the most frequently recommended videos on HN for old-school game development hacks under tight hardware constraints.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"playstation game development\",\"categories\":[\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Game programming & engines\",\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"crash bandicoot\",\"naughty dog\",\"playstation\",\"memory optimization\",\"game history\"],\"quote\":\"Immensely interesting video!\",\"recommenders\":13,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"qv6UVOQ0F44\",\"title\":\"MarI/O - Machine Learning for Video Games\",\"channel\":\"SethBling\",\"summary\":\"SethBling shows MarI/O, a program using neural networks and the NEAT genetic algorithm to learn to play Super Mario World. The description links the source code, the paper and background reading.\",\"why_hn\":\"The go-to accessible introduction to neuroevolution and NEAT, praised for crediting prior research.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"neuroevolution\",\"categories\":[\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"Speedrunning, glitches & game hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"neat\",\"genetic algorithms\",\"neural networks\",\"super mario world\",\"machine learning\"],\"quote\":\"his brief videos give a really nice introduction to the topic that should be accessible to most people\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"sWblpsLZ-O8\",\"title\":\"A Mind Is Born (256 bytes)\",\"channel\":\"lftkryo\",\"summary\":\"A Commodore 64 demo by Linus Akesson that fits video and music into 256 bytes, winning the Oldskool 4K Intro compo at Revision 2017. A technical write-up accompanies it.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is HN's favorite example of extreme code compression, a compelling song and visuals from a program smaller than a tweet.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"demoscene size coding\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\"],\"tags\":[\"demoscene\",\"commodore 64\",\"256 bytes\",\"chiptune\",\"linus akesson\"],\"quote\":\"not only because it is 256 bytes, but because it is a compelling song too.\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"uxykI30fS54\",\"title\":\"Why This Channel Exists (and why I hate Houston)\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes explains why the channel exists, through the creator's walk in suburban Houston. It shows how car-dependent design makes cities hostile to pedestrians.\",\"why_hn\":\"Strongly recommended as the entry point to a channel on urban planning and car dependency in North America.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"car-dependent city design\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\"],\"tags\":[\"urban planning\",\"houston\",\"walkability\",\"car dependency\",\"not just bikes\"],\"quote\":\"I watched an excellent video about Houston\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"x1bX3F7uTrg\",\"title\":\"The Loving Trap\",\"channel\":\"Ben Woodhams\",\"summary\":\"A three-minute parody of Adam Curtis's documentary style, assembled from archive footage with his typical narration and leaps of logic.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the standard companion to any Adam Curtis film, since it lampoons the style and gives reasons to take his claims with skepticism.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"adam curtis parody\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"adam curtis\",\"parody\",\"documentary style\",\"archive footage\",\"media criticism\"],\"quote\":\"You should always acompany this documentary with a watch of this short little video.\",\"recommenders\":15,\"score\":11.5},{\"vid\":\"-zRN7XLCRhc\",\"title\":\"LISA11 - Fork Yeah! The Rise and Development of illumos\",\"channel\":\"USENIX\",\"summary\":\"Bryan Cantrill's LISA11 talk on how illumos arose after Oracle closed OpenSolaris. It covers the history of Sun, ZFS, DTrace and Zones, and the diaspora of kernel engineers, with a famous rant about Oracle and Larry Ellison.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the definitive, entertaining account of Sun's fate under Oracle, and quotes its Ellison lawnmower advice whenever Oracle behaves badly.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"illumos and oracle history\",\"categories\":[\"Operating systems & kernels\",\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\",\"Tech company stories & documentaries\"],\"tags\":[\"illumos\",\"opensolaris\",\"oracle\",\"sun microsystems\",\"bryan cantrill\",\"open source\"],\"quote\":\"If you're interested how it came to be check out this epic talk by Bryan Cantrill\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":11.0},{\"vid\":\"4XpnKHJAok8\",\"title\":\"Tech Talk: Linus Torvalds on git\",\"channel\":\"Google\",\"summary\":\"Linus Torvalds gives a Google tech talk on git, the source control system he created two years earlier. He argues for distributed version control over centralized systems such as CVS and Subversion.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as the iconic explanation of why git is designed as it is and why distributed VCS beats centralized ones.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"distributed version control\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"git\",\"linus torvalds\",\"version control\",\"subversion\",\"linux\"],\"quote\":\"This talk by Linus is iconic, if you haven't watched it you should.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":11.0}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"7IsMeKl-Sv0\",\"title\":\"Why American Cities Are Broke - The Growth Ponzi Scheme [ST03]\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes explains the Strong Towns argument that car-dependent American suburbs cost more to maintain than they generate in taxes. It describes the growth Ponzi scheme that props up municipal finances.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN keeps pointing to it as the clearest explanation of how low-density infrastructure is financed and why it is unsustainable.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"municipal finance and suburban sprawl\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\"],\"tags\":[\"strong towns\",\"urban planning\",\"suburbs\",\"infrastructure costs\",\"car dependence\"],\"quote\":\"I can't recommend this video [1] by Not Just Bikes enough.\",\"recommenders\":14,\"score\":11.0},{\"vid\":\"7xTGNNLPyMI\",\"title\":\"Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT\",\"channel\":\"Andrej Karpathy\",\"summary\":\"Andrej Karpathy's three-hour-plus general-audience deep dive into how ChatGPT-style LLMs are built, covering the full training stack from pretraining to post-training. It also gives mental models for their quirks, like hallucination, and how to use them well.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN's most frequent recommendation for a clear, approachable foundation in how LLMs work without heavy math.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"large language models\",\"categories\":[\"LLMs & generative AI\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\"],\"tags\":[\"llm training\",\"chatgpt\",\"pretraining\",\"hallucination\",\"tokenization\",\"andrej karpathy\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend Andrej Karpathy's Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":11.0},{\"vid\":\"AnrlSqtpOkw\",\"title\":\"Alan Kay's tribute to Ted Nelson at \\\"Intertwingled\\\" Festival\",\"channel\":\"TheTedNelson\",\"summary\":\"Alan Kay's tribute to Ted Nelson at the Intertwingled festival, presented using a revived Smalltalk-78 system running on emulated 1970s Xerox PARC hardware. It shows live, user-programmable software from that era.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it again and again as a demonstration of how programmable and composable 1970s systems were compared with today's.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"smalltalk and xerox parc\",\"categories\":[\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"alan kay\",\"smalltalk-78\",\"ted nelson\",\"xerox parc\",\"malleable software\",\"computer history\"],\"quote\":\"a great example of what's not-even-close to possible on today's systems\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":11.0},{\"vid\":\"DnPmg0R1M04\",\"title\":\"The Century of the Self - Part 1: \\\"Happiness Machines\\\"\",\"channel\":\"JustAdamCurtis\",\"summary\":\"The first part of Adam Curtis' documentary series on Sigmund Freud and his nephew Edward Bernays, who invented public relations. It shows how Bernays used Freud's ideas to link mass-produced goods to unconscious desires and create modern consumer persuasion.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it constantly in threads about advertising, propaganda and manipulation as the origin story of the consumer society.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of public relations\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"adam curtis\",\"edward bernays\",\"freud\",\"advertising\",\"propaganda\",\"consumerism\"],\"quote\":\"There's a great four-part documentary by Adam Curtis about Edward Bernays\",\"recommenders\":13,\"score\":11.0},{\"vid\":\"JjDsP5n2kSM\",\"title\":\"Jonathan Blow - How to program independent games -  CSUA Speech\",\"channel\":\"Alexandre Franco\",\"summary\":\"Jonathan Blow speaks at UC Berkeley's CSUA about how to program independent games. He argues for keeping code simple, avoiding unnecessary work, and shipping.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a talk whose advice on simplicity and avoiding unnecessary work applies well beyond games.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"indie game programming\",\"categories\":[\"Indie game development\",\"Game programming & engines\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"jonathan blow\",\"simplicity\",\"premature optimization\",\"shipping software\",\"game development\"],\"quote\":\"It's about much more than games. To me, it's about identifying and not doing unnecessary work.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":11.0},{\"vid\":\"O5BJVO3PDeQ\",\"title\":\"The Engoodening of No Man's Sky\",\"channel\":\"Internet Historian\",\"summary\":\"Internet Historian tells the story of No Man's Sky, from its over-hyped and badly received launch to years of updates by Hello Games that redeemed the game. It is a long, comedic mini-documentary.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently recommended as an entertaining account of a failed launch and recovery, especially in threads about Star Citizen and over-promising.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"no man's sky history\",\"categories\":[\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Gaming culture & analysis\",\"Indie game development\"],\"tags\":[\"no man's sky\",\"hello games\",\"game development\",\"video game history\",\"sean murray\"],\"quote\":\"surprisingly a well told, heart-warming (albeit quite long) video telling the story of No Man's Sky\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":11.0},{\"vid\":\"ShEez0JkOFw\",\"title\":\"Tim Ewald - Clojure: Programming with Hand Tools\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"Tim Ewald's Clojure/conj talk uses furniture making with hand tools versus machine tools as an allegory for software. It explores how automation changes what we make and how we think about our work, and why Clojure's small set of simple tools matters.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly names it among the best programming talks, loved for its craftsmanship message that complex tooling can hide simpler solutions.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"craftsmanship and simple tools in software\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Woodworking, metalworking & workshop skills\"],\"tags\":[\"clojure\",\"woodworking\",\"hand tools\",\"automation\",\"craftsmanship\",\"simplicity\"],\"quote\":\"I loved this talk on how as your tooling becomes more complex and automated, you start to lose sight of simpler solutions to problems.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":11.0},{\"vid\":\"ZhuHCtR3xq8\",\"title\":\"Brian Beckman: Don't fear the Monad\",\"channel\":\"jasonofthel33t\",\"summary\":\"Microsoft's Brian Beckman gives an hour-long, whiteboard-style walk through monads, building from function composition and monoids using school-level math. It is aimed at practicing programmers rather than category theorists.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as the clearest and most correct beginner explanation of monads, with several commenters saying it finally made them click.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"monads\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Logic & formal methods\"],\"tags\":[\"monads\",\"functional programming\",\"function composition\",\"monoids\",\"haskell\"],\"quote\":\"This video is all you, as a practicing programmer, need to know about monads and it's just 1 hour long.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":11.0},{\"vid\":\"akVL7QY0S8A\",\"title\":\"The Coming Collapse of the Middle Class with Elizabeth Warren\",\"channel\":\"University of California Television (UCTV)\",\"summary\":\"Elizabeth Warren's 2007 Berkeley lecture, when she was a Harvard law professor, on why middle-class families are under growing financial strain. She draws on bankruptcy research to examine rising costs of housing, health care and education.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it often in debates on inequality, cost disease and stagnant middle-class income, praising its data-driven approach.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"middle class finances\",\"categories\":[\"Labor, work & inequality\",\"University lectures & courses\",\"US politics & elections\"],\"tags\":[\"elizabeth warren\",\"bankruptcy\",\"two-income trap\",\"housing costs\",\"inequality\"],\"quote\":\"it's gripping stuff, because she develops insights based on research and hard-nosed science.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":11.0},{\"vid\":\"fU9hR3kiOK0\",\"title\":\"\\\"Turning the database inside out with Apache Samza\\\" by Martin Kleppmann\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Martin Kleppmann argues that databases are global shared mutable state and proposes treating them as an ever-growing log of immutable facts. Derived data such as caches, indexes and views is then built by stream processors like Apache Samza. The talk draws on Kafka and LinkedIn's experience with commit logs.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters repeatedly cite it as the talk that reframed how they think about caching, derived data and event sourcing.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"stream processing and event logs\",\"categories\":[\"Databases & storage\",\"Distributed systems\"],\"tags\":[\"apache samza\",\"kafka\",\"event sourcing\",\"immutable data\",\"commit log\",\"derived data\"],\"quote\":\"This talk on Apache Samza completely changed how I think about caching and derived data in general\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":11.0},{\"vid\":\"l9ZqXlHl65g\",\"title\":\"The Problem With Facebook\",\"channel\":\"2veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium's Derek Muller argues that Facebook's business model works against pages and creators, because it charges them to reach the audiences they built. He explains how filtering and paid promotion undermine the value of likes.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it, with its follow-up, as an exposé of fake likes and bot activity, and as a warning before spending on Facebook ads.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"facebook advertising and fake engagement\",\"categories\":[\"Tech industry & business models\",\"Product, marketing & sales\",\"Internet culture & memes\"],\"tags\":[\"facebook\",\"advertising\",\"click farms\",\"veritasium\",\"social media\"],\"quote\":\"before you waste any money, watch these two videos\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":11.0},{\"vid\":\"lJ8ydIuPFeU\",\"title\":\"\\\"How NOT to Measure Latency\\\" by Gil Tene\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Gil Tene of Azul Systems explains common pitfalls in measuring latency and response time, including why averages mislead and the problem of coordinated omission. He introduces open source tools like HdrHistogram for better measurement and reporting.\",\"why_hn\":\"The canonical recommendation whenever percentiles, tail latency, load testing or averages come up, described by some as life changing.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"latency measurement\",\"categories\":[\"Software performance & optimization\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"coordinated omission\",\"percentiles\",\"tail latency\",\"hdrhistogram\",\"performance testing\"],\"quote\":\"This a life changing video by Gil Tene on this topic.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":11.0},{\"vid\":\"mPqWstVnRjQ\",\"title\":\"Joe Rogan Experience #1035 - Paul Stamets\",\"channel\":\"PowerfulJRE\",\"summary\":\"A two-hour Joe Rogan Experience conversation with mycologist Paul Stamets about fungi, mycelium, bioremediation and medicinal mushrooms. It touches on Lion's Mane, psilocybin and the many uses of fungi.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters offer it as a fascinating, accessible introduction to the world of fungi whenever mushrooms or mycelium come up.\",\