"c++17\",\"commodore 64\",\"constexpr\",\"6502\",\"compiler optimization\",\"retro games\"],\"quote\":\"there is a wonderful talk by Jason Turner about writing a simple Pong clone for the Commodore 64 in modern C++\",\"recommenders\":13,\"score\":11.0},{\"vid\":\"36myc8wQhLo\",\"title\":\"USENIX ATC '21/OSDI '21 Joint Keynote Address-It's Time for Operating Systems to Rediscover Hardware\",\"channel\":\"USENIX\",\"summary\":\"Timothy Roscoe of ETH Zurich delivers the joint USENIX ATC/OSDI 2021 keynote arguing that operating systems must reckon with modern hardware, which is a heterogeneous collection of processors, firmware and coprocessors rather than a simple machine. It shows how much runs below and beside Linux.\",\"why_hn\":\"Repeatedly recommended as a revelatory talk on how little control the main OS has over modern SoCs and PCs.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"operating systems and hardware\",\"categories\":[\"Operating systems & kernels\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\"],\"tags\":[\"operating systems\",\"heterogeneous hardware\",\"firmware\",\"usenix\",\"systems research\"],\"quote\":\"There's this great USENIX talk by Timothy Roscoe [1]\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"3LopI4YeC4I\",\"title\":\"Why Being Delusional is a Superpower\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium examines the role of luck in success, with simulations and examples showing how small advantages and chance can compound. It argues that top outcomes are disproportionately luck-driven while still crediting effort.\",\"why_hn\":\"Linked in threads about meritocracy, careers and startups as a reminder that luck shapes who ends up on top.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"luck and success\",\"categories\":[\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Probability & game theory\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"luck\",\"meritocracy\",\"veritasium\",\"success\",\"simulation\"],\"quote\":\"if you attribute just 5% of outcomes to luck, then the people who have the best outcomes are among the luckiest people\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"5IUj1EZwpJY\",\"title\":\"The Only Unbreakable Law\",\"channel\":\"Molly Rocket\",\"summary\":\"Casey Muratori's lecture, originally given at the Technical University of Madrid, arguing that Conway's Law is the only viable candidate for a true law of software architecture. He explains how software mimics the organization over time, using examples like Windows' multiple volume controls.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the definitive explanation of Conway's Law and links it in almost any thread about org structure shaping software.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"conway's law\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Management, leadership & organizations\"],\"tags\":[\"conway's law\",\"software architecture\",\"casey muratori\",\"organizational design\",\"windows ui\"],\"quote\":\"Casey Muratori has an excellent lecture on this same topic\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"7rNY7xKyGCQ\",\"title\":\"Dr. Michael Greger | How Not To Die | Talks at Google\",\"channel\":\"Talks at Google\",\"summary\":\"Dr. Michael Greger presents his book How Not to Die at Google NYC, arguing that diet and lifestyle changes can prevent or reverse many of the top causes of death. He walks through evidence on whole-food plant-based eating.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently recommended in threads on nutrition, cancer and veganism as a science-focused case for plant-based diets, with at least one commenter saying it made them go vegan.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"plant-based nutrition\",\"categories\":[\"Nutrition & diet science\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"michael greger\",\"how not to die\",\"whole food plant-based\",\"preventive medicine\",\"diet and disease\"],\"quote\":\"If you're interested in the science, here's a long (but good) talk by Dr. Michael Greger\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"8SkdfdXWYaI\",\"title\":\"Using Python to Code by Voice\",\"channel\":\"Next Day Video\",\"summary\":\"Tavis Rudd describes how RSI forced him to program by voice, and shows the Python and Emacs Lisp system he built with voice recognition. A live demo creates and deploys a small system without touching the keyboard.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recurs in threads about RSI, accessibility and voice coding as the go-to demo of a custom spoken command language.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"voice programming\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"voice coding\",\"rsi\",\"emacs\",\"dragon naturallyspeaking\",\"accessibility\",\"python\"],\"quote\":\"Travis Rudd's incredible demo of voice coding [1]\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"8X69_42Mj-g\",\"title\":\"Clasp: Common Lisp using LLVM and C++ for Molecular Metaprogramming\",\"channel\":\"Google TechTalks\",\"summary\":\"Christian Schafmeister's Google Tech Talk on designing spiroligomer molecules and building Clasp, a Common Lisp on LLVM that interoperates with C++, to program molecular design. It covers the chemistry, automatic differentiation for force fields, and how the compiler is built.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly cites it as a striking example of Common Lisp used to push the frontier of science, and as one of the most inspiring tech talks.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"common lisp for molecular design\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"Chemistry\"],\"tags\":[\"clasp\",\"llvm\",\"common lisp\",\"nanotechnology\",\"metaprogramming\",\"chemistry\"],\"quote\":\"I challenge anyone to watch this lecture from Christian Schafmeister and say they are not impressed\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"8hAw1z8GdE8\",\"title\":\"Personal Growth Series: Dr. William Dement on Healthy Sleep and Optimal Perfo...\",\"channel\":\"Google TechTalks\",\"summary\":\"A Google Tech Talk by sleep researcher Dr. William Dement on healthy sleep and performance. He argues sleep debt must be repaid and describes the three characteristics of healthy sleep.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN has recommended it for years as the most useful and entertaining talk on sleep, often to people struggling with tiredness or productivity.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"sleep science\",\"categories\":[\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\",\"Neuroscience & the brain\"],\"tags\":[\"sleep debt\",\"sleep\",\"productivity\",\"william dement\",\"health\"],\"quote\":\"The source of information about this that I have found most useful is an hour long talk given by Dr. William Dement\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"IlU-zDU6aQ0\",\"title\":\"Marty Lobdell - Study Less Study Smart\",\"channel\":\"Pierce College District WA\",\"summary\":\"Psychology professor Marty Lobdell gives a one-hour lecture on how to study effectively, covering short focused sessions with breaks, study environment, note-taking and memory techniques. It was aimed at students who cram without improving.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it for productivity, focus and working-from-home threads, since its advice on breaks and mental modes applies beyond studying.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"study skills\",\"categories\":[\"Education & learning\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"studying\",\"productivity\",\"memory\",\"breaks\",\"focus\"],\"quote\":\"Marty Lobdell did a fantastic talk a while back [1] that changed the way I used to study\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"LKtk3HCgTa8\",\"title\":\"\\\"Simple Made Easy\\\" - Rich Hickey (2011)\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"Rich Hickey's Strange Loop 2011 keynote separates 'simple' (not intertwined) from 'easy' (familiar or near at hand) and argues that software complexity comes from complecting things. It offers a vocabulary for judging designs and abstractions.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is one of HN's most frequently recommended programming talks, cited whenever people debate complexity, simplicity and abstraction, and valued well beyond Clojure.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software complexity and design\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"rich hickey\",\"simplicity\",\"complexity\",\"clojure\",\"software design\",\"strange loop\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend Rich Hickey's \\\"Simple Made Easy\\\" talk as a way to think about software complexity.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"NVGuFdX5guE\",\"title\":\"You Need a Theme, Not a Goal\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey argues for picking a theme for the year or season instead of setting goals or new year's resolutions. He explains how a theme gives direction without a pass/fail target.\",\"why_hn\":\"A staple recommendation in new year and goal-setting threads, with many commenters adopting yearly themes.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"yearly themes versus goals\",\"categories\":[\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"new year's resolutions\",\"self-improvement\",\"habits\",\"productivity\",\"themes\"],\"quote\":\"CGP Grey has an excellent video on this exact kind of goal setting\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"OSGv2VnC0go\",\"title\":\"Transforming Code into Beautiful, Idiomatic Python\",\"channel\":\"Next Day Video\",\"summary\":\"Raymond Hettinger's PyCon 2013 talk on transforming code into idiomatic Python through a series of before-and-after refactorings. It shows how to use iterators, dictionaries, named tuples and other language features well.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as a must-watch classic for writing Pythonic code, though somewhat dated for modern Python.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"idiomatic python\",\"categories\":[\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"python\",\"raymond hettinger\",\"pycon\",\"refactoring\",\"best practices\"],\"quote\":\"the excellent talk \\\"Transforming Code into Beautiful, Idiomatic Python\\\".\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"SGJ5cZnoodY\",\"title\":\"Shenzhen: The Silicon Valley of Hardware (Full Documentary) | Future Cities | WIRED\",\"channel\":\"WIRED Investigations\",\"summary\":\"A WIRED Future Cities documentary on Shenzhen, covering its hardware-prototyping culture and shanzhai copycat manufacturing. It looks at how the city's ecosystem evolved and what the rest of the world can learn.\",\"why_hn\":\"Strongly recommended in Shenzhen and hardware threads for its view of fast cycles, loose IP and the maker ecosystem.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"shenzhen hardware ecosystem\",\"categories\":[\"Manufacturing & industrial processes\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\",\"Startups & entrepreneurship\"],\"tags\":[\"shenzhen\",\"shanzhai\",\"hardware startups\",\"manufacturing\",\"china\"],\"quote\":\"There's an excellent documentary on the hardware/hacking ecosystem in Shenzhen on YouTube\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"TH9VCN6UkyQ\",\"title\":\"Ideas about a new programming language for games.\",\"channel\":\"Jonathan Blow\",\"summary\":\"Jonathan Blow's first talk on designing a new programming language for games, which became Jai. He explains the problems with C++ and why Go, Rust and D do not fit game development.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN programmers recommend it, and the Jai series that followed, for the game-industry view on replacing C++ and for watching a language being invented.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming language design for games\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Game programming & engines\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"jonathan blow\",\"jai\",\"c++\",\"game development\",\"language design\"],\"quote\":\"I also found watching his process for inventing the language extremely instructive, just as a programmer.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"WUvTyaaNkzM\",\"title\":\"The essence of calculus\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"The first video of 3Blue1Brown's Essence of Calculus series, which builds toward integrals, derivatives, and the fundamental theorem of calculus from a simple geometry question. It aims to make viewers feel they could have invented calculus themselves.\",\"why_hn\":\"A staple recommendation for anyone learning math, praised for visual intuition that makes memorized formulas make sense.\",\"content_type\":\"course\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"calculus intuition\",\"categories\":[\"Pure mathematics\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"calculus\",\"derivatives\",\"integrals\",\"visual math\",\"3blue1brown\"],\"quote\":\"When I discovered that video, all the formulas they tried to make me memorize started making a LOT more sense.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"XA2WjJbmmoM\",\"title\":\"How to Do 90% of What Plugins Do (With Just Vim)\",\"channel\":\"thoughtbot\",\"summary\":\"A thoughtbot meetup talk by Max Cantor on getting most of what popular plugins provide using only built-in Vim features. It covers autocomplete, file jumping, filesystem navigation, build integration and snippets, with slides and a vimrc available.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the canonical talk for the case that vanilla Vim goes a long way and plugin dependencies are a burden.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"vim without plugins\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"vim\",\"vimrc\",\"editor workflow\",\"plugins\",\"minimalism\"],\"quote\":\"Max Cantor has a great talk about that on YouTube\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"bglWCuCMSWc\",\"title\":\"The Wrong Way to Set Speed Limits [ST06]\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes explains how North American speed limits are set and why posted limits matter far less than street design. It argues that drivers follow the road's cues, so safe speeds have to be built into the street.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended again and again in traffic-safety threads as an eye-opener on road design versus posted limits and driver behavior.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"road design and speed limits\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\"],\"tags\":[\"speed limits\",\"traffic engineering\",\"urban planning\",\"road safety\",\"netherlands\"],\"quote\":\"it really opened my eyes on the relationship between rules and actual behavior.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"h7apO7q16V0\",\"title\":\"The Fast Fourier Transform (FFT): Most Ingenious Algorithm Ever?\",\"channel\":\"Reducible\",\"summary\":\"Reducible's visual walkthrough of the Fast Fourier Transform, motivated by polynomial multiplication. It shows how the FFT's key ideas can be discovered by asking the right questions.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly calls it a fantastic explanation that made FFT click, with some rewatching it periodically.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"fast fourier transform\",\"categories\":[\"Algorithms & data structures\",\"Information theory & signal processing\"],\"tags\":[\"fft\",\"polynomial multiplication\",\"algorithms\",\"convolution\",\"signal processing\"],\"quote\":\"It derives the FFT algorithm from polynomial multiplication and it’s fantastic . I rewatch it every 6 months or so.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"iYJKd0rkKss\",\"title\":\"Dick Proenneke in Alone in the Wilderness\",\"channel\":\"swererbob\",\"summary\":\"A condensed film of Dick Proenneke's footage of building a cabin and living alone in the Alaskan wilderness, using hand tools and local materials. It draws on the 8mm films he shot himself.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it often and warmly, as an inspiring and peaceful view of self-reliance and handcraft, in threads on cabins, solitude and woodworking.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"wilderness cabin building\",\"categories\":[\"Documentaries & storytelling\",\"Woodworking, metalworking & workshop skills\",\"Animals & nature\"],\"tags\":[\"dick proenneke\",\"alaska\",\"cabin building\",\"self-reliance\",\"hand tools\",\"woodworking\"],\"quote\":\"I think HN would really appreciate the story of Dick Proenneke\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"jVkLVRt6c1U\",\"title\":\"Mike Monteiro: F*ck You, Pay Me\",\"channel\":\"CreativeMornings HQ\",\"summary\":\"Mike Monteiro's CreativeMornings talk on getting paid for creative and freelance work, covering contracts, working with lawyers, and refusing to work for free. It is delivered with blunt humor.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN regulars send freelancers and consultants to it whenever clients don't pay or contracts come up.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"freelancing and getting paid\",\"categories\":[\"Startups & entrepreneurship\",\"UX & UI design\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"freelancing\",\"contracts\",\"client payment\",\"negotiation\",\"design business\"],\"quote\":\"I watched this video from Mike Monteiro and I always refer to it since.\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"mw2z9lV3W1g\",\"title\":\"We the People, and the Republic we must reclaim | Lawrence Lessig\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"In this TED talk, Lawrence Lessig argues that American politics is corrupted by Congress's dependence on funding from a tiny fraction of citizens. He uses rapid-fire visuals and calls for a bipartisan push to reclaim the Republic.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it repeatedly as the root-cause argument that money in politics must be fixed before other issues, and as background for his Mayday PAC.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"money in politics\",\"categories\":[\"US politics & elections\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\"],\"tags\":[\"lawrence lessig\",\"campaign finance\",\"political corruption\",\"lobbying\",\"democracy reform\"],\"quote\":\"This TED lecture[0] is only 18 minutes long and it offers rather a chilling perspective on any sensible reform.\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"oyLBGkS5ICk\",\"title\":\"Spec-ulation Keynote - Rich Hickey\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"Rich Hickey's keynote on software versioning, arguing that breaking changes are the real problem and that libraries should only grow by providing more and requiring less. It critiques semantic versioning and ties into Clojure spec.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it in every versioning and API stability debate as a challenging, contrarian talk worth watching.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software versioning\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"semantic versioning\",\"api design\",\"backward compatibility\",\"clojure\",\"rich hickey\"],\"quote\":\"Whenever an idea about API versioning springs into mind it is always worthwhile to watch Rich Hickey's Spec-ulation talk\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"p33CVV29OG8\",\"title\":\"Modern Dictionaries by Raymond Hettinger\",\"channel\":\"SF Python\",\"summary\":\"Python core developer Raymond Hettinger explains how Python's dictionary implementation evolved into the compact, ordered dict in Python 3.6. He uses pictures and small bits of pure Python, and covers key-sharing, compaction and versioning.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the definitive, engaging talk on hash table internals and Python dict ordering, and recommends it in tech-talk lists.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"python dictionary internals\",\"categories\":[\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Algorithms & data structures\"],\"tags\":[\"python\",\"hash tables\",\"dict\",\"cpython\",\"data structures\"],\"quote\":\"one of the most insightful videos I've seen in a long time.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"pgoetgxecw8\",\"title\":\"Refterm Lecture Part 1 - Philosophies of Optimization\",\"channel\":\"Molly Rocket\",\"summary\":\"Casey Muratori's first refterm lecture lays out philosophies of optimization, distinguishing optimization from non-pessimization, which means avoiding needless work. It uses his fast terminal renderer project to show how fast computers really are.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it constantly in software performance debates as the primer on non-pessimization and why modern software is slow.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software performance\",\"categories\":[\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"non-pessimization\",\"optimization\",\"casey muratori\",\"refterm\",\"terminal emulators\"],\"quote\":\"The video on Philosophies of Optimisation is a good primer on the tenets of the course.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"qJEWOTZnFeg\",\"title\":\"\\\"Money doesn't interest me\\\" - Ton Roosendaal 2017 Interview\",\"channel\":\"Blender Guru\",\"summary\":\"A long interview by Blender Guru with Ton Roosendaal, creator of Blender. They cover Blender's origins, the move to open source, why Ton is not driven by money, Autodesk, and what sets Blender apart from other open source projects.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the best account of Blender's history and its user-focused approach as a free software success story.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"blender history\",\"categories\":[\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Animation & visual effects\",\"Hacker culture & free software\"],\"tags\":[\"blender\",\"open source\",\"ton roosendaal\",\"3d software\",\"software history\"],\"quote\":\"I highly encourage to watch the whole interview\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"rX0ItVEVjHc\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2014: Mike Acton \\\"Data-Oriented Design and C++\\\"\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Mike Acton of Insomniac Games argues that a program's purpose is transforming data, and shows how common C++ approaches work against that in performance-critical game development. He covers cache behavior, data layout and the limits of compilers.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the go-to talk HN points to for data-oriented design and hardware-aware performance thinking, despite Acton's blunt style.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"data-oriented design\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Software performance & optimization\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"data-oriented design\",\"c++\",\"cache performance\",\"game engines\",\"struct of arrays\"],\"quote\":\"one of the most digestible ways to start thinking about performance in high throughput systems\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"s7tWHJfhiyo\",\"title\":\"Why Your Vote Doesn't Count\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey explains first-past-the-post voting and why it pushes elections toward two dominant parties. It is the first in his series on electoral systems.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN's go-to explainer for why FPTP leads to a two-party system, linked in countless voting-reform and US politics threads.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"voting systems\",\"categories\":[\"US politics & elections\",\"Probability & game theory\"],\"tags\":[\"first past the post\",\"two-party system\",\"electoral reform\",\"democracy\",\"cgp grey\"],\"quote\":\"this CGP Grey video explains how a first-past-the-post voting system all-but-guarantees a two party system to emerge over time\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"tX4H_ctggYo\",\"title\":\"Siggraph2019 Geometric Algebra\",\"channel\":\"Bivector\",\"summary\":\"A SIGGRAPH 2019 course on geometric algebra. Charles Gunn gives an intro, then Steven De Keninck gives a programmer-focused course with live examples in ganja.js.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters repeatedly point people to it as the most accessible, developer-friendly introduction to geometric algebra and its use in graphics and physics.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"geometric algebra\",\"categories\":[\"Computer graphics & rendering\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"geometric algebra\",\"siggraph\",\"computer graphics\",\"rotors\",\"ganja.js\",\"quaternions\"],\"quote\":\"just watch this mind blowing video\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"yJDv-zdhzMY\",\"title\":\"The Mother of All Demos, presented by Douglas Engelbart (1968)\",\"channel\":\"Marcel\",\"summary\":\"The full recording of Douglas Engelbart's 1968 demonstration, which introduced the mouse, hypertext, video conferencing, word processing, and real-time collaborative editing. It runs about 100 minutes.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as an essential piece of computing history that shows how many modern interface ideas were already solved in 1968.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of computing\",\"categories\":[\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"History of computing\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"douglas engelbart\",\"mother of all demos\",\"hypertext\",\"mouse\",\"collaborative editing\",\"human-computer interaction\"],\"quote\":\"If you haven't seen it, it's well worth the hour and forty minutes.\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"zDZFcDGpL4U\",\"title\":\"RSA ANIMATE: Changing Education Paradigms\",\"channel\":\"Royal Society of Arts\",\"summary\":\"An RSA Animate drawn from a Sir Ken Robinson talk arguing that current education systems are modeled on the industrial age. It discusses divergent thinking, standardization, and how schools disengage kids.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an accessible, widely shared critique of schooling, and cites it in threads on ADHD, creativity and education reform.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"education reform\",\"categories\":[\"Education & learning\"],\"tags\":[\"ken robinson\",\"education\",\"creativity\",\"divergent thinking\",\"rsa animate\"],\"quote\":\"One of my favorite videos on reforming education\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":10.5},{\"vid\":\"2pWv7GOvuf0\",\"title\":\"RL Course by David Silver - Lecture 1: Introduction to Reinforcement Learning\",\"channel\":\"Google DeepMind\",\"summary\":\"The first lecture of David Silver's reinforcement learning course, introducing the field and its basic concepts. The course follows the Sutton and Barto textbook.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently recommended as an excellent, clear introductory RL course from the lead of AlphaGo, pairing well with Sutton and Barto.\",\"content_type\":\"course\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"reinforcement learning\",\"categories\":[\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"reinforcement learning\",\"david silver\",\"deepmind\",\"sutton and barto\",\"machine learning\"],\"quote\":\"Really clear explanations that went hand in hand when I was reading Sutton and Barto's introductory book.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":10.0},{\"vid\":\"2sjqTHE0zok\",\"title\":\"Lecture 6: Version Control (git) (2020)\",\"channel\":\"Missing Semester\",\"summary\":\"A lecture from MIT's Missing Semester course covering version control with git. It builds up git from its data model rather than from commands.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN frequently calls it the best single video on git because it teaches the underlying data structures.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"git version control\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"git\",\"data model\",\"missing semester\",\"mit\",\"command line\"],\"quote\":\"I think that this video should be considered mandatory viewing if you're a developer using Git\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":10.0},{\"vid\":\"3kEfedtQVOY\",\"title\":\"28c3: The Science of Insecurity\",\"channel\":\"28c3\",\"summary\":\"Meredith Patterson and Sergey Bratus argue that networked software stays insecure because protocols and inputs are too complex to recognize safely. They propose using formal language theory and grammars to build secure, verifiable input handling.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it one of the best security talks and the standard introduction to LANGSEC, which they use to argue for simpler, well-specified input formats.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"language-theoretic security\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Computability & complexity theory\",\"Logic & formal methods\"],\"tags\":[\"langsec\",\"parsers\",\"formal grammars\",\"input validation\",\"protocol security\"],\"quote\":\"The best talk on computer security I have seen is Meredith Patterson's Science of Insecurity at 28c3\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":10.0},{\"vid\":\"3nwwKbM_vJc\",\"title\":\"Mindfulness with Jon Kabat-Zinn\",\"channel\":\"Google\",\"summary\":\"Jon Kabat-Zinn leads an hour-long Google tech talk on mindfulness, introducing the basic ideas and including a guided meditation. It is aimed at newcomers.\",\"why_hn\":\"For over a decade HN has pointed people asking about stress, focus or meditation to this as a restful and approachable introduction to mindfulness.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"mindfulness meditation\",\"categories\":[\"Mental health & neurodiversity\",\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"mindfulness\",\"meditation\",\"jon kabat-zinn\",\"stress reduction\",\"google tech talk\"],\"quote\":\"Its almost embarrassing how much that video changed my perception of my decisionmaking.\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":10.0},{\"vid\":\"42QuXLucH3Q\",\"title\":\"Is Most Published Research Wrong?\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explains why a large share of published research findings may be false, covering p-values, p-hacking, publication bias and low replication rates. It draws on John Ioannidis's well-known paper and describes reforms such as preregistration.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters link it whenever a shaky study or the p<0.05 threshold comes up, as a short primer on false positives in science.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"scientific reproducibility\",\"categories\":[\"Scientific method & science communication\",\"Data science & statistics\"],\"tags\":[\"p-values\",\"p-hacking\",\"replication crisis\",\"statistics\",\"publication bias\"],\"quote\":\"This video is a good primer\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":10.0}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"443UNeGrFoM\",\"title\":\"How I program C\",\"channel\":\"Eskil Steenberg\",\"summary\":\"Eskil Steenberg's two-hour talk on his personal approach to writing C, covering code style, structure, naming, pointers and large-project organization. He warns it contains a fair bit of programming religion.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the one long programming video worth watching, a provocative and influential take on C.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"c programming style\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"c\",\"eskil steenberg\",\"code structure\",\"naming\",\"pointers\",\"software philosophy\"],\"quote\":\"the only 2 hour YouTube video I'll ever recommend\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":10.0},{\"vid\":\"5Ge2RcvDlgw\",\"title\":\"The Extreme Engineering of ASML’s EUV Light Source\",\"channel\":\"Asianometry\",\"summary\":\"Asianometry explains the EUV light source inside ASML's lithography machines, where lasers hit falling tin droplets twice to produce 13.5 nm light. It walks through the engineering of the droplet generator, the pre-pulse and main laser, and the plasma that results.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters repeatedly point to it whenever EUV, ASML or chip manufacturing comes up, as a clear and astonishing explanation of one of the hardest engineering problems in chipmaking.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"EUV lithography\",\"categories\":[\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\"],\"tags\":[\"asml\",\"euv\",\"tin droplets\",\"semiconductor manufacturing\",\"lasers\",\"plasma\"],\"quote\":\"This video from Asianometry on EUV light sources is well worth the watch. It's pretty incredible.\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":10.0},{\"vid\":\"BzhbAK1pdPM\",\"title\":\"30 Essential Ideas you should know about ADHD, 1A Intro, Chronic Developmental Disability\",\"channel\":\"Adhd Videos\",\"summary\":\"