 Derbycon 2015 talk on the M/o/Vfuscator, a compiler that emits only x86 mov instructions, showing mov is Turing complete. It explains how a full machine is built from mov and why the output is a nightmare to reverse engineer.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN calls it a legendary, clearly explained hacker talk whenever mov-only computing or anti-decompilation comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"mov-only computation\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"Computability & complexity theory\"],\"tags\":[\"movfuscator\",\"x86\",\"reverse engineering\",\"turing completeness\",\"obfuscation\"],\"quote\":\"Christopher Domas's conference talk on the movfuscator is legendary\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"T647CGsuOVU\",\"title\":\"Imaginary Numbers Are Real [Part 1: Introduction]\",\"channel\":\"Welch Labs\",\"summary\":\"The first part of Welch Labs' series on imaginary numbers, presenting them as the natural extension of the number line into two dimensions. It covers the history and the math with strong visuals.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters rate the series as some of the best visual teaching on complex numbers and recommend it for building intuition.\",\"content_type\":\"course\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"complex numbers\",\"categories\":[\"Pure mathematics\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"imaginary numbers\",\"mathematics\",\"visual math\",\"math history\",\"welch labs\"],\"quote\":\"The greatest audiovisual content on the topic I have ever seen\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"Tb823aqgX_0\",\"title\":\"Bottom Up vs  Top Down Design in Clojure - Mark Bastian\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"Mark Bastian contrasts top-down object-oriented design with bottom-up, data-oriented design in Clojure. He models a board game as plain data plus small composable functions and compares it with a roughly 22-class OOP version.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as the talk that made data-oriented, REPL-driven bottom-up design click.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"bottom-up software design\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"clojure\",\"data-oriented design\",\"oop vs functional\",\"repl\",\"board games\"],\"quote\":\"is the talk that really made it clear to me how to design programs this way.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"UCgoxQCf5Jg\",\"title\":\"TempleOS | Down the Rabbit Hole\",\"channel\":\"Fredrik Knudsen\",\"summary\":\"Fredrik Knudsen's 86-minute documentary on Terry A. Davis, creator of TempleOS, tracing his work, his schizophrenia and his decline. It is a chronological video essay built on his public posts and videos.\",\"why_hn\":\"Regarded as the definitive, if heartbreaking, account of Terry Davis and TempleOS.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"terry davis and templeos\",\"categories\":[\"Documentaries & storytelling\",\"Mental health & neurodiversity\",\"Operating systems & kernels\"],\"tags\":[\"templeos\",\"terry davis\",\"schizophrenia\",\"operating systems\",\"video essay\"],\"quote\":\"This video is the definitive rundown on his life and illness\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"Wpv-Qb-dB6g\",\"title\":\"Plover: Thought to Text at 240 WPM\",\"channel\":\"Next Day Video\",\"summary\":\"Mirabai Knight's PyCon 2013 talk on Plover, a free cross-platform stenography engine that lets people write at up to 240 WPM on ordinary hardware, including a demo of coding by steno.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN's standard reference when typing speed, ergonomics, or alternative input comes up, for its live demo and the open-source steno ecosystem.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"stenography text input\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"plover\",\"stenography\",\"typing speed\",\"open source\",\"live coding\"],\"quote\":\"Mirabai Knight's talk about this is one of my all time faves\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"XZ3w_jec1v8\",\"title\":\"\\\"The Economics of Programming Languages\\\" by Evan Czaplicki (Strange Loop 2023)\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Evan Czaplicki, creator of Elm, examines how programming languages are funded. He looks at where compiler engineers' salaries come from, why Go has about 5 engineers while Dart has about 30, and how this limits language design.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended repeatedly as a funny, informative talk on the economics behind programming languages and open source.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming language economics\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"open source funding\",\"elm\",\"language adoption\",\"strange loop\",\"evan czaplicki\"],\"quote\":\"Very highly recommend this funny, informative talk to understand aspects of creating a programming language other than the theoretical ones.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"Z7Wl2FW2TcA\",\"title\":\"BlackHat USA 2011: SSL And The Future Of Authenticity\",\"channel\":\"Christiaan008\",\"summary\":\"Moxie Marlinspike's BlackHat 2011 talk examines weaknesses of SSL and the certificate authority system and proposes Convergence, a decentralized notary approach with trust agility.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters repeatedly recommend it as the best explanation of why the CA system is broken and what alternatives exist.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"ssl certificate authorities\",\"categories\":[\"Cryptography\",\"Security & hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"moxie marlinspike\",\"convergence\",\"certificate authorities\",\"mitm\",\"tls\",\"trust agility\"],\"quote\":\"best talk I know of this subject\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"bpAi70WWBlw\",\"title\":\"Seattle is Dying | A KOMO News Documentary\",\"channel\":\"KOMO News\",\"summary\":\"A one-hour 2019 KOMO News documentary hosted by Eric Johnson on Seattle's homelessness and drug crisis, with interviews and looks at proposed solutions including a drug court program in Rhode Island. It argues addiction and mental illness are at the core of the problem.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it in homelessness threads about San Francisco and Seattle as an eye-opening, widely viewed argument for treatment-first policy, though some see it as pro-law-enforcement framing.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"seattle homelessness\",\"categories\":[\"Social issues & culture\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Law, policing & justice\"],\"tags\":[\"homelessness\",\"addiction\",\"seattle\",\"drug policy\",\"local news\"],\"quote\":\"Seattle Is Dying” is a great investigative report about the problems and potential solutions to homelessness.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"dK3zRXhrFZY\",\"title\":\"Julia: to Lisp or not to Lisp?\",\"channel\":\"European Lisp Symposium\",\"summary\":\"Stefan Karpinski's European Lisp Symposium 2016 talk asks whether Julia is a Lisp. He covers the features it borrows from Lisp, such as macros and closures, and where it departs, including syntax and its type system, with generated machine code shown.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a brilliant talk on Julia's design and how it is or isn't a Lisp.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"julia language design\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"julia\",\"lisp\",\"macros\",\"type system\",\"language design\"],\"quote\":\"the recent brilliant talk about design of Julia\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"gSPNRu4ZPvE\",\"title\":\"Life in Syntropy\",\"channel\":\"Agenda Gotsch\",\"summary\":\"A short film from Agenda Gotsch introducing syntropic agriculture and Ernst Gotsch's agroforestry methods, made for COP21. It shows degraded land regenerated into productive forest farms.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN users repeatedly offer it as the best introduction to agroforestry as a climate and food solution.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"syntropic agriculture\",\"categories\":[\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\",\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\"],\"tags\":[\"agroforestry\",\"ernst gotsch\",\"regenerative agriculture\",\"soil building\",\"carbon storage\"],\"quote\":\"Ever since watching the video on syntropic agriculture which I bumped into by reading a comment here in HN\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"h6fcK_fRYaI\",\"title\":\"The Egg - A Short Story\",\"channel\":\"Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell\",\"summary\":\"Kurzgesagt animates Andy Weir's short story The Egg, in which a person learns that they are every human who has ever lived. It adds music and narration to the original text.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it a beloved story and link the animation when threads touch on consciousness, empathy or identity.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"philosophical short story\",\"categories\":[\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Literature & writing craft\",\"Consciousness & cognition\"],\"tags\":[\"the egg\",\"andy weir\",\"reincarnation\",\"consciousness\",\"animation\"],\"quote\":\"The Egg is one of my life long favorite histories.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"lKXe3HUG2l4\",\"title\":\"\\\"The Mess We're In\\\" by Joe Armstrong\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Joe Armstrong, co-inventor of Erlang, gives a funny and opinionated Strange Loop talk on the tangled state of software: build tools, package managers, entropy and the physical limits of computation. He argues for ideas like naming things by content hash and throwing away your first version.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it constantly as a witty, still-accurate diagnosis of why software is bloated and messy, and as a go-to recommendation in any thread on modules, package managers or software quality.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"software complexity\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Functional programming\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"erlang\",\"joe armstrong\",\"entropy\",\"package managers\",\"content addressing\",\"strange loop\"],\"quote\":\"one of those talks which were incredibly funny and informative at the same time\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"mScpHTIi-kM\",\"title\":\"Once you understand game theory, you understand the world.\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explores the Prisoner's Dilemma and Axelrod's tournaments, showing why tit-for-tat and forgiving strategies win in repeated games. It extends the ideas to cooperation in evolution and conflict.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters reach for it constantly as an approachable explanation of why cooperation and reciprocity are rational strategies.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"game theory\",\"categories\":[\"Probability & game theory\",\"Evolution & genetics\"],\"tags\":[\"prisoner's dilemma\",\"tit for tat\",\"cooperation\",\"axelrod\",\"evolutionary game theory\"],\"quote\":\"Veratasium just made an approachable video on this subject recently, I definitely recommend.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"msVuCEs8Ydo\",\"title\":\"The Speed of Light is NOT About Light\",\"channel\":\"PBS Space Time\",\"summary\":\"PBS Space Time argues that the speed of light is better understood as the speed of causality, the universal limit on how fast cause and effect propagate. Light travels at that speed because it is massless, but the limit itself is a property of spacetime.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters send people to it whenever a thread confuses the speed of light with a light-specific limit, and several say it deepened their understanding of relativity.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"special relativity\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"speed of light\",\"causality\",\"relativity\",\"physics\",\"spacetime\"],\"quote\":\"The video about “the speed of causality” is the one that caused me to level up\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"nm1TxQj9IsQ\",\"title\":\"Master Your Sleep & Be More Alert When Awake\",\"channel\":\"Andrew Huberman\",\"summary\":\"Andrew Huberman's podcast episode on what makes us sleepy, helps us sleep soundly and keeps us alert. It lays out the science and a set of behavioral tools such as light timing.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN users repeatedly credit it with fixing their sleep, especially its advice on morning light and dimming lights in the evening.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"sleep and circadian rhythm\",\"categories\":[\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\",\"Neuroscience & the brain\"],\"tags\":[\"sleep\",\"huberman\",\"circadian rhythm\",\"light exposure\",\"neuroscience\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend it. The key is light.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"oIG9ztQw2Gc\",\"title\":\"Jim Keller: Moore’s Law is Not Dead\",\"channel\":\"UC Berkeley EECS\",\"summary\":\"Jim Keller gives a UC Berkeley EECS colloquium arguing that Moore's Law is not dead. He surveys upcoming technologies and the multiple innovation curves that can keep transistor density and performance scaling.\",\"why_hn\":\"The go-to citation in Moore's Law threads, a convincing counterargument from a top CPU designer.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"moore's law\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"jim keller\",\"semiconductors\",\"cpu architecture\",\"transistor scaling\",\"chip design\"],\"quote\":\"It is worth watching Jim Keller's lecture at UC Berkeley literally titled \\\"Moore's law is not dead\\\".\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"pW-SOdj4Kkk\",\"title\":\"Jonathan Blow - Preventing the Collapse of Civilization (English only)\",\"channel\":\"bus\",\"summary\":\"Jonathan Blow's DevGAMM 2019 talk argues that software knowledge is being lost between generations and that growing layers of abstraction could lead to a decline in technological civilization.\",\"why_hn\":\"A perennial HN reference for arguments that software is getting worse and that hard-won knowledge is disappearing.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"software decline and lost knowledge\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Software performance & optimization\",\"Futurism & technology and society\"],\"tags\":[\"jonathan blow\",\"abstraction\",\"software quality\",\"game development\",\"knowledge loss\"],\"quote\":\"Very much worth watching in its entirety and thinking about\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"r3snVCRo_bI\",\"title\":\"VidMe or Why Platforms Aren't Your Friends\",\"channel\":\"Folding Ideas\",\"summary\":\"Folding Ideas uses the rise and fall of the YouTube competitor VidMe to examine why platforms are not friends to creators. It argues that alternatives to big platforms tend to attract the users banned from the original and lack a core vision.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it whenever a thread asks why clones of or alternatives to YouTube, Reddit or Twitter fail or turn toxic.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"platform alternatives\",\"categories\":[\"Internet culture & memes\",\"Tech industry & business models\",\"Product, marketing & sales\"],\"tags\":[\"vidme\",\"youtube\",\"creator economy\",\"network effects\",\"social platforms\"],\"quote\":\"Folding Ideas (on YouTube) had a solid video about VidMe, which is a case study in how NOT to do this\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"rOwMW6agpTI\",\"title\":\"27C3: Adventures in analyzing Stuxnet (Bruce Dang from Microsoft)\",\"channel\":\"basti0007\",\"summary\":\"Bruce Dang of Microsoft gives a 27C3 talk on how the Stuxnet vulnerabilities were found and analyzed. He walks through the techniques used to triage them and the details of each zero-day exploit, down to the opcode level.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the definitive technical deep dive into Stuxnet's zero-days from someone who analyzed it first-hand.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"stuxnet analysis\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"stuxnet\",\"zero-day\",\"malware analysis\",\"reverse engineering\",\"windows vulnerabilities\"],\"quote\":\"For an amazing technical deep dive into each 0-day Stuxnet vulnerability watch this talk with Bruce Dang from Microsoft[1].\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"roBkg-iPrbw\",\"title\":\"Trackers: The Sound of 16-Bit\",\"channel\":\"Ahoy\",\"summary\":\"Ahoy's documentary traces tracker music from early Amiga software like SoundMonitor and Soundtracker through the demoscene, Protracker, and PC trackers such as Screamtracker and FastTracker. It covers the sound, culture and history of 16-bit module music.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the go-to chronological, in-depth overview of tracker music, recommended whenever trackers, the Amiga or the demoscene come up.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"tracker music history\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\"],\"tags\":[\"trackers\",\"amiga\",\"demoscene\",\"chiptunes\",\"soundtracker\",\"protracker\"],\"quote\":\"is an incredible, in-depth overview of trackers, their sound and history\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"sGG-A80Tl5g\",\"title\":\"Eating less Meat won't save the Planet. Here's Why\",\"channel\":\"Joseph Everett - WIL\",\"summary\":\"Joseph Everett, with Dr. Frank Mitloehner, argues that eating less meat will not do much for the climate. He covers beef's water footprint, land and feed use, and methane.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a counterpoint to claims about beef's environmental cost, though some critique its methane argument.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"livestock and climate\",\"categories\":[\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\",\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\",\"Nutrition & diet science\"],\"tags\":[\"beef\",\"greenhouse gas emissions\",\"water use\",\"methane\",\"diet and climate\"],\"quote\":\"I was much more concerned about my beef consumption before watching this WIL video. It put things in perspective for me.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"tIuj-oMN-Fk\",\"title\":\"Dr. Jason Fung - 'Therapeutic Fasting - Solving the Two-Compartment Problem'\",\"channel\":\"Low Carb Down Under\",\"summary\":\"Dr. Jason Fung, a nephrologist, explains therapeutic fasting as a treatment for obesity and type 2 diabetes. He frames it around the two-compartment problem of fat storage and insulin resistance.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters repeatedly recommend it as a free, evidence-citing overview of fasting that counters worries about muscle loss and safety.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"therapeutic fasting\",\"categories\":[\"Nutrition & diet science\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"fasting\",\"insulin resistance\",\"type 2 diabetes\",\"obesity\",\"jason fung\"],\"quote\":\"I always recommend people watch this by Dr. Jason Fung\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"vsMydMDi3rI\",\"title\":\"Frank Abagnale | Catch Me If You Can | Talks at Google\",\"channel\":\"Talks at Google\",\"summary\":\"Frank Abagnale, the real subject of Catch Me If You Can, speaks at Google about his life as a con man turned FBI fraud and cybersecurity expert, including advice on fraud, breaches and credit cards.\",\"why_hn\":\"Praised as a charismatic, engaging talk with practical security lessons, like why credit cards protect you better than debit cards.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"fraud and social engineering\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"frank abagnale\",\"fraud prevention\",\"social engineering\",\"cybersecurity\",\"talks at google\"],\"quote\":\"One of the best google talks I have seen.\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"wbSwFU6tY1c\",\"title\":\"Falcon Heavy Test Flight\",\"channel\":\"SpaceX\",\"summary\":\"SpaceX's livestream of the Falcon Heavy test flight, including the launch, side booster landings and Tesla Roadster payload. It was the first launch of the most powerful operational rocket at the time.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it as a landmark launch broadcast, and also dissect details like the duplicated booster landing feeds.\",\"content_type\":\"news\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"falcon heavy launch\",\"categories\":[\"Space exploration & spaceflight\"],\"tags\":[\"spacex\",\"falcon heavy\",\"rocket launch\",\"booster landing\",\"livestream\"],\"quote\":\"You should watch the launch, its probably one of the most interesting engineering projects of the last few years.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"wfm2xCKOCNk\",\"title\":\"Why Japan Looks the Way it Does: Zoning\",\"channel\":\"Life Where I'm From\",\"summary\":\"A video explaining why Japanese cities look the way they do, through Japan's national tiered zoning system that permits mixed use and flexible development. It compares this approach with more restrictive zoning elsewhere.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as the go-to explanation of Japanese zoning in housing and urbanism debates.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"japanese zoning\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\"],\"tags\":[\"zoning\",\"urban planning\",\"tokyo\",\"housing\",\"mixed-use\"],\"quote\":\"The best videos on this (in my opinion)\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"wh4aWZRtTwU\",\"title\":\"Brown; color is weird\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"Technology Connections explains why brown is not a spectral color but a context-dependent perception, essentially dark orange seen against brighter surroundings.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as a surprisingly magical explainer on color perception, often cited in color-space discussions.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"color perception\",\"categories\":[\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Graphic design & typography\"],\"tags\":[\"brown\",\"color vision\",\"rgb\",\"perception\",\"optics\"],\"quote\":\"made me watch a 20 minutes videos on the color brown and makes it feel like magic\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"yCcWpzY8dIA\",\"title\":\"Conflict Resolution for Eventual Consistency • Martin Kleppmann • GOTO 2016\",\"channel\":\"GOTO Conferences\",\"summary\":\"Martin Kleppmann's GOTO Berlin 2016 talk on how collaborative editors, offline apps, and multi-datacenter databases handle concurrent updates. It covers conflict resolution approaches including operational transformation and CRDTs, with a worked look at text CRDTs.\",\"why_hn\":\"Treated as the go-to approachable yet deep primer on CRDTs and collaborative editing, recommended repeatedly in threads on Automerge, local-first software and OT versus CRDT.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"CRDTs and eventual consistency\",\"categories\":[\"Distributed systems\",\"Databases & storage\"],\"tags\":[\"crdt\",\"collaborative editing\",\"operational transformation\",\"automerge\",\"distributed systems\"],\"quote\":\"Rest of the talk is also highly recommended for anyone interested in an approachable primer on CRDTs.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":9.0},{\"vid\":\"0PclgBd6_Zs\",\"title\":\"\\\"Programming a 144-computer chip to minimize power\\\" - Chuck Moore (2013)\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Chuck Moore, inventor of Forth, presents GreenArrays' 144-core asynchronous chip at Strange Loop 2013. He explains how idle cores draw almost no power and how tight code minimizes energy use.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the favorite recommendation for understanding Moore's minimalist, low-power multi-core designs and stack machines.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"low-power multicore forth chips\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Functional programming\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\"],\"tags\":[\"chuck moore\",\"forth\",\"greenarrays\",\"stack machines\",\"low power\"],\"quote\":\"This talk by Chuck Moore at Strange Loop was amazing\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"15RTC22Z2xI\",\"title\":\"Blockchains Are a Bad Idea (James Mickens)\",\"channel\":\"HBS Digital Initiative\",\"summary\":\"James Mickens of Harvard gives a funny lecture at HBS's Future Assembly arguing that blockchains are a very bad idea. He covers their trust, legal and practical shortcomings for real-world problems.\",\"why_hn\":\"A staple HN recommendation in crypto and Web3 threads as an entertaining, compelling case against blockchain.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"criticism of blockchains\",\"categories\":[\"Cryptography\",\"Futurism & technology and society\"],\"tags\":[\"blockchain\",\"cryptocurrency\",\"james mickens\",\"trust\",\"web3\"],\"quote\":\"James Mickens' \\\"Blockchains are a bad idea\\\" talk [1] is a great explanation that discusses exactly that.\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"5PmHRSeA2c8\",\"title\":\"DebConf 14: QA with Linus Torvalds\",\"channel\":\"Adnan Hodzic\",\"summary\":\"A 72-minute Q&A with Linus Torvalds at DebConf 14. Topics include the pain of distributing Linux binaries, distro fragmentation, kernel backwards compatibility, GPLv2 versus GPLv3 and conduct.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it as Linus's own explanation of why shipping software on Linux is hard and why he dislikes GPLv3.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"linux software distribution\",\"categories\":[\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"linus torvalds\",\"packaging\",\"gplv3\",\"distros\",\"binary compatibility\",\"debconf\"],\"quote\":\"Highly recommend watching Linus's answer ~6 minutes in\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"5TFDG-y-EHs\",\"title\":\"NaN Gates and Flip FLOPS\",\"channel\":\"suckerpinch\",\"summary\":\"Tom Murphy (suckerpinch) presents a deliberately trollish computer architecture built on IEEE-754 floating point, using NaN and infinity to define logic gates. He builds working hardware around the idea.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN fans hold it up as a favorite among his videos and an example of gloriously bonkers technical art, and cite it in floating-point discussions.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"floating point computing\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Satire & parody\"],\"tags\":[\"ieee-754\",\"nan\",\"logic gates\",\"tom7\",\"hardware\",\"humor\"],\"quote\":\"Suckerpinch barely ever releases a video, but when he does they are great.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"5pYeoZaoWrA\",\"title\":\"This is Financial Advice\",\"channel\":\"Folding Ideas\",\"summary\":\"Dan Olson's long Folding Ideas video on the GameStop meme stock phenomenon, its online community and the conspiracy thinking around it. It runs over two hours.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a thorough, sober critique of meme-stock culture and a good starting point whenever GameStop news appears.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"gamestop meme stock culture\",\"categories\":[\"Gaming culture & analysis\",\"Investing & personal finance\",\"Internet culture & memes\"],\"tags\":[\"gamestop\",\"meme stocks\",\"conspiracy theories\",\"online communities\",\"investing\"],\"quote\":\"I would highly recommend \\\"This is Financial Advice\\\" by Dan Olson to anyone not already familiar with the community.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"6y8XgGhXkTQ\",\"title\":\"Why Are You So Angry? Part 1: A Short History of Anita Sarkeesian\",\"channel\":\"Innuendo Studios\",\"summary\":\"The first part of Innuendo Studios' six-part video essay series on the online hostility around Anita Sarkeesian and GamerGate. It lays out the history and the motivations of the people involved.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends the series as a nuanced analysis of online harassment, defensiveness, and gaming culture conflict.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"gamergate and online harassment\",\"categories\":[\"Gaming culture & analysis\",\"Social issues & culture\",\"Internet culture & memes\"],\"tags\":[\"gamergate\",\"anita sarkeesian\",\"video essay\",\"online harassment\",\"gaming culture\"],\"quote\":\"This video series does a pretty amazing job in describing those two groups and their motivations\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"7Hk9jct2ozY\",\"title\":\"DNA animation (2002-2014) by Drew Berry and Etsuko Uno wehi.tv #ScienceArt\",\"channel\":\"WEHImovies\",\"summary\":\"A compilation of molecular animations of DNA replication and gene transcription by Drew Berry and Etsuko Uno at WEHI, made with no narration. It shows molecular machinery at work inside cells.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended whenever people want to see the mechanical, jostling reality of molecular biology, which many find awe-inspiring.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"molecular biology animation\",\"categories\":[\"Biology & biotechnology\",\"Evolution & genetics\",\"Animation & visual effects\"],\"tags\":[\"dna\",\"transcription\",\"wehi\",\"cell biology\",\"molecular machines\"],\"quote\":\"Seeing the mechanical nature of gene transciption and chemical signaling blew my mind.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"8Ab3ArE8W3s\",\"title\":\"\\\"Stop Writing Dead Programs\\\" by Jack Rusher (Strange Loop 2022)\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Jack Rusher's Strange Loop 2022 talk argues that most programming languages are still designed around punchcard-era assumptions. He makes the case for live, interactive programming environments, drawing on Lisp, Smalltalk and REPL-driven development.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it constantly as the talk on live programming and what modern tooling is missing, though a few found it too much.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"live programming environments\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"live programming\",\"repl\",\"lisp\",\"developer tools\",\"language design\"],\"quote\":\"One of my favorite talks is\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":8.5}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"9QMGAtxUlAc\",\"title\":\"Principles of Technology Leadership | Bryan Cantrill | Monktoberfest 2017\",\"channel\":\"RedMonk Tech Events\",\"summary\":\"Bryan Cantrill's Monktoberfest 2017 talk on leadership and organizational principles, ranging from the Gettysburg Address to Uber and Amazon. He argues for the difference between principles and values and criticizes vague corporate principles.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as a classic, must-watch talk for any discussion of company values, and especially for its takedown of Amazon's leadership principles.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"technology leadership and company values\",\"categories\":[\"Management, leadership & organizations\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"bryan cantrill\",\"company values\",\"leadership principles\",\"amazon\",\"uber\",\"engineering culture\"],\"quote\":\"No discussion of company values would be complete without a link to Bryan Cantrill’s classic talk,\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"9XaYdCdwiWU\",\"title\":\"#HITB2012KUL D1T3 - The Grugq - OPSEC: Because Jail is for wuftpd\",\"channel\":\"Hack In The Box Security Conference\",\"summary\":\"The Grugq's Hack In The Box 2012 talk on operational security for hackers, drawing on hacker indictments and counter-intelligence research. It reviews OPSEC goals and methods for avoiding detection and arrest, with case studies of how hackers got caught.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the go-to talk on OPSEC and pseudonymity, recommended in threads about Tor, Silk Road and staying anonymous online.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"operational security\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\"],\"tags\":[\"opsec\",\"anonymity\",\"tor\",\"grugq\",\"hacker arrests\"],\"quote\":\"It's a great watch if you're interested in how various (in)famous hackers thought they were secure and got