ers return to it as a worldview-shaping talk whose predictions about technology and startup ideas turned out to be prescient.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"startup ideas\",\"categories\":[\"Startups & entrepreneurship\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"paul graham\",\"y combinator\",\"startup ideas\",\"pycon\",\"predictions\"],\"quote\":\"It was the talk that brought about the biggest change in the way I think about the world, my ambitions.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"YnWhqhNdYyk\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2015: Kate Gregory “Stop Teaching C\\\"\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Kate Gregory argues at CppCon 2015 that C++ should be taught without starting from C: no char* strings, printf or raw arrays at first, with references before pointers.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people cite it constantly in C versus C++ debates as the case for teaching modern C++ idioms rather than C first.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"teaching c++\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Education & learning\"],\"tags\":[\"c++\",\"modern c++\",\"pedagogy\",\"cppcon\",\"kate gregory\"],\"quote\":\"Not only is she a great presenter, but she makes amazing points about what is fundamentally wrong with how C++ is taught in classrooms around the globe.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"Z4oYSByyRak\",\"title\":\"Zig: A programming language designed for robustness, optimality, and clarity –  Andrew Kelley\",\"channel\":\"Recurse Center\",\"summary\":\"Andrew Kelley, creator of Zig, introduces the language at the Recurse Center's Localhost series in 2018. He covers its goals of robustness, optimality and clarity, C interop, cross-compilation and explicit allocators.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as the definitive introduction to Zig straight from its author, and links to it whenever Zig's design choices come up.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"zig programming language\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"zig\",\"systems programming\",\"allocators\",\"cross-compilation\",\"language design\"],\"quote\":\"here's a great talk on zig from the recurse center\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"Zk11vI-7czE\",\"title\":\"Michael Moore Presents: Planet of the Humans | A Film by Jeff Gibbs | Full Documentary\",\"channel\":\"Michael Moore\",\"summary\":\"Planet of the Humans, a full documentary by Jeff Gibbs presented by Michael Moore, arguing that the green movement has been co-opted by corporate and wealthy interests and that techno-fixes are not enough.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as a contrarian critique of renewables and green tech, often recommended with caveats about bias and flawed conclusions.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"green energy critique\",\"categories\":[\"Renewables, batteries & storage\",\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"renewables\",\"greenwashing\",\"biomass\",\"climate\",\"michael moore\"],\"quote\":\"Strongly recommended.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"_ZG8HBuDjgc\",\"title\":\"DOUGLAS ADAMS: Parrots, the Universe and Everything\",\"channel\":\"University of California Television (UCTV)\",\"summary\":\"Douglas Adams gives a talk at UCSB, recorded shortly before his death, about the odd lives of animals and what they suggest about humanity's future. It draws on his Last Chance to See work and is funny and wide-ranging.\",\"why_hn\":\"Adams fans repeatedly call it one of his best talks, a funny and moving look at extinction and the environment.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"douglas adams on nature and extinction\",\"categories\":[\"Animals & nature\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Literature & writing craft\"],\"tags\":[\"douglas adams\",\"last chance to see\",\"extinction\",\"humor\",\"environment\"],\"quote\":\"Watch the whole thing. You will thank me later.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"fD2briZ6fB0\",\"title\":\"Incels | ContraPoints\",\"channel\":\"ContraPoints\",\"summary\":\"ContraPoints' 35-minute video essay on incels, examining the community, its worldview and how people get radicalized. It is delivered with theatrical style and an empathetic tone.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an empathetic, non-judgmental analysis of incel radicalization and how to counter it.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"incel radicalization\",\"categories\":[\"Social issues & culture\",\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Internet culture & memes\"],\"tags\":[\"contrapoints\",\"incels\",\"online radicalization\",\"video essay\",\"dating\"],\"quote\":\"Natalie Wynn's video^0 on incels always struck me as uniquely useful\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"gCWtkvDQ2ZI\",\"title\":\"\\\"Unison: a new distributed programming language\\\" by Paul Chiusano\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Paul Chiusano's Strange Loop 2019 talk introduces Unison, an open-source functional language for distributed, elastic systems. It covers content-addressed code identified by hashes of the syntax tree, a codebase manager, and algebraic effects called abilities.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the best introduction to Unison, with commenters finding hash-addressed immutable code mind-blowing.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"unison programming language\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Programming language design\",\"Distributed systems\"],\"tags\":[\"unison\",\"content-addressed code\",\"distributed computing\",\"algebraic effects\",\"functional programming\"],\"quote\":\"this talk might be a better introduction than just skimming at the landing page.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"m3svKOdZijA\",\"title\":\"Hey Underscore, You're Doing It Wrong!\",\"channel\":\"InfoQ\",\"summary\":\"Brian Lonsdorf explains why Underscore.js falls short as a functional programming library, then introduces currying, composition and point-free style in JavaScript. He argues for data-last argument ordering.\",\"why_hn\":\"A repeatedly recommended introduction to functional JavaScript that explains currying and why data should come last.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"functional programming in javascript\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Web development\"],\"tags\":[\"currying\",\"function composition\",\"point-free\",\"underscore.js\",\"ramda\"],\"quote\":\"this video gives a pretty good explanation in ~30 minutes\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"nJPERZDfyWc\",\"title\":\"Everything is a Remix (Original Series)\",\"channel\":\"Kirby Ferguson\",\"summary\":\"Kirby Ferguson's video essay series arguing that creativity is built on copying, transforming and combining existing work. It covers music, film and technology, then turns to copyright and patents.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN invokes it constantly in debates over originality, copyright and AI training, and calls it the best explanation of creativity as remixing.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"creativity and remix culture\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Film analysis & criticism\"],\"tags\":[\"remix\",\"copyright\",\"creativity\",\"originality\",\"video essay\"],\"quote\":\"The best explanation of creativity that I have found is a video series by Kirby Ferguson called Everything Is a Remix\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"pTn6Ewhb27k\",\"title\":\"Why No One Has Measured The Speed Of Light\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explains why the one-way speed of light has never been measured, only the round-trip speed, since measuring it requires synchronized clocks that assume the answer. It explores the implications for relativity.\",\"why_hn\":\"Used as the standard reference in threads about light speed, clock synchronization and untestable assumptions in physics.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"one-way speed of light\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"speed of light\",\"special relativity\",\"clock synchronization\",\"veritasium\",\"physics\"],\"quote\":\"I found the video very compelling even as a thought experiment.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"prXuyMCgbTc\",\"title\":\"Exploring the Tech and Design of Noita\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"In this 2019 GDC session, Petri Purho of Nolla Games explains the technical side of Noita's physics engine, including scaling a falling-sand simulation to large continuous worlds and adding destructible rigid bodies. It also shows how the emergent physics drives the gameplay.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the go-to talk for anyone building falling-sand or pixel-physics simulations, and several commenters say they modeled their own engines on it.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"falling sand game engines\",\"categories\":[\"Game programming & engines\",\"Game design\"],\"tags\":[\"noita\",\"gdc\",\"cellular automata\",\"physics simulation\",\"game architecture\",\"multithreading\"],\"quote\":\"Highly recommended if you want to dive deeper into the topic.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"r-A78RgMhZU\",\"title\":\"A Talk Near the Future of Python (a.k.a., Dave live-codes a WebAssembly Interpreter)\",\"channel\":\"David Beazley\",\"summary\":\"David Beazley's PyCon India closing keynote, in which he live-codes a stack machine and grows it into an interpreter that runs WebAssembly, then uses it to play a Rust-written game. It requires no prior WebAssembly knowledge.\",\"why_hn\":\"Regarded as a brilliant live-coding talk that teaches WASM and interpreter writing, with several calling it among the best conference talks they have seen.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"webassembly interpreters\",\"categories\":[\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"webassembly\",\"python\",\"live coding\",\"interpreters\",\"stack machine\"],\"quote\":\"it's quite possible the best conference talk I've ever seen.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"rI8tNMsozo0\",\"title\":\"Rails Conf 2012 Keynote: Simplicity Matters by Rich Hickey\",\"channel\":\"Confreaks\",\"summary\":\"Rich Hickey's keynote at RailsConf 2012, a close sibling of his 'Simple Made Easy' talk. He distinguishes simple (not intertwined) from easy (familiar), and argues that software complexity comes from complecting things, with Rails-specific examples including large gem collections.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an alternative or companion to 'Simple Made Easy', and quotes its ideas about simplicity, complexity and 'hairballs' in discussions of frameworks, JSON and data design.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software simplicity\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"rich hickey\",\"simple vs easy\",\"complexity\",\"clojure\",\"rails\"],\"quote\":\"maybe watch this version instead, since it's almost the same talk but for a Rails Conference, with a few references\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"vTMF6xEiAaY\",\"title\":\"Roblox Pressured Us to Delete Our Video. So We Dug Deeper.\",\"channel\":\"People Make Games\",\"summary\":\"A People Make Games follow-up investigation into Roblox, built on stories from players and creators after the channel's first video. It covers the platform's treatment of young developers and its moderation.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited heavily in Roblox threads as a damning investigation into child labor, exploitation, and weak moderation, and suggested viewing for parents.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"roblox exploitation\",\"categories\":[\"Gaming culture & analysis\",\"Labor, work & inequality\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"roblox\",\"child labor\",\"moderation\",\"people make games\",\"game platforms\"],\"quote\":\"Watch both People Make Games video about Roblox and try to argue that the company should not deserve all the public controversy it is getting.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"wlR5gYd6um0\",\"title\":\"Mastering the Vim Language\",\"channel\":\"thoughtbot\",\"summary\":\"Chris Toomey of thoughtbot explains Vim as a language of commands, motions and text objects that combine into precise edits. He shows how to master the built-in pieces and extend them.\",\"why_hn\":\"Repeatedly called the best video introduction to Vim's underlying model.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"vim editing\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"vim\",\"text objects\",\"motions\",\"modal editing\",\"editor productivity\"],\"quote\":\"In video form, the best introduction I have seen is Chris Toomey’s\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"wo84LFzx5nI\",\"title\":\"Casey Muratori – The Big OOPs: Anatomy of a Thirty-five-year Mistake – BSC 2025\",\"channel\":\"Better Software Conference\",\"summary\":\"Casey Muratori's two-hour talk tracing the history of object-oriented programming back to the 1960s and examining how its ideas, and later entity component systems, came about. It is followed by a Q&A.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it for its source-based history of OOP, though some dispute its account of Alan Kay.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of object-oriented programming\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Software performance & optimization\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"oop\",\"casey muratori\",\"entity component system\",\"programming history\",\"software design\"],\"quote\":\"which really is hunting back and back for the cosmogenesis of objects, and covers so much terrain.\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":8.5},{\"vid\":\"08mwXICY4JM\",\"title\":\"What Really Happened During the Texas Power Grid Outage?\",\"channel\":\"Practical Engineering\",\"summary\":\"Practical Engineering's explanation of the February 2021 Texas winter storm and the resulting power grid outage. It lays out the facts about ERCOT, the grid's isolation, and how generation across fuel types failed in the cold.\",\"why_hn\":\"Whenever Texas grid failures come up, HN people link it as the calm, technically informed account that cuts through political blame.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"power grid failure\",\"categories\":[\"Power grid, heating & buildings\",\"Civil engineering & construction\"],\"tags\":[\"texas\",\"ercot\",\"winter storm\",\"electrical grid\",\"infrastructure\"],\"quote\":\"The best low-emotion, technically informed analysis of \\\"Snowpocalypse\\\" is probably this one from \\\"Practical Engineering\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"3Y3jE3B8HsE\",\"title\":\"The Voting System That Should Replace Every Election\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"A short CGP Grey explainer on the alternative vote (instant runoff voting) versus first past the post, made around the 2011 UK referendum. It shows how ranked ballots are tallied and why they avoid spoiler effects.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN's go-to link whenever ranked-choice voting or voting reform comes up, because it explains the mechanics quickly and entertainingly.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ranked-choice voting\",\"categories\":[\"US politics & elections\",\"Probability & game theory\"],\"tags\":[\"alternative vote\",\"instant runoff\",\"first past the post\",\"electoral reform\",\"spoiler effect\"],\"quote\":\"I've been a huge fan of this system ever since CGP Grey made an excellent video on how it works and why it's great.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"6avJHaC3C2U\",\"title\":\"The Art of Code - Dylan Beattie\",\"channel\":\"NDC Conferences\",\"summary\":\"Dylan Beattie's NDC London talk on code written for art, fun and curiosity rather than utility. It includes quines, live music programming with Sonic Pi, and a memorable closing.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN regularly names it among the best programming talks ever, praising the storytelling, the demos and the ending.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"creative coding\",\"categories\":[\"Creative coding & generative art\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"quines\",\"sonic pi\",\"esoteric programming\",\"live coding\",\"fizzbuzz\"],\"quote\":\"one of the most captivating tech talks I've ever seen.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"7EmboKQH8lM\",\"title\":\"Clean Code - Uncle Bob / Lesson 1\",\"channel\":\"UnityCoin\",\"summary\":\"The first lesson of Uncle Bob (Robert C. Martin)'s \\\"Coding Better World Together\\\" series. It makes the case for clean code and for treating software craftsmanship as an ethical and professional responsibility. It runs about 109 minutes.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it better than the Clean Code book, a must-watch for engineers that changes how they read and write code.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"clean code\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"clean code\",\"uncle bob\",\"software ethics\",\"agile\",\"professionalism\"],\"quote\":\"Clean Code is a great book, but the presentation is so much better\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"7ImvlS8PLIo\",\"title\":\"'A Universe From Nothing' by Lawrence Krauss, AAI 2009\",\"channel\":\"Richard Dawkins Foundation for Reason & Science\",\"summary\":\"Lawrence Krauss's 2009 talk on the current picture of the universe, how it will end and how it could have come from nothing. It covers the expanding universe, cosmic background radiation and the cosmological constant.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN regards it as a famous, mesmerizing talk, especially for the point that distant galaxies will become unobservable.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cosmology\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"Religion, belief & skepticism\"],\"tags\":[\"lawrence krauss\",\"universe from nothing\",\"expanding universe\",\"physics\",\"cosmological constant\"],\"quote\":\"There is also one of my favorite talks tangentially related by Lawrence Krauss.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"AF8d72mA41M\",\"title\":\"Why It Was Almost Impossible to Make the Blue LED\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium tells the story of how Shuji Nakamura and others made the blue LED, long thought nearly impossible. It covers the materials challenge of gallium nitride and the persistence it took.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a gripping story of single-minded persistence against the herd, and as the back story behind modern LED lighting.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"invention of the blue led\",\"categories\":[\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\",\"Scientific method & science communication\",\"Materials science\"],\"tags\":[\"blue led\",\"nakamura\",\"gallium nitride\",\"invention\",\"persistence\"],\"quote\":\"is goes into great detail on what it took to make the blue LED. It's quite fascinating.\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"EKWGGDXe5MA\",\"title\":\"Richard Feynman Computer Science Lecture - Hardware, Software and Heuristics\",\"channel\":\"Muon Ray\",\"summary\":\"A lecture by Richard Feynman on how computers work, using the analogy of a filing clerk that becomes progressively dumber and faster. It also covers heuristics and how computers handle data and solve problems in finite time.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently recommended as the clearest plain-language explanation of what a computer fundamentally is, with at least one commenter saying it changed how they think about programming.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"how computers work\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"University lectures & courses\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"feynman\",\"filing clerk analogy\",\"heuristics\",\"computing fundamentals\",\"information processing\"],\"quote\":\"This actually changed the way I think about it after years of programming.\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"HCUct4NlxE0\",\"title\":\"Joe Ades , Greatest Salesman in New York City History !!! #KONYH !!\",\"channel\":\"KING OF NEW YORK HACKS\",\"summary\":\"A short street-level clip of Joe Ades, the New York pitchman who sold vegetable peelers to crowds on the sidewalk. It shows his patter, showmanship and crowd handling in action.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats him as a master class in salesmanship and live presentation, where how you sell matters more than what you sell.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"street salesmanship\",\"categories\":[\"Product, marketing & sales\",\"Arts & entertainment (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"sales\",\"pitching\",\"presentation\",\"new york\",\"peeler\"],\"quote\":\"I urge you, I implore you, I beg you, stop what you're doing and watch Joe in action\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"Ip3X9LOh2dk\",\"title\":\"The determinant | Chapter 6, Essence of linear algebra\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"A 10-minute 3Blue1Brown chapter explaining the determinant as the factor by which a linear transformation scales area or volume. It builds the idea geometrically with animations before any formulas.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly points people to it as the clearest visual intuition for determinants, often saying it finally made the concept click.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"linear algebra\",\"categories\":[\"Pure mathematics\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"determinant\",\"linear transformations\",\"geometric intuition\",\"3blue1brown\",\"matrices\"],\"quote\":\"I have warmed up to determinants ever since I saw 3Blue1Brown give a fairly intuitive explanation for them\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"J3FcbFqSoQY\",\"title\":\"I Genetically Engineered *MYSELF* to Fix Lactose Intolerance\",\"channel\":\"The Thought Emporium\",\"summary\":\"The Thought Emporium's host describes making and taking a DIY gene therapy to treat his own lactose intolerance. It uses an AAV virus to deliver DNA coding for the lactose-digesting enzyme, made with biohacker Gabriel Licina's help.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN keeps citing it as the standard example of accessible DIY gene engineering and self-experimentation, in threads on gene therapy, CRISPR and biohacking.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"diy gene therapy\",\"categories\":[\"Biology & biotechnology\",\"Evolution & genetics\"],\"tags\":[\"gene therapy\",\"biohacking\",\"lactose intolerance\",\"aav\",\"self-experimentation\",\"genetic engineering\"],\"quote\":\"The rest of the channel is pure biohacking gold.\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"KLjgBLwH3Wc\",\"title\":\"Minding your mitochondria | Dr. Terry Wahls | TEDxIowaCity\",\"channel\":\"TEDx Talks\",\"summary\":\"Dr. Terry Wahls tells how she used diet to manage her multiple sclerosis and get out of a wheelchair, drawing on mitochondrial biology. TED flagged the talk as health advice based on personal narrative.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as a moving personal story that motivated dietary changes, though it is also cited as anecdotal evidence.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"diet and multiple sclerosis\",\"categories\":[\"Nutrition & diet science\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"terry wahls\",\"nutrition\",\"multiple sclerosis\",\"mitochondria\",\"wahls protocol\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend the talk, it is both very informative and very moving.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"KrksBdWcZgQ\",\"title\":\"Breaking the x86 Instruction Set\",\"channel\":\"Black Hat\",\"summary\":\"Christopher Domas presents at Black Hat 2017 how page fault analysis and processor fuzzing can exhaustively search the x86 instruction set. The talk uncovers undocumented instructions and hardware bugs and shows limits of disassemblers.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a clever, entertaining and easy-to-follow talk on finding hidden x86 instructions with the Sandsifter tool.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"x86 undocumented instructions\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\"],\"tags\":[\"x86\",\"fuzzing\",\"sandsifter\",\"disassemblers\",\"cpu security\",\"reverse engineering\"],\"quote\":\"I found it easy to follow and pretty entertaining.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"Mfsnlbd-4xQ\",\"title\":\"Functional Composition - Chris Ford\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"Chris Ford builds up from a single sine wave through layered abstractions to a Bach canon, using Clojure and the Overtone audio environment. The talk shows music theory as a natural fit for functional programming.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as a delightful talk that makes music theory click and introduces Clojure and Overtone.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"music and functional programming\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Creative coding & generative art\"],\"tags\":[\"clojure\",\"overtone\",\"music theory\",\"functional programming\",\"live coding\",\"sine waves\"],\"quote\":\"Recommend this talk that made Music 'click' for me.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"NdSD07U5uBs\",\"title\":\"Alan Kay, 2015: Power of Simplicity\",\"channel\":\"Kenneth Friedman\",\"summary\":\"Alan Kay's 2015 talk on the power of simplicity, arguing that systems become simpler by building on more sophisticated fundamental building blocks and that big research horizons drove invention at Xerox PARC. It ranges across computing, tools and scaling software.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly cites it as a thought-provoking talk on reducing software complexity and on the state of computing research.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"software simplicity\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"alan kay\",\"xerox parc\",\"complexity\",\"abstraction\",\"computing research\"],\"quote\":\"Easily over of the most thought provoking presentations I have seen recently.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"QG0Apol-oj0\",\"title\":\"What your Differential Pairs Wish You Knew with Rick Hartley - AltiumLive Keynote\",\"channel\":\"Altium\",\"summary\":\"Rick Hartley's AltiumLive keynote on differential pairs in PC boards. He argues the rules from cables and twisted pairs do not carry over, and covers which pair geometry gives the best impedance control, the right spacing, routing practice and how much skew is acceptable.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN engineers cite Hartley as the intuitive, field-based alternative to cargo-cult PCB advice, especially on differential pair routing, reference planes and EMI.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"pcb differential pair design\",\"categories\":[\"Electronics & circuit design\"],\"tags\":[\"differential pairs\",\"pcb layout\",\"signal integrity\",\"impedance control\",\"emi\"],\"quote\":\"this is absolutely worth your time if you design PCBs.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"TfYf_rPWUdY\",\"title\":\"mRNA Translation (Advanced)\",\"channel\":\"DNA Learning Center\",\"summary\":\"A short animation from the DNA Learning Center showing how mRNA carries a gene's message to the ribosome, where tRNA and codons are used to assemble amino acids into a protein.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently linked as a mind-blowing visualization of how RNA is translated into protein, especially in threads on mRNA vaccines and cell biology.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"mrna translation\",\"categories\":[\"Biology & biotechnology\",\"Evolution & genetics\"],\"tags\":[\"mrna\",\"ribosome\",\"protein synthesis\",\"molecular biology\",\"animation\"],\"quote\":\"viewing these visualizations blew my mind\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"U7VwtOrwceo\",\"title\":\"Guarding Against Physical Attacks: The Xbox One Story — Tony Chen, Microsoft\",\"channel\":\"Platform Security Summit\",\"summary\":\"Microsoft's Tony Chen explains how the Xbox One was designed to resist physical attacks, including secure boot, encrypted memory, a security processor and a hypervisor. The goal was to make any hardware attack cost more than the price of many games.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it constantly in console, DRM and hardware-security threads as the authoritative account of Xbox One's threat model.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"console hardware security\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Consumer devices & gadgets\"],\"tags\":[\"xbox one\",\"secure boot\",\"physical attacks\",\"hypervisor\",\"anti-piracy\",\"trusted hardware\"],\"quote\":\"This is a very interesting talk from Microsoft about how they made Xbox immune to physical attacks\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"Uj-rK8V-rik\",\"title\":\"Google Self-Driving Car Project | SXSW Interactive 2016\",\"channel\":\"SXSW\",\"summary\":\"Chris Urmson's SXSW 2016 talk on Google's self-driving car project, covering 1.3 million self-driven miles and the gap between hype and reality. It shows sensor visualizations and playbacks of hard situations the cars met, and discusses failures such as the low-speed bus collision.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it repeatedly as one of the best candid talks on how a real self-driving system sees the road and where it struggles.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"self-driving cars\",\"categories\":[\"Self-driving & autonomous vehicles\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"waymo\",\"google self-driving car\",\"lidar\",\"autonomous driving\",\"sensor data\"],\"quote\":\"It's one of the best talks on the subject.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"Up7LcbGZFuo\",\"title\":\"Domain Modeling Made Functional - Scott Wlaschin\",\"channel\":\"NDC Conferences\",\"summary\":\"Scott Wlaschin's NDC talk on using statically typed functional programming, F# in particular, to model a business domain. Types are used to represent the domain precisely and to encode business rules so that illegal states cannot be built, with the compiler acting as an instant unit test.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend it as an eye-opening talk whose domain-modeling ideas carry over to other languages and make a strong compiler feel like a coding partner.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"functional domain modeling\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Software architecture & design\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"f#\",\"domain-driven design\",\"type systems\",\"functional programming\",\"making illegal states unrepresentable\"],\"quote\":\"If anyone is looking for a really eye-opening video take a look at that this.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"VscdPA6sUkc\",\"title\":\"'Diablo':  A Classic Game Postmortem\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"David Brevik's GDC 2016 postmortem of Diablo covers how it went from a turn-based claymation DOS concept to a real-time action RPG, plus the story of Blizzard North and development anecdotes.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently recommended whenever Diablo or its origins come up, for its candid account of the human side of game development.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"game development postmortem\",\"categories\":[\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Game design\"],\"tags\":[\"diablo\",\"blizzard north\",\"action rpg\",\"gdc\",\"game history\"],\"quote\":\"heartily recommended if you want to know about the human aspect behind game dev\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"WFCvkkDSfIU\",\"title\":\"Animations of unseeable biology | Drew Berry | TED\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Drew Berry shows scientifically accurate animations of molecular processes inside cells, such as DNA replication, at TEDxSydney.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it for mind-blowing visuals that make cell biology tangible.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"molecular biology animation\",\"categories\":[\"Biology & biotechnology\",\"Data visualization & information design\"],\"tags\":[\"dna replication\",\"cell biology\",\"scientific visualization\",\"protein machines\",\"ted\"],\"quote\":\"I had a much greater appreciation for how our bodies are made up of trillions of tiny protein machines.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"X2wLP0izeJE\",\"title\":\"Ira Glass on Storytelling 3\",\"channel\":\"warphotography\",\"summary\":\"A five-minute clip of Ira Glass on the gap between good taste and early creative work. He says beginners know their work falls short and must keep producing to close the gap.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters return to it as motivation for creators who feel their work is not good enough yet.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"creative work and taste\",\"categories\":[\"Productivity & self-improvement\",\"Literature & writing craft\"],\"tags\":[\"ira glass\",\"taste gap\",\"creativity\",\"motivation\",\"storytelling\"],\"quote\":\"Ira Glass's take on this is so good and I go back to it often\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"XheAMrS8Q1c\",\"title\":\"Michael Levin: The electrical blueprints that orchestrate life | TED\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Biologist Michael Levin explains the bioelectric layer that coordinates cells to build organs and bodies, showing footage of two-headed worms and introducing xenobots. He discusses implications for medicine and the nature of life.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters use it as the accessible 20-minute entry point to Levin's bioelectricity research whenever regeneration or cell signaling comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"bioelectricity and regeneration\",\"categories\":[\"Biology & biotechnology\",\"Evolution & genetics\"],\"tags\":[\"michael levin\",\"xenobots\",\"bioelectricity\",\"regeneration\",\"morphogenesis\",\"ted\"],\"quote\":\"If you have 20 minutes to watch this ted talk interview I highly recommend it.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"YQ_xWvX1n9g\",\"title\":\"Line Goes Up – The Problem With NFTs\",\"channel\":\"Folding Ideas\",\"summary\":\"Dan Olson's roughly two-hour video essay critiques NFTs, cryptocurrency, and Web3, covering their technical, economic, and ideological flaws. It is heavily sourced and ends with the advice to ask whether a project needs crypto to work.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the go-to long-form critique of crypto that HN commenters recommend whenever skeptics ask for a thorough, well-researched takedown.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cryptocurrency and nft critique\",\"categories\":[\"Cryptography\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Tech industry & business models\"],\"tags\":[\"nfts\",\"web3\",\"crypto scams\",\"video essay\",\"folding ideas\"],\"quote\":\"probably the most detailed and incisive critique of cryptocurrency and NFTs that I've seen\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"agZ0xISi40E\",\"title\":\"The EU Has Orbits, and the UK Fell Out of All of Them\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey's six-minute explainer of the relationships the UK could have with the EU, based on Barnier's slide, shown as orbits of different closeness. It explains why the UK's red lines ruled out the closer options.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the quickest clear overview of the Brexit options, though later commenters note it predates the final outcome.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"brexit eu relationships\",\"categories\":[\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\",\"World history\"],\"tags\":[\"cgp grey\",\"brexit\",\"european union\",\"uk politics\",\"trade\"],\"quote\":\"taking from the amazing short overview here\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"atvtBE6t48M\",\"title\":\"Intro to Synthesis Part 1 - The Building Blocks of Sound & Synthesis\",\"channel\":\"matrixsynth\",\"summary\":\"Dean Friedman's near-hour-long introduction to synthesis, covering the building blocks of sound and the controls on a synthesizer. He slowly adjusts knobs so viewers hear what each parameter does.\",\"why_hn\":\"Repeatedly called the best beginner course on audio synthesis, since the concepts have not changed since it was made.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"sound synthesis\",\"categories\":[\"Music theory, synthesis & production\"],\"tags\":[\"synthesizers\",\"subtractive synthesis\",\"audio\",\"music production\",\"oscillators\",\"filters\"],\"quote\":\"It's the best educational video I've seen on YT\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"d8RRE2rDw4k\",\"title\":\"The Best Country in the World for Drivers\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes argues that the Netherlands, famed for cycling, is also the best country for drivers. It explains how prioritizing walking, cycling and transit makes driving faster and more pleasant.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the argument that better alternatives to driving also improve things for drivers, and as an introduction to Dutch urban planning.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"dutch urban planning\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\"],\"tags\":[\"netherlands\",\"driving\",\"cycling infrastructure\",\"urbanism\",\"traffic\"],\"quote\":\"One of the many excellent videos on the Not Just Bikes YouTube channel [1] shows how the Netherlands has done it.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"fHNmRkzxHWs\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2014: Chandler Carruth \\\"Efficiency with Algorithms, Performance with Data Structures\\\"\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Chandler Carruth's CppCon 2014 talk on writing fast C++, explaining why data structure choice and memory layout matter more than clever algorithms. It covers why standard containers like unordered_map perform poorly on modern CPUs.\",\"why_hn\":\"A perennial recommendation for anyone learning performance optimization, cited whenever hash tables, cache behavior or STL containers come up.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"c++ performance\",\"categories\":[\"Software performance & optimization\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Algorithms & data structures\"],\"tags\":[\"c++\",\"data structures\",\"cache locality\",\"unordered_map\",\"performance optimization\",\"cppcon\"],\"quote\":\"I'm also a big fan of \\\"Efficiency with Algorithms, Performance with Data Structures\\\" by Chandler Carruth at CppCon 2014.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":8.0}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"hX2aZUav-54\",\"title\":\"It’s Time To Break Up With Our Gas Stoves | Climate Town\",\"channel\":\"Climate Town\",\"summary\":\"Climate Town argues for moving away from gas stoves. It covers the gas industry's decades of marketing and lobbying, health effects of gas cooking, methane leaks, and the case for induction.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it in gas stove and induction threads for exposing how the idea that gas is better for cooking was a marketing ploy.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"gas stove electrification\",\"categories\":[\"Power grid, heating & buildings\",\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\"],\"tags\":[\"gas stoves\",\"induction cooking\",\"natural gas lobbying\",\"building electrification\",\"methane\"],\"quote\":\"Here's a great video on the topic by Climate Town, an awesome educator on climate change\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"hxM8QmyZXtg\",\"title\":\"[EPILEPSY WARNING] How fast should an unoptimized terminal run?\",\"channel\":\"Molly Rocket\",\"summary\":\"Casey Muratori shows how fast a terminal renderer can run, building a simple reference renderer that handles Unicode and colored text far quicker than Windows Terminal. He argues that common excuses for slow terminals do not hold up.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the canonical reference for the claim that modern software wastes hardware, and the proof-of-concept behind the Windows Terminal performance debate.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software performance\",\"categories\":[\"Software performance & optimization\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"terminal rendering\",\"casey muratori\",\"windows terminal\",\"refterm\",\"optimization\"],\"quote\":\"Casey spent <1 week making RefTerm, a skeleton proto-terminal with the same constraints Microsoft people had\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":8.0},{\"vid\":\"j3mhkYbznBk\",\"title\":\"Richard Feynman  