annel\":\"PyData\",\"summary\":\"James Powell's nearly two-hour PyData talk builds up Python's data model, decorators, generators and context managers, coding live in Vim. It aims to take intermediate programmers toward real Python expertise.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN calls it one of the best technical talks and the clearest explanation of decorators, generators and context managers.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"python data model\",\"categories\":[\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"python\",\"decorators\",\"generators\",\"context managers\",\"pydata\"],\"quote\":\"It's legitimately the best technical talk I think I've seen and I can't recommend it highly enough.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"dPHPNgIihR0\",\"title\":\"Manufactured Discontent and Fortnite\",\"channel\":\"Folding Ideas\",\"summary\":\"Dan Olson of Folding Ideas critiques Fortnite's monetization, arguing it uses manufactured discontent, such as shop design and skins, to push spending. The video dissects the psychological techniques behind the game's business model.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it as the definitive critique of Fortnite's monetization and of manipulative free-to-play design.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"fortnite monetization\",\"categories\":[\"Gaming culture & analysis\",\"Game design\"],\"tags\":[\"fortnite\",\"microtransactions\",\"manufactured discontent\",\"game design\",\"video essay\"],\"quote\":\"There's a great video[1] that goes into detail on the psychological manipulation used in Fortnite's monetization model.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.5}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"fpbOEoRrHyU\",\"title\":\"Net Neutrality: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)\",\"channel\":\"LastWeekTonight\",\"summary\":\"John Oliver's 13-minute Last Week Tonight segment explains the 2014 net neutrality fight over cable companies creating unequal internet speeds. It uses comedy to make a dull regulatory topic engaging and tells viewers how to comment to the FCC.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters use it as the go-to funny, accessible explanation of net neutrality and credit it with driving public comments to the FCC.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"net neutrality\",\"categories\":[\"Networking & internet infrastructure\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Tech industry & business models\"],\"tags\":[\"net neutrality\",\"fcc\",\"isp competition\",\"john oliver\",\"telecom policy\"],\"quote\":\"John Oliver did a much better job (and funnier, btw)\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"gB9n2gHsHN4\",\"title\":\"Fractals are typically not self-similar\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"3Blue1Brown explains fractal dimension and shows why many fractals are not strictly self-similar. Self-similarity turns out to be a special case of the broader idea of fractional dimension.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the standard pointer for a clear, visual explanation of what fractals are, and for correcting the idea that fractal means self-similar.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"fractals\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"fractal dimension\",\"self-similarity\",\"3blue1brown\",\"mathematics\",\"visual math\"],\"quote\":\"Here is are two very clear explanations of fractal dimension by the man himself, Grant from 3b1b.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"hx7BXih7zx8\",\"title\":\"Andrej Karpathy - AI for Full-Self Driving at Tesla\",\"channel\":\"Matroid\",\"summary\":\"Andrej Karpathy, then Tesla's head of AI, presents at ScaledML 2020 on how Tesla builds full self-driving using vision-based neural nets and fleet data collection. He covers training custom detectors and neural nets gradually replacing traditional code.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly points to it as the best public technical overview of Tesla's FSD approach, used both to defend and to critique Tesla's progress.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"self-driving neural networks\",\"categories\":[\"Self-driving & autonomous vehicles\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"Computer vision, speech & NLP\"],\"tags\":[\"tesla autopilot\",\"computer vision\",\"fleet learning\",\"andrej karpathy\",\"autonomous driving\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend watching head of AI at Tesla Andrej Karpathy's talk on their self driving architecture.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"l8XOZJkozfI\",\"title\":\"The Voting System That's Actually Too Good for Politicians to Allow\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey explains the Single Transferable Vote, a proportional ranked-ballot system, and why politicians have little incentive to adopt it. It is a short animated explainer of how multi-winner ranked voting works.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN's default link for explaining STV in any thread on voting reform, ranked choice or gerrymandering.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"single transferable vote\",\"categories\":[\"US politics & elections\",\"Politics & history (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"voting systems\",\"stv\",\"ranked choice\",\"electoral reform\",\"proportional representation\"],\"quote\":\"CGP Grey did a great video on STV\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"l_F9jxsfGCw\",\"title\":\"Darius Kazemi, Tiny Subversions - XOXO Festival (2014)\",\"channel\":\"XOXO Festival\",\"summary\":\"Darius Kazemi's XOXO 2014 talk on releasing 72 weird internet side projects in a year. Framed as 'How I Won the Lottery,' it argues that success needs both luck and buying many tickets.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as the favorite antidote to survivorship-bias success stories, arguing that trying many cheap things raises your odds.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"luck and side projects\",\"categories\":[\"Internet culture & memes\",\"Startups & entrepreneurship\",\"Creative coding & generative art\"],\"tags\":[\"side projects\",\"survivorship bias\",\"luck\",\"indie creators\",\"bots\",\"xoxo\"],\"quote\":\"Darius Kazemi's iconic 2014[1] talk\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"nM9f0W2KD5s\",\"title\":\"Peter Thiel on \\\"The Portal\\\", Episode #001: \\\"An Era of Stagnation & Universal Institutional Failure.\\\"\",\"channel\":\"Eric Weinstein\",\"summary\":\"Peter Thiel is the first guest on Eric Weinstein's The Portal, in a nearly three-hour conversation about technological stagnation, institutional failure and the link between growth and violence. They discuss universities, the economy and the search for a more ambitious future.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend it as a thought-provoking, wide-ranging case for the stagnation thesis, worth hearing even if you disagree with Thiel.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"technological stagnation\",\"categories\":[\"Futurism & technology and society\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\"],\"tags\":[\"peter thiel\",\"eric weinstein\",\"stagnation\",\"institutions\",\"innovation\"],\"quote\":\"I recommend that people listen to that episode even if it's not interesting to you.\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"nYg6jzotiAc\",\"title\":\"HD Feynman: FUN TO IMAGINE complete (with optional Chinese subtitles)\",\"channel\":\"Christopher Sykes\",\"summary\":\"A restored HD upload of the 1983 BBC series Fun to Imagine, in which Richard Feynman talks informally about everyday science. Topics include jiggling atoms, fire, rubber bands, magnets, electricity, mirrors, seeing, big numbers and ways of thinking.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as an equation-free, charming way to hear Feynman explain physics to laypeople, including his famous refusal to explain magnetism with simple analogies.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"physics explained for laypeople\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\",\"Scientific method & science communication\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"feynman\",\"physics\",\"magnetism\",\"science communication\",\"bbc\"],\"quote\":\"It's an hour of fun science, and especially accessible for anyone who is curious about physics but doesn't enjoy all the equations.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"o0VrZPBskpg\",\"title\":\"Toward Rational, Authentic Food Choices | Melanie Joy | TEDxMünchen\",\"channel\":\"TEDx Talks\",\"summary\":\"Psychologist Melanie Joy's TEDxMunchen talk introduces carnism, the invisible belief system that conditions people to eat some animals but not others, and how it shapes reactions to food choices.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in vegetarian and animal-welfare threads as an introduction to the psychology behind how we treat animals differently.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"carnism and food psychology\",\"categories\":[\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Nutrition & diet science\"],\"tags\":[\"carnism\",\"meat eating\",\"animal ethics\",\"psychology\",\"veganism\"],\"quote\":\"This TED talk from Dr. Melanie Joy is a good introduction to Carnism\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"rHIkrotSwcc\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2019: Chandler Carruth “There Are No Zero-cost Abstractions”\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Chandler Carruth argues in a CppCon 2019 talk that C++ has no truly zero-cost abstractions. Using examples like unique_ptr, ABI constraints and compile-time costs, he shows that abstractions cost runtime, compile time or human effort.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it whenever zero-cost abstraction claims come up, especially for why unique_ptr is not free on some ABIs.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"c++ abstraction costs\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"c++\",\"zero-cost abstractions\",\"unique_ptr\",\"abi\",\"compilers\",\"performance\"],\"quote\":\"Chandler presents his point clearly and goes deep enough into the subject.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"rRbY3TMUcgQ\",\"title\":\"Erlang The Movie II: The Sequel\",\"channel\":\"gar1t\",\"summary\":\"A humorous 10-minute parody sequel to Erlang: The Movie, in which Erlang's creators discuss the aging language and meet a surprise helper. It plays on the Outlaw Techno Psychobitch joke for OTP.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN loves it as a funny, shorter sequel and even uses it to introduce Erlang in university classes.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"erlang\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"erlang\",\"otp\",\"parody\",\"programming humor\",\"functional programming\"],\"quote\":\"I use Erlang the Movie and its Sequel whenever I teach my classes at Oxford Uni.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"s86-Z-CbaHA\",\"title\":\"The Banach–Tarski Paradox\",\"channel\":\"Vsauce\",\"summary\":\"Vsauce's roughly 24 minute explainer on the Banach-Tarski paradox, building from infinity and set theory to the decomposition of a sphere into pieces that form two spheres.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an accurate, entertaining on-ramp to set theory and infinity, worth pausing and rewatching.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"banach-tarski paradox\",\"categories\":[\"Logic & formal methods\",\"Pure mathematics\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"infinity\",\"set theory\",\"paradox\",\"vsauce\",\"measure theory\"],\"quote\":\"This vsauce video is actually a really accurate (and entertaining!) introduction to set theory\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"sPhpelUfu8Q\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2017: Juan Pedro Bolivar Puente “Postmodern immutable data structures”\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Juan Pedro Bolivar Puente explains immutable and persistent data structures in C++ and how they support value semantics. He presents the Immer library, rrb-trees, and a prototype text editor that opens a huge file instantly.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people point to it for rrb-trees, text editor data structures and bringing Clojure-style persistent structures to C++.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"immutable data structures\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Functional programming\",\"Algorithms & data structures\"],\"tags\":[\"immutable data\",\"rrb-trees\",\"c++\",\"immer\",\"text editors\",\"functional programming\"],\"quote\":\"Immer [0][1] seems like a really serious attempt at bringing high-quality persistent data structures to C++.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"uNjxe8ShM-8\",\"title\":\"On The Turing Completeness of PowerPoint (SIGBOVIK)\",\"channel\":\"Tom Wildenhain\",\"summary\":\"A short SIGBOVIK 2017 presentation by Tom Wildenhain showing that PowerPoint animations are Turing complete, with a working Turing machine built in slides. The talk is delivered deadpan as a serious research result.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly names it as one of the funniest and best talks, a favorite example of absurd computation.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"turing completeness\",\"categories\":[\"Computability & complexity theory\",\"Satire & parody\"],\"tags\":[\"powerpoint\",\"turing machine\",\"sigbovik\",\"esoteric computing\",\"humor\"],\"quote\":\"Nerding out should always be hilarious like that!\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"w3_0x6oaDmI\",\"title\":\"Why Electronic Voting is a BAD Idea - Computerphile\",\"channel\":\"Computerphile\",\"summary\":\"Tom Scott explains for Computerphile why electronic voting is a bad idea, arguing that paper ballots are transparent, auditable and hard to tamper with at scale. He covers why moving voting to phones, tablets or computers undermines trust and verifiability.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the go-to short explainer HN commenters link whenever e-voting, voting machines or online voting come up.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"electronic voting security\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"US politics & elections\",\"Cryptography\"],\"tags\":[\"e-voting\",\"paper ballots\",\"election security\",\"tom scott\",\"computerphile\"],\"quote\":\"I love [this video]( ) from Tom Scott in which he explains quite well why e-voting is inherently bad.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"wfYbgdo8e-8\",\"title\":\"You Actually Share Your Brain With a Silent Stranger\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey explains split-brain patients, where severing the corpus callosum lets the two hemispheres act independently. He uses this to explore the idea of two minds in one head.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it constantly in consciousness and decision-making threads as a clear, entertaining explanation of split-brain.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"split-brain consciousness\",\"categories\":[\"Neuroscience & the brain\",\"Consciousness & cognition\"],\"tags\":[\"split brain\",\"corpus callosum\",\"consciousness\",\"neuroscience\",\"cgp grey\"],\"quote\":\"I recommend watching this video by GPGrey, one of the best educational channels on YouTube\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"wrwgIjBUYVc\",\"title\":\"A new way to visualize General Relativity\",\"channel\":\"ScienceClic English\",\"summary\":\"ScienceClic explains why the rubber sheet analogy for general relativity is misleading and offers a new visualization that includes the curvature of time and shows gravity as motion through curved spacetime.\",\"why_hn\":\"Called the best visualization of general relativity by many commenters, who say it cleared up long-held misunderstandings of gravity.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"general relativity visualization\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\",\"Cosmology & astrophysics\"],\"tags\":[\"general relativity\",\"spacetime\",\"curvature\",\"gravity\",\"rubber sheet analogy\"],\"quote\":\"hands down the best visualization of general relativity I've seen.\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"wupToqz1e2g\",\"title\":\"Carl Sagan - Pale Blue Dot\",\"channel\":\"carlsagandotcom\",\"summary\":\"Carl Sagan's narration of the Pale Blue Dot passage, reflecting on the Voyager photograph of Earth as a tiny speck in space. It is a short meditation on humanity's smallness and shared home.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters return to it for perspective, comfort and Sagan's soothing voice, and suggest watching it periodically.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cosmic perspective\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"Space exploration & spaceflight\",\"Philosophy & ethics\"],\"tags\":[\"carl sagan\",\"pale blue dot\",\"voyager\",\"perspective\",\"space\"],\"quote\":\"Puts everything in perspective, and makes everything feel insignificant\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"wxL4ElboiuA\",\"title\":\"The Art of Making a Nixie Tube\",\"channel\":\"Dalibor Farný\",\"summary\":\"Dalibor Farny documents the hand manufacture of a nixie tube, from glassblowing to evacuation, with text notes rather than narration. He revived the lost craft of nixie tube making.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it mesmerizing and a great look at how things are actually made by a dedicated craftsman.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nixie tube manufacturing\",\"categories\":[\"Manufacturing & industrial processes\",\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Woodworking, metalworking & workshop skills\"],\"tags\":[\"nixie tube\",\"glassblowing\",\"vacuum tubes\",\"craftsmanship\",\"dalibor farny\"],\"quote\":\"The video in the article is just amazing. You don't normally get to see the exact details of how things get made.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"xt1KNDmOYqA\",\"title\":\"Casey Muratori | Smart-Pointers, RAII, ZII? Becoming an N+2 programmer\",\"channel\":\"xDahl\",\"summary\":\"Casey Muratori argues against smart pointers and RAII, advocating grouped-element thinking, zero-is-initialization and stubs as better ways to structure memory and handle errors.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters in memory management and language design threads cite it as the best explanation of group lifetimes versus per-object ownership.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"memory management paradigms\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"raii\",\"smart pointers\",\"zero is initialization\",\"arena allocation\",\"casey muratori\"],\"quote\":\"The best video on this might be from Casey Muratori\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"yL_-1d9OSdk\",\"title\":\"Chicken chicken chicken\",\"channel\":\"Yoram Bauman\",\"summary\":\"Doug Zongker's deadpan conference presentation of his famously chicken-filled paper, given at the 2007 AAAS humor session. It satirizes academic talks while showing the pitfalls of bad presentation.\",\"why_hn\":\"Beloved as a humor masterpiece and often cited as required viewing on what not to do in an academic talk.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"academic satire\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"chicken\",\"satire\",\"academic talks\",\"humor\",\"presentations\"],\"quote\":\"The presentation is truly a humor masterpiece.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"zNVQfWC_evg\",\"title\":\"Quantum Fields: The Real Building Blocks of the Universe - with David Tong\",\"channel\":\"The Royal Institution\",\"summary\":\"David Tong, a Cambridge theoretical physicist, explains at the Royal Institution that the fundamental building blocks of nature are quantum fields rather than particles. He covers what we know about these fields and how they fit into the Standard Model.\",\"why_hn\":\"Called a favorite and a masterpiece by commenters as the clearest accessible explanation that there are only waves in fields, and what physics still doesn't know.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"quantum field theory\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum physics\",\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"quantum fields\",\"standard model\",\"particle physics\",\"david tong\",\"royal institution\"],\"quote\":\"is a masterpiece. Some very interesting facts, mostly about what we don't know.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"zb7Bs98KmnY\",\"title\":\"Longer-lasting light bulbs: it was complicated\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"Technology Connections examines why light bulbs have the lifespans they do, including the real history of the Phoebus cartel. He shows that bulb life trades off against brightness and efficiency rather than being simple planned obsolescence.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN's go-to link for correcting the lightbulb conspiracy myth, giving an engineer's view of the lifespan versus efficiency tradeoff.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"light bulb lifespan tradeoffs\",\"categories\":[\"Engineering & making (other)\",\"Repair, teardowns & right to repair\",\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\"],\"tags\":[\"incandescent bulbs\",\"phoebus cartel\",\"planned obsolescence\",\"efficiency\",\"lighting\"],\"quote\":\"highly recommended if you want an engineers perspective, with no 'great conspiracy' hype.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"zjkBMFhNj_g\",\"title\":\"[1hr Talk] Intro to Large Language Models\",\"channel\":\"Andrej Karpathy\",\"summary\":\"Andrej Karpathy gives a one-hour general-audience introduction to large language models, covering what they are, how they are trained, comparisons to operating systems, and security challenges such as jailbreaks.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the dense, approachable starting point for understanding LLMs and often cites it for specific claims about scaling and training.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"large language models\",\"categories\":[\"LLMs & generative AI\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\"],\"tags\":[\"llm\",\"pretraining\",\"scaling\",\"jailbreaks\",\"karpathy\",\"system 2\"],\"quote\":\"Start with his short intro to LLMs, only 1 hour long but is dense with valuable stuff\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"zwAD6dRSVyI\",\"title\":\"Thinking outside the 10-dimensional box\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"3Blue1Brown visualizes high-dimensional spheres to explain a surprising puzzle about how a sphere fits in a box as dimensions grow. It builds geometric intuition for counterintuitive properties of high-dimensional space.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the go-to intuitive visualization of high-dimensional geometry, relevant to machine learning, optimization and sphere packing.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"high-dimensional geometry\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"high dimensions\",\"spheres\",\"3blue1brown\",\"visualization\",\"sphere packing\"],\"quote\":\"There's a great 3blue1brown video that demonstrates this unintuitive result in a relatively intuitive way\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.5},{\"vid\":\"-6rWqJhDv7M\",\"title\":\"The Physics and Philosophy of Time - with Carlo Rovelli\",\"channel\":\"The Royal Institution\",\"summary\":\"Physicist Carlo Rovelli gives a public lecture at the Royal Institution on how our understanding of time has changed, from Boltzmann and Einstein to quantum theory and loop quantum gravity. He blends physics, philosophy and art to argue that time is stranger than everyday intuition suggests.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as a lucid, general-audience lecture that shows how wrong common conceptions of time are, often in threads on entropy and the arrow of time.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"physics of time\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\",\"Quantum physics\",\"Physics & space (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"time\",\"entropy\",\"loop quantum gravity\",\"arrow of time\",\"philosophy of physics\"],\"quote\":\"There is a great lecture »The Physics and Philosophy of Time« by Carlo Rovelli [1] aimed at the general public\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"-HFwok9SlQQ\",\"title\":\"Millennials Don't Exist! Adam Conover at Deep Shift\",\"channel\":\"Adam Conover\",\"summary\":\"Adam Conover's talk at a millennial marketing conference argues that generations are an unscientific, condescending idea and that millennials do not exist as a real category. The description notes that researcher Jean Twenge later said he misrepresented some of her work.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it when arguing that generational labels are marketing inventions used to belittle groups.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"generational stereotypes\",\"categories\":[\"Social issues & culture\",\"Product, marketing & sales\"],\"tags\":[\"millennials\",\"generations\",\"marketing\",\"pop sociology\",\"adam conover\"],\"quote\":\"Adam (from Adam Ruins Everything) does a really good talk at a marketing conference on why Millennials don't exist.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"-fny99f8amM\",\"title\":\"HyperNormalisation 2016\",\"channel\":\"crisalist\",\"summary\":\"Adam Curtis's 2016 BBC documentary HyperNormalisation, about 160 minutes long. It cuts together roughly forty years of politics, finance and technology to argue that people have retreated into a simplified fake world.\",\"why_hn\":\"Strongly and repeatedly recommended as a visually distinctive documentary and a guide to postmodern ideas on politics, truth and technology.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"political and cultural history\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"World history\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"adam curtis\",\"politics\",\"postmodernism\",\"media\",\"cyberspace\"],\"quote\":\"he's essentially cross cut the last 40 years into a 3 hour cliff notes guide to some of the darker corners of postmodern philosophy.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"0xS68sl2D70\",\"title\":\"Veritasium: What Everyone Gets Wrong About AI and Learning – Derek Muller Explains\",\"channel\":\"Perimeter Institute for Theoretical Physics\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium's Derek Muller gives a Perimeter Institute livestream on how AI might change teaching and learning. He draws on past technology shifts and cognitive science to argue that learning requires effortful repetition.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a balanced take on AI and education, with the point that learning takes work that no technology can do for you.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ai and education\",\"categories\":[\"Education & learning\",\"AI safety, AGI & impact\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"education\",\"learning science\",\"ai\",\"cognitive science\",\"veritasium\"],\"quote\":\"I think the best take on that I've seen so far was from Derek (Veritasium) in this recent talk\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"1TKSfAkWWN0\",\"title\":\"How Special Relativity Makes Magnets Work\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explains magnetism as a consequence of special relativity. Length contraction of moving charges makes a current in a wire attract or repel a nearby moving charge.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a short, clear explanation of why moving charges create magnetic fields.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"relativity and magnetism\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"special relativity\",\"magnetism\",\"length contraction\",\"electromagnetism\",\"veritasium\"],\"quote\":\"if you are curious why a moving charge creates a magnetic field watch this Veritasium video\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"2KSahAoOLdU\",\"title\":\"Apollo Guidance Computer Part 1: Restoring the computer that put man on the Moon\",\"channel\":\"CuriousMarc\",\"summary\":\"The first episode of CuriousMarc's series restoring a real Apollo Guidance Computer, led by AGC expert Mike Stewart. They set up a makeshift lab in a Houston hotel room to open up a rare machine owned by a private collector.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend the whole AGC restoration series as a deeply nerdy look at real Apollo-era hardware, including core rope memory.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"apollo guidance computer restoration\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Space exploration & spaceflight\"],\"tags\":[\"apollo\",\"agc\",\"retrocomputing\",\"core rope memory\",\"hardware restoration\"],\"quote\":\"My new favorite YouTuber, CuriousMarc, has a whole series of videos about restoring the Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC).\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"3kEpZWGgJks\",\"title\":\"Civil Forfeiture: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)\",\"channel\":\"LastWeekTonight\",\"summary\":\"John Oliver's Last Week Tonight segment on civil asset forfeiture, the process by which police can seize property they suspect is tied to crime. It explains how the property itself is put on trial and how departments profit.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the standard accessible explainer on civil forfeiture, linked in nearly every thread about police seizures.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"civil asset forfeiture\",\"categories\":[\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"civil forfeiture\",\"police\",\"john oliver\",\"law enforcement\",\"property seizure\"],\"quote\":\"John Oliver did a great job of explaining Civil Forfeiture a few years back\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"3wfNl2L0Gf8\",\"title\":\"Debate Noam Chomsky & Michel Foucault - On human nature [Subtitled]\",\"channel\":\"withDefiance\",\"summary\":\"The full 1971 Dutch television debate between Noam Chomsky and Michel Foucault on human nature, with subtitles. They discuss creativity, knowledge, power and politics.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a famous, revealing clash between two thinkers, though some note they largely talk past each other.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"human nature debate\",\"categories\":[\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Consciousness & cognition\",\"Politics & history (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"chomsky\",\"foucault\",\"philosophy\",\"creativity\",\"power\",\"1971\"],\"quote\":\"For anyone who hasn't watched the actual, full video of the debate, you should do so.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"4PaWFYm0kEw\",\"title\":\"Monktoberfest 2016: Bryan Cantrill - Oral Tradition in Software Engineering\",\"channel\":\"RedMonk Tech Events\",\"summary\":\"Bryan Cantrill's Monktoberfest 2016 talk on how software engineers pass knowledge down through oral tradition, from folk rhymes to programmer lore and stories like why C lacks a logical XOR. He argues for writing software with future generations in mind.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an entertaining, well-delivered talk full of stories that shows the value of historical context and long comments in code.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"software engineering culture\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Software (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"oral tradition\",\"bryan cantrill\",\"programming history\",\"code comments\",\"software folklore\"],\"quote\":\"really helped me appreciate that long comments in code can be a treasured part of a software space\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"5_KcQt0z-eE\",\"title\":\"Dani DaOrtiz at Fool US 2022 (the act that Penn and Teller didn’t even try to figure out.)\",\"channel\":\"dani daortiz\",\"summary\":\"A ten-minute card-magic act by Dani DaOrtiz performed on Penn & Teller: Fool Us in 2022. It is built on timing, misdirection and psychology, and the method defeated the show's hosts.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters repeatedly call it among the best magic performances ever, valuing it as acting and craft that stays impressive even once you know the method.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"card magic\",\"categories\":[\"Magic, puzzles & curiosities\"],\"tags\":[\"magic\",\"card tricks\",\"misdirection\",\"penn and teller\",\"performance\"],\"quote\":\"This is one of the greatest performances of all time\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"6UsHHOCH4q8\",\"title\":\"Tobacco: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)\",\"channel\":\"LastWeekTonight\",\"summary\":\"A Last Week Tonight segment on how tobacco companies sustain profits by pushing sales overseas. It covers suits against countries like Australia over plain packaging through trade-deal arbitration.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it in TPP and trade-deal threads as a clear example of ISDS being used by corporations against governments.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"tobacco industry and isds\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\"],\"tags\":[\"tobacco\",\"isds\",\"tpp\",\"plain packaging\",\"john oliver\"],\"quote\":\"I really encourage you to check it out. It's about 18min long, but well worth it\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"7jiPeIFXb6U\",\"title\":\"I don't like notebooks.- Joel Grus (Allen Institute for Artificial Intelligence)\",\"channel\":\"O'Reilly\",\"summary\":\"Joel Grus's JupyterCon talk explaining why he dislikes Jupyter notebooks. He covers hidden state, trouble with reproducibility and testing, and how notebooks frustrate teaching. He shows his preferred workflow and what Jupyter could change.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the go-to funny, irreverent critique of notebooks, linked whenever HN debates notebooks versus testable code.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"jupyter notebooks\",\"categories\":[\"Data science & statistics\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"jupyter\",\"python\",\"data science\",\"reproducibility\",\"software engineering\"],\"quote\":\"The parent article links this great talk that goes into more detail than the parent post and is much easier to understand\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"7vfqkvwW2fs\",\"title\":\"The Marvel Symphonic Universe\",\"channel\":\"Every Frame a Painting\",\"summary\":\"An Every Frame a Painting video essay asking why Marvel films lack memorable musical themes. It blames temp tracks and directors scoring to filler music.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an insightful explanation of why modern film scores sound alike, with lessons beyond Marvel.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"film music and temp tracks\",\"categories\":[\"Film analysis & criticism\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\"],\"tags\":[\"marvel\",\"film scores\",\"temp music\",\"video essay\",\"composers\"],\"quote\":\"You don't have to have seen any Marvel movies, the insights are universal.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"7zrx-b2sLUs\",\"title\":\"Ground Source / Geothermal Heat Pumps and Other Info\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"A Technology Connections video on how ground-source (geothermal) heat pumps work and why they hold efficiency in winter. It also covers related heat pump information, and the description notes air-source heat pumps have since improved.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as part of an excellent, approachable series explaining residential heat pumps.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"heat pumps\",\"categories\":[\"Power grid, heating & buildings\",\"Renewables, batteries & storage\"],\"tags\":[\"geothermal\",\"ground source\",\"hvac\",\"efficiency\",\"home heating\"],\"quote\":\"He made several excellent videos explaining residential heat pumps\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"8X7Ng75e5gQ\",\"title\":\"Vladimir Pozner: How the United States Created Vladimir Putin\",\"channel\":\"Yale University\",\"summary\":\"Russian-American journalist Vladimir Pozner lectures at Yale on how US foreign policy toward Russia after the Soviet collapse shaped Putin, then takes audience questions on election interference, the Skripal poisoning and media independence.\",\"why_hn\":\"During the 2022 Ukraine war threads, HN commenters recommended it as a non-Western perspective on Russia-West relations and Putin's motives.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"us-russia relations\",\"categories\":[\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"putin\",\"ukraine\",\"nato expansion\",\"russian politics\",\"vladimir pozner\"],\"quote\":\"I strongly recommend to watch this talk\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"A8eCGOqgvH4\",\"title\":\"C++ and Beyond 2012: Herb Sutter - atomic Weapons 1 of 2\",\"channel\":\"alexsmail\",\"summary\":\"Herb Sutter's deep C++ and Beyond 2012 talk on atomics and the C++ memory model, covering why programmers chasing top performance need to understand hardware ordering. This is part one of two.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the classic go-to for learning atomics and memory ordering, recommended over books.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"c++ memory model\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"atomics\",\"memory ordering\",\"concurrency\",\"c++\",\"herb sutter\"],\"quote\":\"which are extremely good\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"BCmz-fgp24E\",\"title\":\"What People Get Wrong About Dense Urban Living\",\"channel\":\"Oh The Urbanity!\",\"summary\":\"Oh The Urbanity! argues that most city dwellers live in dense residential neighbourhoods outside downtown, which can be calm and peaceful. It draws on older, denser parts of Toronto and Montreal.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN users repeatedly post it to rebut the idea that urban living means Manhattan or Hong Kong apartment blocks.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"urban density\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\"],\"tags\":[\"urbanism\",\"density\",\"toronto\",\"montreal\",\"walkable neighbourhoods\",\"housing\"],\"quote\":\"Fifteen minutes pedalling in one direction is downtown Amsterdam, fifteen minutes in the other is farm land.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"Dp-mQ3HxgDE\",\"title\":\"The Future of Mathematics?\",\"channel\":\"Microsoft Research\",\"summary\":\"Mathematician Kevin Buzzard explains why he believes the Lean theorem prover will shape the future of mathematics, and describes digitizing his university curriculum in Lean with undergraduates, plus the cultural challenges.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the clearest mathematician's perspective on formal proof assistants and why mathematics should be formalized.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"formal mathematics with lean\",\"categories\":[\"Logic & formal methods\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"lean\",\"theorem proving\",\"kevin buzzard\",\"formalization\",\"proof assistants\"],\"quote\":\"For a perspective of a mathematician that came to evangelize theorem provers, I recommend Kevin Buzzard's MS 2019-09 presentation [0] about LEAN.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"F9cO3-MLHOM\",\"title\":\"All-In Summit: Bill Gurley presents 2,851 Miles\",\"channel\":\"All-In Podcast\",\"summary\":\"Bill Gurley's talk at the All-In Summit 2023, titled 2,851 Miles after the distance between Washington and San Francisco. He describes how incumbents use lobbying and regulation to entrench themselves, followed by a conversation with the hosts.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters repeatedly recommend it as the clearest explanation of regulatory capture, and cite it in threads on AI regulation, GDPR and telecom.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"regulatory capture\",\"categories\":[\"Tech industry & business models\",\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Investing & personal finance\"],\"tags\":[\"regulation\",\"lobbying\",\"incumbents\",\"ai policy\",\"venture capital\"],\"quote\":\"I strongly encourage watching Bill Gurley's talk from the All-In Summit\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"FJJTYQYB1JQ\",\"title\":\"Sorting Algorithms: Speed Is Found In The Minds of People - Andrei Alexandrescu - CppCon 2019\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Andrei Alexandrescu's CppCon 2019 talk revisits sorting, showing how branchless tricks, hybrid strategies and a cost metric can speed up std::sort-style algorithms on modern hardware.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an entertaining talk that starts simple and goes deep on practical performance optimization.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"sorting performance\",\"categories\":[\"Algorithms & data structures\",\"Software performance & optimization\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"sorting algorithms\",\"c++\",\"branch misprediction\",\"optimization\",\"cppcon\"],\"quote\":\"At first he is just showing simple stuff, but stay with it, he goes deep.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"G1r55efei5c\",\"title\":\"!!Con 2016 - How I Code and Use a Computer at 1,000 WPM!! By Sina Bahram\",\"channel\":\"Confreaks\",\"summary\":\"A short !!Con 2016 talk by blind developer Sina Bahram on how he uses a computer and writes code with a screen reader at about 1,000 words per minute. He plays how common interfaces sound and challenges the audience to go a day using only the keyboard.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters repeatedly share it because it is an eye-opening look at screen-reader use, and because at about 6:30 it addresses how inaccessible Hacker News itself is.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"accessibility and screen readers\",\"categories\":[\"UX & UI design\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"blind developers\",\"screen reader\",\"accessibility\",\"keyboard navigation\",\"bangbangcon\"],\"quote\":\"There's an amazing 10min !!Con 2016 talk by Sina Bahram\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"GOGru_4z1Vc\",\"title\":\"Kurt Vonnegut, Shape of Stories (subtitulos castellano)\",\"channel\":\"Eva CollinsAlonso\",\"summary\":\"Kurt Vonnegut's 2004 Case College lecture, with Spanish subtitles, in which he sketches the shapes of stories on a blackboard. He plots story arcs as good and bad fortune against time.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently recommended as a delightful, influential way to think about story structure.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"story structure\",\"categories\":[\"Literature & writing craft\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"kurt vonnegut\",\"storytelling\",\"narrative arcs\",\"writing\"],\"quote\":\"Vonnegut is really special. I always like coming back to his Shape of Stories talks\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"G_eYTctGZw8\",\"title\":\"\\\"Production Prolog\\\" by Michael Hendricks\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Michael Hendricks of PriceCharting shares tricks and pain points from running Prolog in production for 18 months at Strange Loop. It argues Prolog is a productive modern language beyond logic puzzles, and includes a demo of changing code mid-execution.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the go-to answer to whether Prolog is used for real-world work, praised as a survey of its uses, advantages and drawbacks.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"prolog in production\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"prolog\",\"logic programming\",\"strange loop\",\"swi-prolog\",\"web development\"],\"quote\":\"gives an excellent high-level survey of the uses, advantages, and drawbacks of Prolog\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"HI09kat_GeI\",\"title\":\"The Biggest Ideas in the Universe: Introduction\",\"channel\":\"Sean Carroll\",\"summary\":\"Sean Carroll introduces his video series on the biggest ideas in physics, such as symmetry, space and matter, as informal discussions with follow-up Q&A videos. This is the short opening video of the series.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend the series as engaging, substantial physics with just enough math for non-specialists.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"modern physics\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\",\"Quantum physics\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"sean carroll\",\"physics\",\"quantum mechanics\",\"popular science\",\"lecture series\"],\"quote\":\"Just enough math to be interesting but nothing requiring a math degree.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"HQYsFshbkYw\",\"title\":\"Creating a Doom-style 3D engine in C\",\"channel\":\"Bisqwit\",\"summary\":\"Bisqwit builds a Doom or Duke Nukem 3D style software-rendered 3D engine in C from scratch, supporting non-Euclidean geometry. It covers vector rotation and portal rendering with sped-up, tool-assisted coding.\",\"why_hn\":\"A favorite on Ask HN lists of programming YouTube channels, praised for mind-blowing systems and graphics programming and a watchable coding style.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software 3d rendering\",\"categories\":[\"Computer graphics & rendering\",\"Game programming & engines\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"3d engine\",\"portal rendering\",\"c programming\",\"doom\",\"graphics\"],\"quote\":\"My favourite programming YouTuber by a long stretch.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"HUEvRyemKSg\",\"title\":\"28c3: The coming war on general computation\",\"channel\":\"28c3\",\"summary\":\"Cory Doctorow's 28c3 keynote argues that the copyright wars were just a skirmish and that the real fight will be a war on the general-purpose computer. He explains why locked-down devices, DRM and surveillance tools all depend on removing general computation.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it constantly as a prescient talk, cited whenever threads cover DRM, locked-down devices, app verification or restrictions on running your own software.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"digital rights and general-purpose computing\",\"categories\":[\"Hacker culture & free software\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\",\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\"],\"tags\":[\"drm\",\"copyright\",\"cory doctorow\",\"locked-down devices\",\"ccc\"],\"quote\":\"which I very strongly recommend that everybody watch\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"J1q4Ir2J8P8\",\"title\":\"DEF CON 22 - Zoz - Don't Fuck It Up!\",\"channel\":\"DEFCONConference\",\"summary\":\"Zoz's DEF CON 22 talk on operational security in the age of pervasive surveillance. He shows how hard it is to avoid mistakes even for people who should know better.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the usual cite for why good OPSEC is so hard, including entry and exit point correlation and digital exhaust.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"operational security\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\"],\"tags\":[\"opsec\",\"surveillance\",\"nsa\",\"tor\",\"privacy\"],\"quote\":\"According to Zoz in his excellent DEFCON 22 talk\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"JEpsBg0AO6o\",\"title\":\"Paxos lecture (Raft user study)\",\"channel\":\"Diego Ongaro\",\"summary\":\"John Ousterhout's slides-and-voiceover lecture on Paxos, made for the Raft user study comparing how students learn Raft and Paxos. It is a full introduction to the Paxos consensus algorithm.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a clear, lucid way to learn Paxos and Multi-Paxos, ideally alongside the companion Raft video.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"paxos consensus\",\"categories\":[\"Distributed systems\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"paxos\",\"raft\",\"distributed consensus\",\"multi-paxos\",\"distributed systems\"],\"quote\":\"The 2 videos do an excellent job of explaining both the Paxos and Raft algorithms and the key differences between them.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"JqNg819PiZY\",\"title\":\"Demystifying the Higgs Boson with Leonard Susskind\",\"channel\":\"Stanford\",\"summary\":\"Stanford physicist Leonard Susskind explains what the Higgs mechanism is and what it means to give mass to particles. He also covers what is at stake for physics and cosmology.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as an approachable, math-light route to understanding mass and the Higgs field.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"higgs boson\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum physics\"],\"tags\":[\"leonard susskind\",\"higgs mechanism\",\"particle physics\",\"mass\",\"quantum fields\"],\"quote\":\"I highly highly recommend this talk by Leonard Susskind\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"M6vpFV6Wkl4\",\"title\":\"Why We Get Fat: And What to Do About It | Gary Taubes | Talks at Google\",\"channel\":\"Talks at Google\",\"summary\":\"Gary Taubes presents the argument of his book Why We Get Fat to Googlers in 2011. He claims the type of calories matters more than the count, challenges calories-in-calories-out thinking and criticizes the low-fat dietary consensus.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in diet and weight-loss threads as the accessible case for the carbohydrate-insulin view of obesity.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"obesity and diet\",\"categories\":[\"Nutrition & diet science\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"gary taubes\",\"low-carb\",\"obesity\",\"nutrition\",\"calories\"],\"quote\":\"Highly recommend you watch this amazing lecture by Garry Taubes at Google\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"MBRqu0YOH14\",\"title\":\"Optimistic Nihilism\",\"channel\":\"Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell\",\"summary\":\"Kurzgesagt's short animated take on optimistic nihilism. It argues the universe has no inherent meaning, so we are free to create our own and enjoy life.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN users recommend it as a comforting, clear introduction to nihilism for anxiety, mid-life doubts and feeling behind in life.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"optimistic nihilism\",\"categories\":[\"Philosophy & ethics\"],\"tags\":[\"philosophy\",\"meaning of life\",\"nihilism\",\"stoicism\",\"kurzgesagt\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend it, as they explain these concepts very well in a short 6min video.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"MvgN5gCuLac\",\"title\":\"George Carlin Talks About \\\"Stuff\\\"\",\"channel\":\"CappyNJ\",\"summary\":\"George Carlin's classic stand-up routine about 'stuff' and how much of life is spent accumulating and storing it, from Comic Relief 1986. It satirizes American consumerism and possessions.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN keeps linking it in threads on clutter, storage units, real estate and consumerism as a perfectly apt comic take.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"consumerism comedy\",\"categories\":[\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\"],\"tags\":[\"george carlin\",\"stand-up\",\"consumerism\",\"possessions\",\"comic relief\"],\"quote\":\"George Carlin put it quite eloquently\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"N1CBDTgGtOU\",\"title\":\"VisiData Lightning Demo at PyCascades 2018\",\"channel\":\"Saul Pwanson\",\"summary\":\"A five-minute lightning demo of VisiData, a terminal tool for exploring and manipulating data. It uses two months of NYC 311 complaint data to drill down, plot points on a virtual map and save selected rows.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the fastest way to understand what VisiData can do, since seeing it work is clearer than reading about it.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"terminal data exploration\",\"categories\":[\"Data science & statistics\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"visidata\",\"tui\",\"csv\",\"data analysis\",\"python\"],\"quote\":\"visdata is pretty mind-blowing...at least, that's how i felt when i saw the demo the first time\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"NAsM30MAHLg\",\"title\":\"(1/4) Intro/History: Introducing a 100-year-old mechanical computer\",\"channel\":\"engineerguy\",\"summary\":\"The first of four short videos from Bill Hammack (engineerguy) introducing and giving the history of a 100-year-old mechanical computer, Michelson's harmonic analyzer. The series goes on to explain Fourier synthesis, analysis and operation of the machine.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters recommend the series whenever Fourier analysis or analog computing comes up, praising Hammack's clear explanations and the short episodes.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"mechanical fourier analysis\",\"categories\":[\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"Information theory & signal processing\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"fourier series\",\"harmonic analyzer\",\"analog computer\",\"bill hammack\",\"michelson\"],\"quote\":\"There is a series of four fantastic videos\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"ON0A1dsQOV0\",\"title\":\"Gtk to Qt - a strange journey [linux.conf.au 2014]\",\"channel\":\"Konstantin Bläsi\",\"summary\":\"Dirk Hohndel explains why the Subsurface dive log project migrated from Gtk to Qt, the obstacles encountered, and advice for other applications frustrated with Gtk. It is a linux.conf.au 2014 talk.\",\"why_hn\":\"Go-to reference in GTK versus Qt debates, valued for a candid account from experienced developers who tried both toolkits.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"gtk vs qt toolkit migration\",\"categories\":[\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"qt\",\"gtk\",\"subsurface\",\"gui toolkits\",\"cross-platform\",\"linux\"],\"quote\":\"One of the best discussions on the subject, made by old hackers, but new guys to GUI programming, who tried both\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"P-2P3MSZrBM\",\"title\":\"Joscha Bach: Artificial Consciousness and the Nature of Reality | Lex Fridman Podcast #101\",\"channel\":\"Lex Fridman\",\"summary\":\"Lex Fridman has a three-hour conversation with Joscha Bach about artificial consciousness, the workings of the mind, intelligence and the possibly simulated nature of reality.\",\"why_hn\":\"Praised as a long but rewarding conversation, with commenters especially liking Bach's emphasis on modelling as a skill and his bridge between mind and computation.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"artificial consciousness\",\"categories\":[\"Consciousness & cognition\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\"],\"tags\":[\"joscha bach\",\"consciousness\",\"artificial intelligence\",\"simulation\",\"philosophy of mind\"],\"quote\":\"If watching this isn't worth anyone's time, I'm not sure what would be.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"PFDu9oVAE-g\",\"title\":\"Eigenvectors and eigenvalues | Chapter 14, Essence of linear algebra\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"3Blue1Brown's chapter on eigenvectors and eigenvalues in the Essence of Linear Algebra series. It builds a visual intuition for what they mean, why an eigenbasis is useful, and ends with a puzzle.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly names it the best, most intuitive explanation of eigenvalues, and a gateway to the whole linear algebra series.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"linear algebra\",\"categories\":[\"Pure mathematics\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"eigenvectors\",\"eigenvalues\",\"3blue1brown\",\"visual math\",\"eigenbasis\"],\"quote\":\"By far the best explanation of eigenvalues is found on 3blue1brown\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"RNFesa01llk\",\"title\":\"The Hyperloop: BUSTED!\",\"channel\":\"Thunderf00t\",\"summary\":\"Thunderf00t, a physicist, critically assesses the Hyperloop concept, covering the vacuum, pressure, safety, and cost problems of high-speed tube travel. He argues the media accepted the claims uncritically.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly cites it as the best skeptical engineering analysis of why Hyperloop is impractical.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"hyperloop feasibility\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"hyperloop\",\"vacuum trains\",\"engineering critique\",\"thunderf00t\",\"transportation\"],\"quote\":\"His videos on the hyperloop are pretty comprehensive and succinct about their reasoning.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"RuOvn4UqznU\",\"title\":\"Fasting For Survival Lecture by Dr Pradip Jamnadas\",\"channel\":\"The Galen Foundation\",\"summary\":\"Cardiologist Dr. Pradip Jamnadas lectures on the rationale for dietary restriction and fasting, covering insulin resistance, glucose intolerance, and low-carb eating over about 81 minutes.\",\"why_hn\":\"Several HN commenters call it one of the best explanations of fasting and insulin and recommend it in diet and weight loss threads.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"fasting and insulin resistance\",\"categories\":[\"Nutrition & diet science\",\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"fasting\",\"insulin resistance\",\"low carb\",\"diabetes\",\"metabolic health\"],\"quote\":\"This is the best video I have found to explain the incredible changes and benefits of multi-da\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"SqFu5O-oPmU\",\"title\":\"Jonathan Blow: Video Games and the Human Condition\",\"channel\":\"rubbermuck\",\"summary\":\"Jonathan Blow's 2010 Rice University talk argues that game designers refine their techniques in ways that can manipulate players, as with A/B-tested social games. He discusses the ethical responsibility of designers and what games can offer beyond addiction.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a thoughtful critique of addictive game design and the designer's role in building ethical games.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"game design ethics\",\"categories\":[\"Game design\"],\"tags\":[\"jonathan blow\",\"game addiction\",\"farmville\",\"game design\",\"monetization\"],\"quote\":\"Jonathan Blow has a great presentation on the mechanisms of video game addiction and the role of the game designer in building ethically responsible games [0]. I highly recommend it\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"US8QG9I1XW0\",\"title\":\"Functional architecture - The pits of success - Mark Seemann\",\"channel\":\"NDC Conferences\",\"summary\":\"Mark Seemann argues that functional programming naturally yields many of the best practices object-oriented developers learn painfully. He shows how functional architecture leads to a pit of success, with a pure core and impure shell.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a practical demonstration of how functional thinking improves design without going off the deep end.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"functional architecture\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"mark seemann\",\"pit of success\",\"functional programming\",\"f#\",\"software architecture\"],\"quote\":\"Here is a great talk demonstrating that by following a simple functional approach, your code can naturally fall into a “pit of success\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"Uo08uS904Rg\",\"title\":\"Why Is It So Hard to Do Something That Should Be Easy? (Wall of Awful pt. 1)\",\"channel\":\"How to ADHD\",\"summary\":\"A short How to ADHD explainer, featuring Brendan Mahan, on why simple tasks can feel impossible for people with ADHD. It introduces the 'wall of awful', a pile of past failures, shame and anxiety that builds up in front of a task and blocks starting it.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters use it as the go-to name and explanation for task paralysis and procrastination, and say it helped them see why they avoid tasks.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"adhd task paralysis\",\"categories\":[\"Mental health & neurodiversity\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"adhd\",\"procrastination\",\"wall of awful\",\"executive function\",\"motivation\"],\"quote\":\"It totally changed how I approached my procrastination by simply asking myself \\\"why\\\" I was avoiding a task.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"ZTC_RxWN_xo\",\"title\":\"Secret History of Silicon Valley\",\"channel\":\"Computer History Museum\",\"summary\":\"Steve Blank's Computer History Museum lecture argues that Silicon Valley's roots lie in WWII radar and electronic warfare work, Stanford's Fred Terman and Cold War military funding rather than the semiconductor alone.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it a favorite introduction to Valley history and cite it whenever government and military funding of tech comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of silicon valley\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Tech company stories & documentaries\",\"Military history & warfare\"],\"tags\":[\"silicon valley\",\"steve blank\",\"radar\",\"stanford\",\"military funding\",\"history\"],\"quote\":\"my favorite introduction to the valleys history was Steve Blanks The Secret History of Silicon Valley. It's well worth the hour\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"ZWQ0591PAxM\",\"title\":\"How we fit an NES game into 40 Kilobytes\",\"channel\":\"Morphcat Games\",\"summary\":\"Morphcat Games explains how they fit Micro Mages, a full-featured NES game, into 40 kilobytes. It covers compression of sprites and level layouts and other space-saving tricks.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it whenever discussion turns to how old games were so small, as a clear tour of 8-bit optimization and creative work under hardware limits.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"nes game optimization\",\"categories\":[\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Indie game development\",\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\"],\"tags\":[\"nes\",\"compression\",\"retro games\",\"micro mages\",\"size optimization\"],\"quote\":\"Here is my favorite video about 8-bit era optimisations. Makes you appreciate games from this era even more.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"aB8zcAttP1E\",\"title\":\"David Fravor: UFOs, Aliens, Fighter Jets, and Aerospace Engineering | Lex Fridman Podcast #122\",\"channel\":\"Lex Fridman\",\"summary\":\"Lex Fridman interviews Navy pilot David Fravor, a primary witness of the 2004 Tic Tac UFO encounter, about the sighting, military aviation and aerospace engineering. The conversation runs close to four hours.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters send people here for a detailed first-hand account from the pilot, and some say it changed their view on the sightings.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ufo sightings\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\",\"Military history & warfare\"],\"tags\":[\"tic tac\",\"navy pilot\",\"uap\",\"fravor\",\"lex fridman\"],\"quote\":\"this is a great interview interesting in its own right, even without the UFO part\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"bHIhgxav9LY\",\"title\":\"The Biggest Misconception About Electricity\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium argues that electric energy is carried by fields around the wires rather than by electrons moving through them, using a thought experiment with a large circuit.\",\"why_hn\":\"Often recommended as an eye-opening field-based explanation of electricity, though it was also controversial and criticized as pedantic.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"electricity and electromagnetic fields\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"electricity\",\"poynting vector\",\"fields\",\"circuits\",\"physics\"],\"quote\":\"It’s an eye opening alternative explanation\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"bSkpMdDe4g4\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2017: Matt Godbolt “What Has My Compiler Done for Me Lately? Unbolting the Compiler's Lid”\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Matt Godbolt's CppCon 2017 talk shows what modern compilers do with your C++ code, using Compiler Explorer to inspect generated assembly. It covers surprising optimizations and how the tool works.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an entertaining, mind-blowing look at compiler cleverness from a great speaker.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"compiler optimizations\",\"categories\":[\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"compiler explorer\",\"c++\",\"assembly\",\"godbolt\",\"cppcon\"],\"quote\":\"Both are great presentations going into details on some of the crazy optimizations compilers will do.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"cCOL7MC4Pl0\",\"title\":\"Jake Archibald on the web browser event loop, setTimeout, micro tasks, requestAnimationFrame, ...\",\"channel\":\"JSConf\",\"summary\":\"Jake Archibald's JSConf.Asia 2018 talk \\\"In the Loop\\\" explains the browser event loop, covering tasks, microtasks, requestAnimationFrame, and how rendering interleaves with JavaScript. It uses visual walkthroughs to show why code runs in surprising orders and why setTimeout fixes are unreliable.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the best primer on the event loop, praising its entertaining, easy-to-grasp visual explanations.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"browser event loop\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"javascript\",\"event loop\",\"microtasks\",\"requestanimationframe\",\"settimeout\",\"web browsers\"],\"quote\":\"actually making it both entertaining and easily grokable!\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"db-7J5OaSag\",\"title\":\"RiftSketch: Live coding in VR with the Oculus Rift, Firefox WebVR, JavaScript and Three.js\",\"channel\":\"Brian Peiris\",\"summary\":\"Brian Peiris demonstrates RiftSketch, a web app where you write JavaScript with Three.js while wearing an Oculus Rift and watch the world around you change live. It runs on Firefox builds with WebVR.\",\"why_hn\":\"Repeatedly cited as an inspiring demo of what programming in VR could look like, which led some commenters to buy headsets.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"live coding in vr\",\"categories\":[\"VR, AR & spatial computing\",\"Web development\",\"Creative coding & generative art\"],\"tags\":[\"oculus rift\",\"webvr\",\"three.js\",\"live coding\",\"javascript\"],\"quote\":\"this was an impressive demo of someone coding their environment in the Rift.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"f7CW7S0zxv4\",\"title\":\"Dr Shashi Tharoor MP - Britain Does Owe Reparations\",\"channel\":\"OxfordUnion\",\"summary\":\"Shashi Tharoor's Oxford Union speech arguing that Britain owes reparations to its former colonies, focused on the effects of British rule in India. It lists economic and political damage done by colonialism.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters link it repeatedly in colonial history threads as an eloquent, fact-dense short speech.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"colonial reparations\",\"categories\":[\"World history\",\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\"],\"tags\":[\"shashi tharoor\",\"british empire\",\"india\",\"oxford union\",\"colonialism\"],\"quote\":\"This talk has many facts not even commonly known to Indians.\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"fhOHn9TClXY\",\"title\":\"Joe Armstrong & Alan 