ip\"],\"tags\":[\"alan kay\",\"xerox parc\",\"innovation\",\"research\",\"startup school\"],\"quote\":\"Alan Kay gave a couple lectures at Stanford on \\\"How to Invent the Future\\\" and went over all the stuff that came out of there.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"j2Mu8qfVb5I\",\"title\":\"Dr. Robert Zubrin with a brilliant answer to \\\"Why Should We Go To Mars?\\\"\",\"channel\":\"Jamin Gray\",\"summary\":\"A short clip of Dr. Robert Zubrin, author of The Case for Mars, answering why humanity should go to Mars. He argues that societies grow by challenging themselves and that exploration inspires young people.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters repeatedly link it as the best concise answer to the question of why go to Mars when problems remain on Earth.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"mars exploration\",\"categories\":[\"Space exploration & spaceflight\"],\"tags\":[\"robert zubrin\",\"mars\",\"space exploration\",\"case for mars\",\"motivation\"],\"quote\":\"Robert Zubrin has what I consider a great, comprehensive answer to why we should go to Mars.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"kYfNvmF0Bqw\",\"title\":\"Steve Jobs Secrets of Life\",\"channel\":\"Silicon Valley Historical Association\",\"summary\":\"A short excerpt from a 1994 interview of Steve Jobs in which he says everything around us was made up by people no smarter than you, and that you can change and shape the world.\",\"why_hn\":\"Quoted in threads on motivation, agency and regret as an encouraging reminder that the world can be changed.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"agency and changing the world\",\"categories\":[\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"steve jobs\",\"motivation\",\"entrepreneurship\",\"life advice\",\"1994 interview\"],\"quote\":\"the world is something you can change, not merely participate in.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"lw6TaiXzHAE\",\"title\":\"OOPSLA Conference: Growing a Language\",\"channel\":\"Computer History Museum\",\"summary\":\"Guy Steele's classic 1998 OOPSLA keynote on how a programming language must grow with its user community. He delivers it in a playful style, starting with only monosyllabic words and defining new terms as he goes.\",\"why_hn\":\"Treated as a must-watch for language designers, and cited whenever extensibility and language size are debated.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming language design\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"guy steele\",\"language growth\",\"extensibility\",\"oopsla\",\"java\"],\"quote\":\"In case anyone hasn't seen this most excellent talk on this topic, I highly recommend\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"oAHbLRjF0vo\",\"title\":\"The Fastest Way to Board a Plane Feels Like the Slowest\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey compares airplane boarding methods, based on an arXiv paper, and shows why the intuitive back-to-front approach is slow while random or outside-in boarding does better.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the default link in any thread about boarding or deplaning order, praised as an excellent, clear treatment.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"airplane boarding efficiency\",\"categories\":[\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\",\"Algorithms & data structures\"],\"tags\":[\"boarding methods\",\"queueing\",\"airlines\",\"simulation\",\"cgp grey\"],\"quote\":\"Highly recommend this video of plane boarding methods by CGP Grey\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"oHf1vD5_b5I\",\"title\":\"DEF CON 22 - Deviant Ollam & Howard Payne - Elevator Hacking - From the Pit to the Penthouse\",\"channel\":\"DEFCONConference\",\"summary\":\"Deviant Ollam and Howard Payne give an in-depth DEF CON 22 explanation of how elevators work, from the pit to the penthouse. It covers their control modes and how pen testers use elevators to bypass building security.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN brings it up in any thread about elevators or physical security as a fascinating, entertaining deep dive.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"elevator hacking\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"elevators\",\"physical security\",\"def con\",\"pen testing\",\"deviant ollam\"],\"quote\":\"If you liked the original post, you will probably LOVE this DEFCON talk about elevator hacking\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"oHlpmxLTxpw\",\"title\":\"Why We Won't Raise Our Kids in Suburbia\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes argues that car-dependent suburbs rob children of independence, contrasting North American suburbia with Dutch cities where kids walk and bike on their own. It is the creator's remake of an earlier video on the topic.\",\"why_hn\":\"The standard link in threads about child autonomy, helicopter parenting and car-centric planning.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"urban planning and children\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Social issues & culture\"],\"tags\":[\"suburbs\",\"netherlands\",\"car dependence\",\"child independence\",\"cycling\"],\"quote\":\"Not Just Bikes has a really good video on why suburbia (usually single family housing) is often worse for children than denser environments.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"o_4EX4dPppA\",\"title\":\"\\\"The Hard Parts of Open Source\\\" by Evan Czaplicki\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Evan Czaplicki, creator of Elm, discusses the hard parts of running an open source project: online community conflict, why its patterns arise, and a cultural history of open source. He offers ideas for more civil, productive communication.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an insightful look at the people side of open source maintenance, especially in Elm and maintainer-burnout threads.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"open source communities\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Hacker culture & free software\"],\"tags\":[\"open source\",\"elm\",\"maintainers\",\"online communities\",\"strange loop\"],\"quote\":\"Regardless of whether you agree with his approach to developing a programming language, it is a talk worth listening to. Very insightful.\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"p6K4iIMlouI\",\"title\":\"7 Habits For Effective Text Editing 2.0\",\"channel\":\"GoogleTalksArchive\",\"summary\":\"Vim creator Bram Moolenaar's 2007 Google Tech Talk on habits for efficient text editing. He explains how to spot repetition and inefficiency, then use editor features to fix it.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a lasting philosophy for improving your editing process incrementally, and it was widely remembered after Moolenaar's death.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"text editing efficiency\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"vim\",\"bram moolenaar\",\"editor habits\",\"productivity\",\"automation\"],\"quote\":\"There is a wonderful presentation called '7 Habits For Effective Text Editing 2.0' [1] explaining this philosophy.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"qI_g07C_Q5I\",\"title\":\"Introduction to NoSQL • Martin Fowler • GOTO 2012\",\"channel\":\"GOTO Conferences\",\"summary\":\"Martin Fowler's GOTO 2012 introduction to NoSQL databases: where they came from, their data models, how consistency thinking differs, and when to use them. He argues they won't make relational databases obsolete and introduces polyglot persistence.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as a balanced, beginner-friendly overview of SQL versus NoSQL, and some teachers assign it to students.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"nosql databases\",\"categories\":[\"Databases & storage\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"nosql\",\"martin fowler\",\"polyglot persistence\",\"consistency\",\"data models\"],\"quote\":\"one of the rare videos I've required students in my database classes to watch.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"rFejpH_tAHM\",\"title\":\"dotGo 2015 - Rob Pike - Simplicity is Complicated\",\"channel\":\"dotconferences\",\"summary\":\"Rob Pike's dotGo 2015 talk argues that Go only seems simple, and that its simplicity hides real complexity that is part of the design. He explains the thinking behind Go's restraint in features.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it to explain why Go deliberately resists syntactic sugar and new features.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"go language design\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"go\",\"rob pike\",\"simplicity\",\"language design\",\"dotgo\"],\"quote\":\"It's an active choice of the language to not allow many syntactic sugars that make the code have a much higher learning curve.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"rOzrJjdZDRQ\",\"title\":\"[HOPE X] Elevator Hacking: From the Pit to the Penthouse\",\"channel\":\"themarimbaincident\",\"summary\":\"A HOPE X talk giving an in-depth explanation of how elevators work, from their history in hacker culture to using their control systems to bypass building security. It covers features and abilities of elevators that most people never see.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it one of the most entertaining and informative con talks, and a good window into elevators from a hacker's view.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"elevator systems\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Hacker culture & free software\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"elevators\",\"hacker culture\",\"physical security\",\"hope x\",\"building systems\"],\"quote\":\"This version is the best, with all details, no secrets withheld.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"sG9UMMq2dz4\",\"title\":\"The Patent Scam Intro\",\"channel\":\"Austin Meyer\",\"summary\":\"Austin Meyer, developer of the X-Plane flight simulator, explains how he was sued by a patent troll over a trivial claim about an app's license server. He walks through how the shell-company litigation works and why ignoring the suit makes things worse.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters pass it around as an eye-opening first-hand account of patent trolling and the Texas courts that favor it.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"patent trolls\",\"categories\":[\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Startups & entrepreneurship\"],\"tags\":[\"patent trolls\",\"software patents\",\"litigation\",\"x-plane\",\"eastern district of texas\"],\"quote\":\"This video from Austin Meyer (creator of X-Plane) is pretty eye-opening\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"tISy7EJQPzI\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2017: Titus Winters “C++ as a \\\"Live at Head\\\" Language”\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Titus Winters' CppCon 2017 talk argues for C++ and Google's Abseil to be maintained as a live at head language, relying on testing and automated tooling rather than never changing or promising no compatibility. It covers Hyrum's law and the limits of semantic versioning.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended whenever versioning, semver, monorepos and backward compatibility are debated, for its nuanced treatment of dependencies.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"software versioning and compatibility\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"live at head\",\"semver\",\"hyrum's law\",\"monorepo\",\"abseil\",\"c++\"],\"quote\":\"a much more nuanced look at dependencies & versioning\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"u4ZoJKF_VuA\",\"title\":\"Start with why -- how great leaders inspire action | Simon Sinek | TEDxPugetSound\",\"channel\":\"TEDx Talks\",\"summary\":\"Simon Sinek's TEDx talk arguing that great leaders and companies inspire action by communicating why they do something before how or what. He uses Apple as his main example.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a quick way to get the core of 'Start with Why' for pitches and communication, though some note small companies are not Apple.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"leadership and communication\",\"categories\":[\"Management, leadership & organizations\",\"Product, marketing & sales\"],\"tags\":[\"simon sinek\",\"start with why\",\"leadership\",\"pitching\",\"apple\"],\"quote\":\"A much faster way to understand the central message of \\\"Start with Why\\\" would be to watch the TEDx talk\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"vq2nnJ4g6N0\",\"title\":\"Tensorflow and deep learning - without a PhD by Martin Görner\",\"channel\":\"Devoxx\",\"summary\":\"Martin Gorner's Devoxx talk introduces deep learning with TensorFlow, walking through neural networks for MNIST, how to choose an architecture and how to diagnose networks that fail to train.\",\"why_hn\":\"Repeatedly called an excellent, practical introduction that covers a lot in two hours and explains issues academic textbooks skip.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"deep learning with tensorflow\",\"categories\":[\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"Computer vision, speech & NLP\"],\"tags\":[\"tensorflow\",\"neural networks\",\"mnist\",\"deep learning\",\"convolutional networks\"],\"quote\":\"Tensorflow and deep learning - without a PhD\\\" is really good, and covers a lot of material in a single 2hr talk.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"wdgULBpRoXk\",\"title\":\"How does a USB keyboard work?\",\"channel\":\"Ben Eater\",\"summary\":\"Ben Eater explains how a USB keyboard communicates, covering the electrical interface, bit and packet encoding, packet contents, and a comparison with PS/2.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a clear, comprehensive explanation of USB signaling and keyboard polling.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"usb protocol\",\"categories\":[\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\"],\"tags\":[\"usb\",\"keyboards\",\"ps/2\",\"polling\",\"ben eater\",\"hardware\"],\"quote\":\"Fantastic breakdown if you have 30 minutes\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"wqn3gR1WTcA\",\"title\":\"Data Brokers: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)\",\"channel\":\"LastWeekTonight\",\"summary\":\"John Oliver's Last Week Tonight segment explains how data brokers collect and sell personal information, including a stunt in which his team bought data to identify members of Congress.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an accessible overview of the data broker industry in privacy discussions.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"data brokers and privacy\",\"categories\":[\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"data brokers\",\"privacy\",\"john oliver\",\"surveillance\",\"personal data\"],\"quote\":\"I strongly recommend this episode of Last Week Tonight\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"xP5-iIeKXE8\",\"title\":\"Life in life\",\"channel\":\"Phillip Bradbury\",\"summary\":\"A two-minute video of Conway's Game of Life running inside Conway's Game of Life, built from OTCA metapixels and Golly. It zooms smoothly between scales.\",\"why_hn\":\"The canonical visual for nested simulation, Turing completeness and self-reference; HN readers call it mind-blowing and reach for it constantly.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cellular automata\",\"categories\":[\"Computability & complexity theory\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"game of life\",\"otca metapixel\",\"golly\",\"turing completeness\",\"nested simulation\"],\"quote\":\"here's a famous and really clear sub-2 minutes representation of the scales involved\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"yNzQ_sLGIuA\",\"title\":\"The Epidemic of Fake Disease\",\"channel\":\"Medlife Crisis\",\"summary\":\"A Medlife Crisis video by cardiologist Rohin Francis on overdiagnosis and over-screening. It explains how detecting more disease does not necessarily mean saving more lives, and covers harms such as unnecessary treatment.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to in threads about cancer screening, PSA tests, full-body MRIs and consumer diagnostics as an explanation of why more testing can hurt.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"overdiagnosis and screening\",\"categories\":[\"Medicine & infectious disease\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"overdiagnosis\",\"cancer screening\",\"medicine\",\"over-testing\",\"public health\"],\"quote\":\"This video does a good job also of covering the topic of over-testing/over-treating potential cancer\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"ybcvlxivscw\",\"title\":\"What Languages Sound Like To Foreigners\",\"channel\":\"Sara Forsberg\",\"summary\":\"Finnish YouTuber Saara improvises gibberish that mimics how various languages sound to people who do not speak them. The sentences are mostly made up, and the clip went viral.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a favorite example of astonishing accent mimicry and fake-language gibberish that sounds real.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"accent mimicry\",\"categories\":[\"Language & linguistics\",\"Internet culture & memes\"],\"tags\":[\"fake languages\",\"accents\",\"gibberish\",\"viral video\",\"linguistics\"],\"quote\":\"Internet personality Saara turned speaking fake languages into a career with this amazing little clip\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"ynbgMKclWWc\",\"title\":\"Democracy at Work: Curing Capitalism | Richard Wolff | Talks at Google\",\"channel\":\"Talks at Google\",\"summary\":\"Economist Richard Wolff's Talks at Google lecture on his book Democracy at Work. He critiques capitalism and argues for worker-run cooperatives as a path to a more democratic economy.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters share it as an accessible case for worker cooperatives and workplace democracy as a model for a modern left.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"worker cooperatives\",\"categories\":[\"Labor, work & inequality\",\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\"],\"tags\":[\"richard wolff\",\"democracy at work\",\"worker coops\",\"critique of capitalism\",\"socialism\"],\"quote\":\"I recently came across an interesting talk by Richard Wolff at Google [1]\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"z6yL0_sDnX0\",\"title\":\"James Burke Connections, Ep. 4 \\\"Faith in Numbers\\\"\",\"channel\":\"DRS_Education\",\"summary\":\"Episode 4 of James Burke's Connections series, tracing how commercialism, climate change and the Black Death shaped the move to the Renaissance. It follows threads through waterpower, the printing press, the Jacquard loom and the Hollerith punch card toward modern computing.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters strongly recommend it for its account of how the Jacquard loom and punch cards led to computers.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of computing origins\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"World history\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"james burke\",\"connections\",\"jacquard loom\",\"punch cards\",\"history of technology\"],\"quote\":\"Every computer engineer, computer scientist and software developer should learn about the Jacquard loom\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":7.0},{\"vid\":\"-VsmF9m_Nt8\",\"title\":\"Adriano Celentano - Prisencolinensinainciusol\",\"channel\":\"Le Pietre Rotolanti\",\"summary\":\"A 1972 song by Adriano Celentano whose lyrics are nonsense syllables designed to sound like American English to non-English speakers. It is a rock and rap-flavored novelty track with a video.\",\"why_hn\":\"It comes up whenever threads touch on fake languages, accents, or gibberish that sounds plausible, and commenters like sharing it with people who haven't heard it.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"fake english gibberish song\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Language & linguistics\"],\"tags\":[\"celentano\",\"gibberish\",\"accents\",\"italian music\",\"1972\"],\"quote\":\"For anyone who hasn't seen it before, here's a song by an Italian composer made to sound like American English\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"0SARbwvhupQ\",\"title\":\"Google I/O 2009 - The Myth of the Genius Programmer\",\"channel\":\"Google for Developers\",\"summary\":\"Brian Fitzpatrick and Ben Collins-Sussman argue that the 'genius programmer' ideal harms collaboration. They advocate trading ego for growth, sharing work early, and understanding how tools shape social behavior.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN regulars recommend it as a career-shaping talk on code review, humility and collaborative development.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"collaborative software development\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Management, leadership & organizations\"],\"tags\":[\"code review\",\"ego\",\"bus factor\",\"team culture\",\"open source\"],\"quote\":\"One of the best talks about code reviews and similiar things\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"1-dUkyn_fZA\",\"title\":\"Emacs + org-mode + python in reproducible research; SciPy 2013 Presentation\",\"channel\":\"Enthought\",\"summary\":\"John Kitchin shows how Emacs org-mode with Python lets researchers combine narrative, math, figures and executable code in one document. It exports to LaTeX, HTML and PDF, and he used it for papers and e-books.\",\"why_hn\":\"Repeatedly recommended as the canonical demo of org-mode for literate programming and reproducible research.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"reproducible research\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Data science & statistics\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"org-mode\",\"emacs\",\"python\",\"literate programming\",\"scipy\"],\"quote\":\"on Emacs + org-mode + python in reproducible research is worth a watch\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"1MGjFKs9bnU\",\"title\":\"FarewellEtaoinShrdlu\",\"channel\":\"Drinks and Dice\",\"summary\":\"A documentary on the last day the New York Times was printed with linotype machines in 1978, following the process from the newsroom to the press. It marks the move to computerized typesetting.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently recommended as an absorbing look at hot-metal typesetting and the end of the linotype era.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"linotype typesetting\",\"categories\":[\"Graphic design & typography\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"linotype\",\"new york times\",\"printing history\",\"typesetting\",\"newspapers\"],\"quote\":\"the final day the New York Times was printed using their linotype machines.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"1qSTcxt2t74\",\"title\":\"Captain Disillusion: World's Greatest Blenderer - Live at the Blender Conference 2018\",\"channel\":\"Captain Disillusion\",\"summary\":\"Captain Disillusion's comedic talk at the 2018 Blender Conference recounts how he uses Blender as a production tool for his VFX-debunking web series. It is part case study and part love letter to the Blender community.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an entertaining and inspiring Blender talk, and often links it in threads about learning Blender.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"blender\",\"categories\":[\"Animation & visual effects\",\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\"],\"tags\":[\"blender\",\"vfx\",\"3d animation\",\"conference talk\",\"captain disillusion\"],\"quote\":\"this is a must watch for inspiration and laughs\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"2AXv49dDQJw\",\"title\":\"Watch electricity hit a fork in the road at half a billion frames per second\",\"channel\":\"AlphaPhoenix\",\"summary\":\"AlphaPhoenix uses a custom high-speed measurement setup to watch voltage waves travel down wires and split at a fork. It answers how electricity knows where to go and how quickly that process happens.\",\"why_hn\":\"Praised as the clearest visual explanation of transmission lines, impedance and signal propagation, with several commenters saying it made it all click.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"electricity and signal propagation\",\"categories\":[\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Fundamental physics\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"transmission lines\",\"impedance\",\"electromagnetism\",\"high-speed measurement\",\"circuits\"],\"quote\":\"by far the best explanations of electricity that I've encountered in any format\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"3Fx5Q8xGU8k\",\"title\":\"Competition is for Losers with Peter Thiel (How to Start a Startup 2014: 5)\",\"channel\":\"Y Combinator\",\"summary\":\"Peter Thiel's lecture from the How to Start a Startup course on business strategy and monopoly theory, arguing that competition destroys profits and that startups should aim to build monopolies. It covers the ideas of Zero to One.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a serious, influential talk on market size and monopoly, though some commenters criticize its philosophy as endorsing anti-competitive goals.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"startup monopoly strategy\",\"categories\":[\"Startups & entrepreneurship\",\"Tech industry & business models\"],\"tags\":[\"peter thiel\",\"zero to one\",\"competition\",\"monopoly\",\"startup strategy\",\"y combinator\"],\"quote\":\"this talk from YC's Startup School 2014 covering the ideas from Zero to One is excellent\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"3VQ382QG-y4\",\"title\":\"Lambda Calculus - Fundamentals of Lambda Calculus & Functional Programming in JavaScript\",\"channel\":\"Fullstack Academy\",\"summary\":\"Gabriel Lebec's Fullstack Academy talk introduces lambda calculus syntax, the history of mathematical logic, and the essence of functional programming using JavaScript. It also covers combinators, currying and Church encodings.\",\"why_hn\":\"Repeatedly recommended as one of the most approachable introductions to lambda calculus and combinators.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"lambda calculus\",\"categories\":[\"Computability & complexity theory\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"lambda calculus\",\"combinators\",\"functional programming\",\"javascript\",\"currying\"],\"quote\":\"they are the most approachable videos on the topic I have ever seen.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"3ZTGwcHQfLY\",\"title\":\"I Tried To Make Something In America (The Smarter Scrubber Experiment) - Smarter Every Day 308\",\"channel\":\"SmarterEveryDay\",\"summary\":\"Destin Sandlin of Smarter Every Day tries to manufacture a grill scrubber entirely in the United States. Along the way he runs into missing supply chains, tooling and institutional manufacturing knowledge.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it constantly in US manufacturing debates as a vivid demonstration of how much domestic industrial capability has been lost.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"us manufacturing decline\",\"categories\":[\"Manufacturing & industrial processes\",\"Business & economics (other)\",\"Labor, work & inequality\"],\"tags\":[\"manufacturing\",\"supply chain\",\"injection molding\",\"made in usa\",\"smarter every day\"],\"quote\":\"Here's a great video by Smarter Every Day that came out a few months ago\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"4bM3Gut1hIk\",\"title\":\"4 2 1 Christopher Domas   The future of RE Dynamic Binary Visualization\",\"channel\":\"Adrian Crenshaw\",\"summary\":\"Christopher Domas presents his Cantor Dust tool at Derbycon 2012, a 3D visual approach to reverse engineering binaries by visualizing their structure dynamically.\",\"why_hn\":\"Regarded as one of the coolest talks ever, with a futuristic binary visualization demo that has inspired later tools.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"binary visualization\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Data visualization & information design\"],\"tags\":[\"reverse engineering\",\"cantor dust\",\"christopher domas\",\"binary analysis\",\"data visualization\",\"security\"],\"quote\":\"really blew my mind when I saw it.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"4d0EGIt1SPc\",\"title\":\"The REAL Possibility of Mapping Alien Planets!\",\"channel\":\"PBS Space Time\",\"summary\":\"PBS Space Time explains the proposal to use the Sun's gravity as a giant lens, sending a train of small light-sail spacecraft to the focal point roughly 550 AU out. It covers how such a telescope could image exoplanets in surprising detail and the engineering challenges involved.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the go-to explanation whenever a thread touches on imaging exoplanets or solar gravitational lens telescopes.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\"