 how a differential works fully understandable in clear, simple language.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"differential gears\",\"categories\":[\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\"],\"tags\":[\"differential\",\"gears\",\"automotive\",\"mechanical engineering\",\"vintage training film\"],\"quote\":\"after watching this ancient Chevrolet training video from the 1930's I feel like I completely understand how diffs work\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"KXuZi9aeGTw\",\"title\":\"User-level threads....... with threads. - Paul Turner - Google\",\"channel\":\"Linux Plumbers Conference\",\"summary\":\"Paul Turner of Google presents kernel interfaces for user-level scheduling of real threads, aiming at a uniform synchronous programming model across C++, Java, Go and Python. It introduces the switchto-style system calls behind Google's fibers.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as the key explanation of cooperative, user-scheduled kernel threads and the prior art behind later async and coroutine debates.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"user-level threading\",\"categories\":[\"Operating systems & kernels\",\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\"],\"tags\":[\"linux kernel\",\"fibers\",\"scheduling\",\"concurrency\",\"switchto\",\"google\"],\"quote\":\"Paul Turner explained this really well at this year's Linux Plumbers Conference. The whole talk is fantastic\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"KZjFVdU8VLI\",\"title\":\"Interactive programming Flappy Bird in ClojureScript\",\"channel\":\"Bruce Hauman\",\"summary\":\"Bruce Hauman live-codes Flappy Bird in ClojureScript in about six minutes, using figwheel to hot-reload changes into the running game. The demo shows game behavior being changed interactively without restarting.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the canonical demo HN people cite for live interactive development and hot reloading, as proof the workflow is real and not smoke and mirrors.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"interactive programming\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Game programming & engines\",\"Web development\"],\"tags\":[\"clojurescript\",\"figwheel\",\"hot reloading\",\"live coding\",\"game development\"],\"quote\":\"I still marvel at Bruce Hauman's demo\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"KtQ9nt2ZeGM\",\"title\":\"You are being misled about renewable energy technology.\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"A long Technology Connections video arguing that public understanding of renewable energy is distorted. It covers solar economics, land use compared with ethanol corn, and battery myths such as cobalt.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as an up-to-date, fact-focused case for solar and batteries that readers can cite on land use and costs.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"renewable energy\",\"categories\":[\"Renewables, batteries & storage\",\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\"],\"tags\":[\"solar\",\"batteries\",\"ethanol land use\",\"energy myths\",\"technology connections\"],\"quote\":\"Alec Watson’s recent, and excellent, Technology Connections YouTube piece on renewable energy.\",\"recommenders\":12,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"LMbI6sk-62E\",\"title\":\"Orbital Rings\",\"channel\":\"Isaac Arthur\",\"summary\":\"Isaac Arthur explains the orbital ring, a megastructure launch-assist system that could move bulk cargo cheaply to orbit and beyond. He compares it with space elevators and discusses power transmission.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters offer it as the likely future of cheap access to space, feasible without exotic materials.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"orbital rings\",\"categories\":[\"Space exploration & spaceflight\",\"Civil engineering & construction\"],\"tags\":[\"megastructures\",\"launch costs\",\"space elevator\",\"isaac arthur\",\"space infrastructure\"],\"quote\":\"we can build an orbital ring around Earth with today's technology, and it'd be much better than space elevators.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"MBnnXbOM5S4\",\"title\":\"The more general uncertainty principle, regarding Fourier transforms\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"3Blue1Brown shows how the uncertainty principle arises from Fourier transforms. A wave packet cannot be narrow in both position and frequency, so the principle is a property of waves rather than of measurement.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the clearest visual explanation that uncertainty is intrinsic to waves, separate from measurement disturbance.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"fourier uncertainty principle\",\"categories\":[\"Information theory & signal processing\",\"Quantum physics\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"fourier transform\",\"uncertainty principle\",\"wave packets\",\"quantum mechanics\",\"signal processing\",\"3blue1brown\"],\"quote\":\"The clearest, most intuitive explanation of the Fourier Uncertainty Principle that I have come across is by Grant Sanderson of 3Blue1Brown.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"ML4tw_UzqZE\",\"title\":\"Building The World's First Computer - Greeks, Clocks and Rockets - Antikythera Mechanism Episode 1\",\"channel\":\"Clickspring\",\"summary\":\"The first episode of Clickspring's series on reproducing the Antikythera Mechanism by hand, laying out the plan and the history. The series follows a machinist building the ancient Greek device with traditional tools and methods.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends the series for its extraordinary craftsmanship and its credible demonstration of how the mechanism could have been made.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"antikythera mechanism reconstruction\",\"categories\":[\"Woodworking, metalworking & workshop skills\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"antikythera mechanism\",\"clickspring\",\"clockmaking\",\"machining\",\"ancient technology\"],\"quote\":\"Highly recommend for anyone who is fascinated by the care that goes into handmade mechanical devices.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"MZvmYaFkNJI\",\"title\":\"Build a real-time Twitter clone in 15 minutes with LiveView and Phoenix 1.5\",\"channel\":\"Chris McCord\",\"summary\":\"Phoenix creator Chris McCord builds a real-time Twitter-style app live with Phoenix LiveView 1.5 in about 15 minutes. It shows interactive, real-time updates without writing JavaScript.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points people to it as the demo that shows why LiveView and Elixir are worth a look.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"phoenix liveview\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Web development\"],\"tags\":[\"elixir\",\"phoenix\",\"liveview\",\"real-time web\",\"live coding\"],\"quote\":\"the demo completely blew my mind\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"NdppYYfQJgg\",\"title\":\"38C3 - From Silicon to Sovereignty: How Advanced Chips are Redefining Global Dominance\",\"channel\":\"media.ccc.de\",\"summary\":\"A 38C3 talk on how advanced chips are made, centered on EUV lithography and the global supply chain behind it. It covers technical challenges from raw materials to finished chips and the geopolitical weight of semiconductors.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN users recommend it as an excellent, accessible overview of how modern chips are produced and why EUV matters.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"euv lithography\",\"categories\":[\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\",\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\"],\"tags\":[\"semiconductors\",\"asml\",\"chip manufacturing\",\"supply chain\",\"ccc\",\"geopolitics\"],\"quote\":\"I can highly recommend this to everybody who is \\\"somehow in tech or IT\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"Nq2HkAYbG5o\",\"title\":\"2016 - On The Shoulders Of Giants - Damian Conway\",\"channel\":\"The Perl and Raku Conference - Greenville, SC 2026\",\"summary\":\"Damian Conway's hour-long keynote at the Perl conference, On the Shoulders of Giants, showing off Perl 6 (Raku) features with history, algorithms and a quantum math operator built with grammars. It is delivered with comic flair.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an entertaining, technically masterful showcase of what Perl 6 can do.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"perl 6 language features\",\"categories\":[\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"perl 6\",\"raku\",\"damian conway\",\"grammars\",\"programming languages\"],\"quote\":\"the whole thing is like watching a great movie\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"PorfLSr3DDI\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2015: Greg Law \\\" Give me 15 minutes & I'll change your view of GDB\\\"\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Greg Law's 15-minute CppCon 2015 lightning talk shows what GDB can do beyond basic use, including its text user interface and reverse debugging.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a fast, brilliant introduction to GDB's TUI mode and lesser-known features, useful for anyone who has only used IDE debuggers.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"gdb debugging\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"gdb\",\"debugging\",\"tui mode\",\"reverse debugging\",\"c++\"],\"quote\":\"this brilliant short talk (15min) about gdb's TUI.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"PqPGOhXoprU\",\"title\":\"Jack Szostak (Harvard/HHMI) Part 1: The Origin of Cellular Life on Earth\",\"channel\":\"Science Communication Lab\",\"summary\":\"Jack Szostak's lecture covers how cellular life could have arisen from chemistry on the early Earth, starting from the odds of life elsewhere in the galaxy. It is part of a series on protocells and the RNA world.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a clear, authoritative lecture series on plausible origin-of-life theories.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"origin of life\",\"categories\":[\"Biology & biotechnology\",\"Evolution & genetics\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"abiogenesis\",\"rna world\",\"protocells\",\"jack szostak\",\"biochemistry\"],\"quote\":\"Jack Szostak does a pretty good job of outlining how life might have arisen.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"Q1lL-hXO27Q\",\"title\":\"Feynman: Magnets (and Why?)  FUN TO IMAGINE  4/ higher quality version!\",\"channel\":\"Christopher Sykes\",\"summary\":\"A 1983 BBC interview in which Richard Feynman is asked how magnets work and explains why 'why' questions are hard to answer in everyday terms. He reflects on what counts as an explanation and the limits of analogy.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it as a model of intellectual honesty and good explanation, often linking it in discussions of why-questions and science communication.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"scientific explanation\",\"categories\":[\"Scientific method & science communication\",\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"feynman\",\"magnets\",\"physics\",\"pedagogy\",\"epistemology\"],\"quote\":\"his riff for 8 minutes on this is just a joy to watch.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"Q2aEzeMDHMA\",\"title\":\"JPEG DCT, Discrete Cosine Transform (JPEG Pt2)- Computerphile\",\"channel\":\"Computerphile\",\"summary\":\"Mike Pound explains the discrete cosine transform and how it underpins JPEG compression. It is part two of Computerphile's JPEG series.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an easy, clear intro to the DCT that finally made it click for several commenters.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"image compression\",\"categories\":[\"Information theory & signal processing\",\"Computer vision, speech & NLP\"],\"tags\":[\"jpeg\",\"discrete cosine transform\",\"compression\",\"mike pound\",\"computerphile\"],\"quote\":\"I finally understood DCT after watching\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"RSTNhvDGbYI\",\"title\":\"Old-fashioned rice cookers are extremely clever\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"Technology Connections explains how old-fashioned non-electronic rice cookers work, using a magnet and spring switch tripped by the Curie point. It shows the thermodynamics and magnetism behind a simple appliance.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a delightful breakdown of the cleverness in dumb appliances and an entry point to the channel.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"rice cooker engineering\",\"categories\":[\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"rice cooker\",\"curie point\",\"magnetism\",\"thermostat\",\"appliances\"],\"quote\":\"I had no idea they were so clever, combining thermodynamics and the physics of magnetism.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"RqQhWitJ1As\",\"title\":\"BlueHat IL 2019 - Andrew \\\"bunnie\\\" Huang - Supply Chain Security: \\\"If I were a Nation State...”\",\"channel\":\"Microsoft Israel R&D Center\",\"summary\":\"Andrew \\\"bunnie\\\" Huang examines how realistic hardware supply chain attacks are, from rogue insiders to nation states, in the wake of the Bloomberg Businessweek report. He explains how undocumented components could be hidden on boards and how hard they are to spot.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people point to it as the sober, technically grounded picture of hardware supply chain security, and an eye-opening one.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"hardware supply chain security\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Electronics & circuit design\"],\"tags\":[\"bunnie huang\",\"supply chain\",\"hardware tampering\",\"bloomberg supermicro\",\"security\"],\"quote\":\"This is an eye opening talk in the subject\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"S0qjK3TWZE8\",\"title\":\"golden balls. the weirdest split or steal ever!\",\"channel\":\"spinout3\",\"summary\":\"A Golden Balls episode in which one contestant announces he will steal, leading to an unexpected outcome in the split-or-steal prisoner's dilemma.\",\"why_hn\":\"The canonical real-world example in game theory discussions of manipulating a prisoner's dilemma.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"prisoner's dilemma\",\"categories\":[\"Probability & game theory\",\"Games (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"game theory\",\"golden balls\",\"split or steal\",\"nash equilibrium\",\"game show\"],\"quote\":\"This is a classic IMO.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"U4FNBMZsqrY\",\"title\":\"DOOM: Behind the Music\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"Mick Gordon's GDC 2017 session on composing the Doom (2016) soundtrack. He details his compositional process, production techniques and the experimental instrument and synth design behind the music.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN raves about it as one of the best conference talks, showing a master's creative process and sound design.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"game music production\",\"categories\":[\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Game design\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"doom\",\"mick gordon\",\"sound design\",\"synthesizers\",\"gdc\"],\"quote\":\"One of my favorite conference talks ever\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.5}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"UJ7Qc3bsxjI\",\"title\":\"Great Earthquakes of the Pacific Northwest\",\"channel\":\"Central Washington University\",\"summary\":\"Geologist Nick Zentner of Central Washington University gives an hour-long public lecture on the great earthquakes of the Pacific Northwest, including the Cascadia subduction zone and the evidence for past megaquakes. It is part of his Downtown Geology Lecture Series, recorded in Ellensburg in 2016.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters offer it as a dry but fascinating, hype-free introduction to Cascadia earthquake risk and the evidence for past events.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cascadia earthquakes\",\"categories\":[\"Geology & earth science\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"cascadia\",\"megathrust\",\"pacific northwest\",\"geology\",\"paleoseismology\",\"nick zentner\"],\"quote\":\"It's better than most TV if you've got the time.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"VZQoAKJPbh8\",\"title\":\"In Search of Tomorrow\",\"channel\":\"Berkeley Institute for Data Science (BIDS)\",\"summary\":\"Chris Granger, creator of Light Table and the Eve language, gives a Berkeley data science lecture on what computation and programming might look like in ten years. He covers the path that led to Eve and ends with demos.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN keeps pointing to it as a thoughtful vision of programming for non-programmers and as background on Eve's motivations.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"future of programming\",\"categories\":[\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"Programming language design\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"eve\",\"chris granger\",\"end-user programming\",\"light table\",\"live programming\"],\"quote\":\"very good talk about the background of eve\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"X-iSQQgOd1A\",\"title\":\"Coding Adventure: Ant and Slime Simulations\",\"channel\":\"Sebastian Lague\",\"summary\":\"Sebastian Lague explores an ant-inspired algorithm and simulates ant and slime mold behavior, using compute shaders. Source code for both simulations is available.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a beautiful example of what simple rules and compute shaders can produce, and many commenters were inspired by it.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"agent-based simulation\",\"categories\":[\"Creative coding & generative art\",\"Computer graphics & rendering\"],\"tags\":[\"slime mold\",\"ant simulation\",\"compute shaders\",\"unity\",\"emergent behavior\"],\"quote\":\"reminded how beautiful coding can be\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"XVbuk3jizGM\",\"title\":\"A New Car UI: How touch screen controls in cars should work\",\"channel\":\"Matthaeus Krenn\",\"summary\":\"Matthaeus Krenn presents a concept for car touchscreen UI where the screen controls only one thing at a time, designed to suit driving. A tablet prototype accompanies the short demo.\",\"why_hn\":\"Repeatedly cited in car touchscreen debates as an elegant, sensible alternative to distracting automotive UIs.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"car touchscreen interface design\",\"categories\":[\"UX & UI design\",\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\"],\"tags\":[\"automotive ui\",\"touchscreen\",\"interaction design\",\"prototype\",\"driver distraction\"],\"quote\":\"I feel compelled to share a video, now 9 years old, that shows how touch screens could be accommodated with sensible design in cars.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"Y9FZ4igNxNA\",\"title\":\"Fourier Optics used for Optical Pattern Recognition\",\"channel\":\"Huygens Optics\",\"summary\":\"Huygens Optics explains the Fourier transform and shows how a lens performs one optically. A DLP projector is used as a Fourier filter to do simple pattern recognition on lithographic character patterns.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as one of the best explanations of Fourier transforms and a clear demo of analog optical computing.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"fourier optics\",\"categories\":[\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Information theory & signal processing\",\"Computer vision, speech & NLP\"],\"tags\":[\"fourier transform\",\"optical computing\",\"pattern recognition\",\"dlp projector\",\"lenses\"],\"quote\":\"The video happens to have one of the best explanations of Fourier transforms I've yet encountered.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"aSEQfqNYNAc\",\"title\":\"Rich Hickey rants about HttpServletRequest in 1080p: Death By Specificity from Clojure Made Simple\",\"channel\":\"Fred Overflow\",\"summary\":\"A five-minute excerpt of Rich Hickey's Clojure Made Simple talk, with new slides, ranting about HttpServletRequest and specificity. He argues for using generic maps instead of many specific classes and getters.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as the classic rant on maps versus objects in discussions of data modeling and OOP.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"data-oriented design\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Functional programming\",\"Databases & storage\"],\"tags\":[\"rich hickey\",\"clojure\",\"maps\",\"oop\",\"data modeling\"],\"quote\":\"A classic rant by Rich Hickey\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"agOdP2Bmieg\",\"title\":\"Bret Victor - The Humane Representation of Thought\",\"channel\":\"Colin McDonnell\",\"summary\":\"Bret Victor's closing keynote at UIST and SPLASH 2014 argues that our tools for thinking ignore most of human sensory and motor capability. He sketches representations that engage more modalities beyond screens and keyboards.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as a foundational Bret Victor talk that reshaped how people think about software and interfaces, often cited alongside Dynamicland.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"human-computer interaction\",\"categories\":[\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"UX & UI design\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"bret victor\",\"direct manipulation\",\"dynamicland\",\"representation\",\"interface design\"],\"quote\":\"have had a huge impact on the way I think about software\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"bJczLlwp-d8\",\"title\":\"Timothy Snyder: The Making of Modern Ukraine. Class 1: Ukrainian Questions Posed by Russian Invasion\",\"channel\":\"YaleCourses\",\"summary\":\"The first class of Timothy Snyder's Yale course on the making of modern Ukraine. It introduces the course and asks what it means for a nation to exist in light of the Russian invasion.\",\"why_hn\":\"Strongly recommended as an accessible, well-taught history of Ukraine and a counter to propaganda narratives.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"ukrainian history\",\"categories\":[\"University lectures & courses\",\"World history\",\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\"],\"tags\":[\"ukraine\",\"timothy snyder\",\"yale\",\"russia\",\"eastern europe\"],\"quote\":\"gives you invaluable understanding about much more than just Ukraine.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"d4EgbgTm0Bg\",\"title\":\"Visualizing the 4d numbers Quaternions\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"3Blue1Brown builds visual intuition for quaternions, showing how to think about this four-dimensional number system using stereographic projection and rotations in 3D space. It is part one of a two-part series with an interactive companion site.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as the best visual explanation of quaternions and a good way to visualize higher dimensions.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"quaternions\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"quaternions\",\"4d\",\"3d rotation\",\"stereographic projection\",\"visualization\"],\"quote\":\"He does a great job of explaining quaternions though visual intuition.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"dW8Cy6WrO94\",\"title\":\"Pythagoras' theorem (a) | Math History | NJ Wildberger\",\"channel\":\"Insights into Mathematics\",\"summary\":\"The first lecture of N J Wildberger's History of Mathematics course, starting with Pythagoras' theorem and following Stillwell's text. It emphasizes the flow of ideas over proofs.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an easy-to-follow math history course that gives perspective on the subject.\",\"content_type\":\"course\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of mathematics\",\"categories\":[\"Pure mathematics\",\"University lectures & courses\",\"Math & theory (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"wildberger\",\"pythagoras\",\"math history\",\"lectures\",\"geometry\"],\"quote\":\"Highly recommend his History of Mathematics lectures on YouTube\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"eMmNy11Mn7g\",\"title\":\"How Valve is Profiting from Steam's Back-Door Casinos\",\"channel\":\"People Make Games\",\"summary\":\"People Make Games investigates the unregulated CS:GO skin gambling economy and Valve's role in it. It includes interviews with people who lost large sums and examines why Valve does not intervene.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as well-researched reporting on skin gambling, especially its effect on minors, in threads about gambling and Valve.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"video game gambling\",\"categories\":[\"Games (other)\",\"Gaming culture & analysis\",\"Law, policing & justice\"],\"tags\":[\"steam\",\"cs:go\",\"skin gambling\",\"valve\",\"regulation\"],\"quote\":\"More people need to see interviews such as those in the People Make Games video\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"fXW-QjBsruE\",\"title\":\"Do Chairs Exist?\",\"channel\":\"Vsauce\",\"summary\":\"A Vsauce video exploring whether chairs exist, through metaphysics, mereology and the philosophy of categorizing objects. It runs about 38 minutes and cites academic work on ontology.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an entertaining tour of how we define and categorize things, and it is cited when concept boundaries come up.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ontology of objects\",\"categories\":[\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"metaphysics\",\"mereology\",\"philosophy\",\"vsauce\",\"categorization\"],\"quote\":\"it's basically an entertaining, whirlwind tour of the philosophy of categorizing and labeling things\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"gbYXBJOFgeI\",\"title\":\"Civil War General-Purpose Computing | Cory Doctorow | Talks at Google\",\"channel\":\"Talks at Google\",\"summary\":\"Cory Doctorow's Talks at Google lecture on the coming civil war over general-purpose computing. He argues that locked-down devices, DRM, and surveillance threaten user control of computers.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is HN's go-to reference whenever device lockdown, DRM, or restrictions on installing software come up, with commenters noting how prescient it was.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"general-purpose computing freedom\",\"categories\":[\"Hacker culture & free software\",\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\"],\"tags\":[\"cory doctorow\",\"drm\",\"digital rights\",\"device lockdown\",\"surveillance\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend reading up on what Cory has to say on DRM and the war on general purpose computing. It may change your mind.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"helScS3coAE\",\"title\":\"Robust-first computing: Demon Horde Sort (full version)\",\"channel\":\"Dave Ackley\",\"summary\":\"Dave Ackley demonstrates the Demon Horde Sort, an inherently robust sorting machine running in his Movable Feast Machine simulator. This is the uncut version with all details and side thoughts.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN readers recommend it as the best introduction to robust-first computing, where randomness and fault tolerance are built into the architecture.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"robust-first computing\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Algorithms & data structures\",\"Creative coding & generative art\"],\"tags\":[\"movable feast machine\",\"robustness\",\"cellular automata\",\"spatial computing\",\"sorting\"],\"quote\":\"it's certainly esoteric, but has some extremely important properties.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"htYh-Tq7ZBI\",\"title\":\"Why can't you multiply vectors?\",\"channel\":\"Freya Holmér\",\"summary\":\"Freya Holmer's Dutch Game Day 2023 talk asking why vectors can't be multiplied, building from complex numbers to a single axiom and arriving at geometric algebra, quaternions and multivectors.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the best entry point to geometric algebra and an intuitive route to understanding quaternions.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"geometric algebra\",\"categories\":[\"Pure mathematics\",\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"vector multiplication\",\"quaternions\",\"clifford algebra\",\"complex numbers\",\"game math\"],\"quote\":\"The absolute first video to watch should be the (ironically titled) Why can't you multiply vectors?\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"i6Fr65PFqfk\",\"title\":\"DjangoCon 2008 Keynote: Cal Henderson\",\"channel\":\"Google for Developers\",\"summary\":\"Cal Henderson's DjangoCon 2008 keynote, \\\"Why I Hate Django\\\", is a funny critique of Django from a Flickr architect. It touches on scaling topics such as database replication.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a brilliant, hilarious talk, though not the most educational.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"django web framework\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"django\",\"cal henderson\",\"flickr\",\"scaling\",\"keynote\",\"humor\"],\"quote\":\"a brilliant (and hilarious) talk\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"iFDe5kUUyT0\",\"title\":\"The IOU Machine: How Currency Is Created | Hidden Secrets of Money Ep. 4\",\"channel\":\"GoldSilver \",\"summary\":\"Episode 4 of Mike Maloney's Hidden Secrets of Money, explaining how currency is created through a chain of IOUs among the Treasury, banks and the Federal Reserve. It covers bonds, Fed checks, debt, taxes and fractional reserve lending, from a gold-and-silver advocate's viewpoint.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended, often in Bitcoin and monetary threads, as a clear introduction to how the US money system works, though it comes with a strong goldbug slant.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"how money is created\",\"categories\":[\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\",\"Investing & personal finance\"],\"tags\":[\"federal reserve\",\"fractional reserve\",\"fiat currency\",\"debt\",\"gold\"],\"quote\":\"This is the best explanation I've ever seen of how the Fed and US Dollar money printing actually works\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"iFTWM7HV2UI\",\"title\":\"We're building a dystopia just to make people click on ads | Zeynep Tufekci\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Technosociologist Zeynep Tufekci argues that the machine-learning systems used to serve ads also decide what political and social information people see. She explains how opaque algorithms can nudge, segment and manipulate users, and warns that the real danger is powerful people using AI to control populations.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it whenever engagement-optimizing feeds, micro-targeting or ad infrastructure come up, as an accessible warning about algorithmic amplification.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"algorithmic manipulation\",\"categories\":[\"Futurism & technology and society\",\"AI safety, AGI & impact\",\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\"],\"tags\":[\"ad targeting\",\"recommendation algorithms\",\"persuasion\",\"social media\",\"ai ethics\"],\"quote\":\"This TED talk[0] on persuasion architectures brings up some interesting moral arguments against this idea.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"iwcYp-XT7UI\",\"title\":\"George Hotz: Comma.ai, OpenPilot, and Autonomous Vehicles | Lex Fridman Podcast #31\",\"channel\":\"Lex Fridman\",\"summary\":\"Lex Fridman's two-hour podcast with George Hotz, founder of Comma.ai, covering openpilot, autonomous driving and Hotz's views on AI and technology.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it for Hotz's reasoning on cameras versus lidar and for an opinionated, original thinker's overview of comma and openpilot.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"self-driving cars\",\"categories\":[\"Self-driving & autonomous vehicles\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"george hotz\",\"comma.ai\",\"openpilot\",\"camera vs lidar\",\"autonomous vehicles\"],\"quote\":\"I strongly recommend Lex Firdman’s recent interview with George Hotz\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"jEbRVNxL44c\",\"title\":\"Zipline - How Rwanda Built A Drone Delivery Service\",\"channel\":\"Real Engineering\",\"summary\":\"Real Engineering explains how Zipline built a drone delivery network for medical supplies in Rwanda. It covers the fixed-wing drones, catapult launch, parachute drop and the logistics behind the service.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the best explanation of how Zipline's whole system works and as an example of drones doing real, useful work.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"drone delivery\",\"categories\":[\"Robotics\",\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"zipline\",\"drones\",\"rwanda\",\"medical logistics\",\"aviation\"],\"quote\":\"this video by Real Engineering that does a great job of explaining how the whole thing works\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"jN7mSXMruEo\",\"title\":\"These Stupid Trucks are Literally Killing Us\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes explains how SUVs and light trucks have grown in size and share of US sales, and why that is dangerous for pedestrians, cyclists and city life. He covers the regulatory loopholes, such as fuel economy rules and the chicken tax, that favored them.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is HN's default link whenever vehicle size comes up, with commenters recommending it for the history of the light-truck loophole and the safety effects of tall bumpers.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"SUVs and light truck regulation\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\"],\"tags\":[\"suvs\",\"pickup trucks\",\"road safety\",\"cafe standards\",\"urbanism\"],\"quote\":\"a very relevant video about the very real problems that SUVs and modern trucks have on urban environments.\",\"recommenders\":11,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"jcKRGpMiVTw\",\"title\":\"Ramanujan: Making sense of 1+2+3+... = -1/12 and Co.\",\"channel\":\"Mathologer\",\"summary\":\"Mathologer examines how identities like 1+2+3+... = -1/12 arise in Ramanujan's work, starting from his 1913 letter to Hardy. The video explains the rules for summing infinite series and why such results are meaningful but not naive sums.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a careful, honest treatment of the -1/12 claim, often preferred over flashier videos.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"divergent series\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"ramanujan\",\"infinite series\",\"-1/12\",\"zeta function\",\"mathematics\"],\"quote\":\"If you have half an hour to spare, I highly recommend to watch\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"kTiP0zKF9bc\",\"title\":\"Robocalypse Now: Using Deep Learning to Combat Cheating in Counter-Strike: Global Offensive\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"Valve's John McDonald presents at GDC 2018 on how Valve uses deep learning (VACnet) to detect cheaters in Counter-Strike: Global Offensive. He covers training on human-reviewed replays and how model output is confirmed by manual review.\",\"why_hn\":\"The go-to link in any anti-cheat thread for a concrete, well-delivered account of machine-learning cheat detection in production.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"machine learning anti-cheat\",\"categories\":[\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"Game programming & engines\",\"Gaming culture & analysis\"],\"tags\":[\"vacnet\",\"cs:go\",\"deep learning\",\"cheat detection\",\"valve\",\"gdc\"],\"quote\":\"The presenter delivered the problem and solution approach so well I'd still like to work on something like this some time.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"kc9HwsxE1OY\",\"title\":\"The Unreasonable Effectiveness of Multiple Dispatch | Stefan Karpinski | JuliaCon 2019\",\"channel\":\"The Julia Programming Language\",\"summary\":\"Julia co-creator Stefan Karpinski explains why multiple dispatch makes Julia code and types reusable across packages with so little friction. He covers sharing basic types and sharing generic algorithms.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as the definitive explanation, from a language designer, of why multiple dispatch makes Julia's composability work.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"multiple dispatch in julia\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"julia\",\"multiple dispatch\",\"code reuse\",\"composability\",\"language design\"],\"quote\":\"a talk by a core developer at JuliaCon 2019 titled 'The unreasonable effectiveness of multiple dispatch'\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"lkIicfzPBys\",\"title\":\"Stevey's Tech Talk E39 -- A guided tour of Emacs\",\"channel\":\"Steve Yegge\",\"summary\":\"Steve Yegge records himself working in Emacs and shows off about a dozen features in a roughly 45-minute screen capture. It is an informal, enthusiastic tour rather than a structured tutorial.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend it as an excellent, convincing overview of Emacs for newcomers and skeptics, shown by someone who clearly loves it.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"emacs\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"emacs\",\"text editors\",\"steve yegge\",\"screencast\",\"developer tools\"],\"quote\":\"Two videos by Steve Yegge should be enough to convince the most dyed-in-the-wool anti-Emacser to consider re-evaluating their position.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"niKblgZupOc\",\"title\":\"CGA Graphics - Not as bad as you thought!\",\"channel\":\"The 8-Bit Guy\",\"summary\":\"The 8-Bit Guy covers IBM's 1981 CGA graphics system, including how composite output and artifacting produced extra colors beyond the nominal palette. He argues CGA looked better in period than it does on RGB monitors.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it for showing how old developers exploited the smeary nature of composite signals and what CGA really looked like on period hardware.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cga graphics\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\"],\"tags\":[\"cga\",\"composite video\",\"artifacting\",\"retro computing\",\"ibm pc\"],\"quote\":\"The 8-Bit guy did a great piece on CGA that goes into detail about how old developers abused the fuzzy smeary nature of composite signals\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"oYk8CKH7OhE\",\"title\":\"Evan Czaplicki - Let's be mainstream! User focused design in Elm - Curry On\",\"channel\":\"Curry On!\",\"summary\":\"Evan Czaplicki, creator of Elm, talks at Curry On 2015 about user-focused language design. He explains the principles behind Elm and how to make a functional, typed language as approachable as JavaScript.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the clearest explanation of Elm's design thinking and of making functional languages mainstream, plus a popular monad explanation clip.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"elm language design\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Functional programming\",\"Web development\"],\"tags\":[\"elm\",\"functional programming\",\"language design\",\"monads\",\"front-end\"],\"quote\":\"He makes a strong case for Elm not as a language-to-rule-them-all, but as a tool for the average programmer\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"p7bzE1E5PMY\",\"title\":\"Visualization of Quantum Physics (Quantum Mechanics)\",\"channel\":\"udiprod\",\"summary\":\"An animated visualization of basic quantum mechanics using a free particle, with simulations based on the actual equations. It covers waves, particles, and how measurement collapses the wavefunction.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it one of the best visual explanations of the wave, particle and measurement relationship, free of silly metaphors.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"quantum mechanics\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum physics\"],\"tags\":[\"wavefunction collapse\",\"quantum waves\",\"physics animation\",\"measurement\",\"free particle\"],\"quote\":\"Highly recommended for anyone trying to grok the relationship between a wave, a particle, and an observation.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"p83QUZ1-P10\",\"title\":\"Amiga Hardware Programming 1 - Use the Assembler and wait for left click\",\"channel\":\"ScoopexUs\",\"summary\":\"The first video in Photon of Scoopex's Amiga hardware programming series. It shows how to set up an assembler and write a minimal 68000 program that waits for a left mouse click.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an approachable start to Amiga demo and 68000 assembly programming.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"amiga assembly programming\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\"],\"tags\":[\"amiga\",\"68000\",\"assembly\",\"demoscene\",\"winuae\"],\"quote\":\"you should fasten your seatbelts and check out this video tutorials by Photon of Scoopex\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"qd_hEja6bzE\",\"title\":\"Pulling Power from the Sky: The Story of Makani [Feature Film]\",\"channel\":\"X, The Moonshot Factory\",\"summary\":\"A feature-length film by the Makani team chronicling their thirteen-year effort to build energy kites that harvest wind power, with footage from test sites in Hawaii, California and Norway. It shows the engineering, the team and the eventual shutdown.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a technically rich, beautifully made documentary on ambitious hardware engineering and failure.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"airborne wind energy\",\"categories\":[\"Renewables, batteries & storage\",\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\",\"Startups & entrepreneurship\"],\"tags\":[\"makani\",\"energy kites\",\"hardware engineering\",\"x moonshot\",\"wind power\",\"startup failure\"],\"quote\":\"There's a beautiful feature film about the story of Makani. It's a great watch with plenty of technical details\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"rE3j_RHkqJc\",\"title\":\"Can You Watch This Without Getting Angry?\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey explains how ideas spread like germs, and why content that triggers outrage spreads fastest, using a study on what makes online content viral. He argues that opposing outrage-driven memes feed each other.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it constantly in threads on social media, polarization and outrage as the canonical explanation of why angry content wins.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"outrage and memes\",\"categories\":[\"Internet culture & memes\",\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Futurism & technology and society\"],\"tags\":[\"memes\",\"social media\",\"outrage\",\"virality\",\"polarization\"],\"quote\":\"Great TED talk on the original twitter kerfuffle referenced in the article\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"rhgwIhB58PA\",\"title\":\"The Biggest Myth In Education\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium debunks the idea of learning styles, such as being a visual learner, with an interview with Daniel Willingham and a memory experiment. It argues that a multimodal approach helps everyone.\",\"why_hn\":\"The standard link whenever someone claims to be a visual learner or learning styles come up, with sources listed in the description.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"learning styles myth\",\"categories\":[\"Education & learning\",\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"learning styles\",\"education\",\"psychology\",\"myths\",\"memory\"],\"quote\":\"Veritasium covered this in much greater detail but still approachable by the laymen\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"s_fkpZSnz2I\",\"title\":\"Irving Finkel | The Ark Before Noah: A Great Adventure\",\"channel\":\"The Institute for the Study of Ancient Cultures\",\"summary\":\"Irving Finkel of the British Museum tells how a 4,000-year-old cuneiform tablet came to him for examination. It revealed the oldest known account of Noah's Ark and led to a book and a documentary.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a funny, fascinating talk on cuneiform tablets and the ancient world by an engaging curator.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ancient mesopotamia\",\"categories\":[\"World history\",\"Religion, belief & skepticism\",\"Language & linguistics\"],\"tags\":[\"cuneiform\",\"noah's ark\",\"british museum\",\"clay tablets\",\"babylon\"],\"quote\":\"equal parts fascinating and hilarious\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"u9KNtnCZDMI\",\"title\":\"Alex Evans at Umbra Ignite 2015: Learning From Failure\",\"channel\":\"Media Molecule\",\"summary\":\"Media Molecule's Alex Evans recounts the journey, including the failures, of finding the Dreams engine, based on his Siggraph 2015 talk. It covers the signed-distance-field and splat-based rendering approach and the artistic goals behind it.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN calls it an inspirational must-see on SDF rendering and a favorite tech talk for graphics programmers.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"signed distance field rendering\",\"categories\":[\"Computer graphics & rendering\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"dreams\",\"sdf\",\"media molecule\",\"siggraph\",\"graphics\",\"game engine\"],\"quote\":\"the Dreams presentation which is an inspirational must see\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"ue-1JoJQaEg\",\"title\":\"Old pinball machines are amazingly complex\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"Technology Connections takes a long, detailed look at how a 1970s electromechanical pinball machine works, from relays to stepper motors and mechanical logic. It is the first part of a series.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points people to it as a deep dive showing how complex logic was built without computers.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"electromechanical pinball machines\",\"categories\":[\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Game history & retro gaming\"],\"tags\":[\"pinball\",\"relays\",\"stepper motors\",\"mechanical logic\",\"technology connections\"],\"quote\":\"I shared it with everyone I know, and now I'm sharing it with you\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"v1WiCGq-PcY\",\"title\":\"Bryan Cranston's Advice to Aspiring Actors\",\"channel\":\"Oscars\",\"summary\":\"A one-minute clip of Bryan Cranston at an Academy new-member reception, advising actors to approach auditions without desperation and focus on solving the problem rather than landing the job.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN applies it to job interviews and confidence, recommending the mindset of not interviewing from a place of desperation.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"interview mindset\",\"categories\":[\"Productivity & self-improvement\",\"Arts & entertainment (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"bryan cranston\",\"auditions\",\"job interviews\",\"confidence\",\"advice\"],\"quote\":\"Nobody explains this better than Bryan Cranston (possibly one of the most successful TV actors of recent times)\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"vgT4Y30DkaA\",\"title\":\"Threads (1984) ORIGINAL TRAILER [HD 1080p]\",\"channel\":\"HD Retro Trailers\",\"summary\":\"The original trailer for Threads, the 1984 British TV film depicting the aftermath of a nuclear attack on Sheffield. The trailer hints at the film's bleak tone.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters use it to point people to Threads, which they call the most harrowing depiction of nuclear war and say should be mandatory viewing.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nuclear war film\",\"categories\":[\"Film & TV clips\",\"Military history & warfare\"],\"tags\":[\"threads\",\"nuclear war\",\"1984 film\",\"trailer\",\"british cinema\"],\"quote\":\"should be mandatory viewing for all people in positions of power\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"w3WYdYyjek4\",\"title\":\"TigerStyle! (Or How To Design Safer Systems in Less Time) by Joran Dirk Greef\",\"channel\":\"TigerBeetle\",\"summary\":\"Joran Dirk Greef's Systems Distributed '23 talk on TigerStyle, the engineering methodology behind TigerBeetle. It covers designing safer systems in less time through up-front design, assertions, and deterministic simulation.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters, including TigerBeetle people, recommend it as the explanation of a methodology for building defect-free, high-assurance software.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"safe systems engineering\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Databases & storage\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"tigerstyle\",\"tigerbeetle\",\"zig\",\"software correctness\",\"deterministic simulation\"],\"quote\":\"The TigerStyle video is well worth watching for anyone who wants to build defect-free software systems\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"wGt4XwBbCvA\",\"title\":\"Jancovici : Can we save energy, jobs and growth at the same time ? 08/01/2018\",\"channel\":\"Jean-Marc Jancovici\",\"summary\":\"Jean-Marc Jancovici lectures at ENS Paris on whether energy use, jobs and growth can all be preserved. He argues for a physical approach to economics, since resource depletion and climate limits make perpetual GDP growth unrealistic.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a sober, physics-based account of how energy underlies the economy.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"energy and economic growth\",\"categories\":[\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\",\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"energy\",\"gdp\",\"climate\",\"nuclear\",\"peak oil\"],\"quote\":\"this doc illustrates the enormous realities of energy challenges with sober clarity often exposed by Jancovici\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"wJyUtbn0O5Y\",\"title\":\"The Inner Life of the Cell Animation\",\"channel\":\"XVIVO Scientific Animation\",\"summary\":\"An award-winning 3D animation by XVIVO for Harvard's BioVisions showing the molecular machinery inside a cell as a white blood cell responds to a stimulus.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a jaw-dropping view of the complexity and machine-like nature of cells.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cell biology animation\",\"categories\":[\"Biology & biotechnology\",\"Animation & visual effects\"],\"tags\":[\"cell biology\",\"molecular animation\",\"white blood cell\",\"motor proteins\",\"harvard biovisions\"],\"quote\":\"For anyone who hasn't already seen it, I highly recommend The Inner Life of a Cell\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"z_dt7NG38V4\",\"title\":\"Eric Bier Demonstrates Cedar\",\"channel\":\"Computer History Museum\",\"summary\":\"Eric Bier, a PARC research scientist, demonstrates the Cedar integrated programming environment and language, an evolution of Mesa from Xerox PARC. The demo shows the Tioga editor, garbage collection, and a full graphical workstation environment.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN keeps citing it as proof of how advanced a GC-based systems language and integrated workstation were decades ago, and as a model for what desktop OSes could be.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"xerox parc cedar environment\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"GUI, HCI & interface history\"],\"tags\":[\"cedar\",\"mesa\",\"tioga editor\",\"xerox parc\",\"computing history\",\"garbage collection\"],\"quote\":\"Be prepared to be amazed with what Xerox PARC and others were doing\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"zrpUDuUtxPM\",\"title\":\"Floyd Toole - Sound reproduction – art and science/opinions and facts\",\"channel\":\"CIRMMT\",\"summary\":\"Floyd Toole's CIRMMT distinguished lecture on the science of sound reproduction, drawing on decades of blind listening research. It covers what listeners prefer in loudspeakers and why measured accuracy and anechoic response matter.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a crash course on audio reproduction science, especially the finding that listeners reliably prefer more accurate speakers in blind tests.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"loudspeaker sound reproduction\",\"categories\":[\"Audio & video engineering\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"floyd toole\",\"blind listening tests\",\"speaker measurements\",\"psychoacoustics\",\"equalization\"],\"quote\":\"In blind testing, the average listener reliably prefers the more accurate speaker.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.5},{\"vid\":\"1e8VZlPBx_0\",\"title\":\"How to Invent the Future II - Stanford CS183F: Startup School\",\"channel\":\"Stanford Online\",\"summary\":\"Part two of Alan Kay's Stanford Startup School lecture on inventing the future. He draws on his time at Xerox PARC and elsewhere to discuss how to think about new technology and what makes ideas useful.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently suggested in Ask HN threads on finding ideas, creativity and why incumbents like Xerox missed what they invented.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"inventing the future\",\"categories\":[\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Startups & entrepreneurship\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"alan kay\",\"xerox parc\",\"innovation\",\"startups\",\"computing history\"],\"quote\":\"I also recommend Alan Kay's video series on \\\"How To Invent The Future\\\".\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"2XLZ4Z8LpEE\",\"title\":\"Using a 1930 Teletype as a Linux Terminal\",\"channel\":\"CuriousMarc\",\"summary\":\"CuriousMarc connects a restored 1930 Model 15 Teletype to a Linux machine and uses it as a terminal, after commenters requested it. The video shows the challenges of linking a 5-bit mechanical device to a modern OS.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters love it as a vivid demonstration of terminal history, explaining why tools like ed and the tty echo model work the way they do.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"teletype as linux terminal\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"History of computing\",\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\"],\"tags\":[\"teletype\",\"retro computing\",\"unix history\",\"terminals\",\"restoration\"],\"quote\":\"It's one of the best things on the Internet, IMO.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"5MgBikgcWnY\",\"title\":\"The first 20 hours -- how to learn anything | Josh Kaufman | TEDxCSU\",\"channel\":\"TEDx Talks\",\"summary\":\"Josh Kaufman's TEDx talk argues that you can reach practical competence in almost any skill with about 20 hours of focused practice, rather than the 10,000 hours often quoted. He describes how becoming a parent led him to rethink how adults learn, and lays out a short method for rapid skill acquisition.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as the standard counterpoint to the 10,000-hour rule and as an encouraging recipe for starting new skills.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"rapid skill acquisition\",\"categories\":[\"Productivity & self-improvement\",\"Education & learning\"],\"tags\":[\"20 hours\",\"learning\",\"deliberate practice\",\"10000 hour rule\",\"self-improvement\"],\"quote\":\"the threshold for acquiring new skills for personal or professional use is somewhere around 20 hours.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"5eo8nz_niiM\",\"title\":\"Over 200,000 Servers in One Place! Visiting Hetzner in Falkenstein (Germany)\",\"channel\":\"der8auer EN\",\"summary\":\"Overclocker der8auer tours Hetzner's Falkenstein data center in Germany, showing custom racks, own mainboards, passively cooled servers and ATX-style cases on shelves.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it whenever Hetzner comes up, as the best look inside their unusual, semi-custom and low-cost data center setup.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"data center tour\",\"categories\":[\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\"],\"tags\":[\"hetzner\",\"data center\",\"server hardware\",\"custom racks\",\"hosting\"],\"quote\":\"There's a great YouTube tour of one of their data centres here\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"5uljtqyBLxI\",\"title\":\"Java on a 1000 Cores - Tales of Hardware / Software CoDesign\",\"channel\":\"Google TechTalks\",\"summary\":\"Cliff Click of Azul Systems gives a Google Tech Talk on running Java business applications on a machine with about a thousand cores. He covers hardware and software codesign, including garbage collection hardware and the JIT.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats Cliff Click's talks as an excellent source on JVMs, garbage collection and hardware-assisted Java.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"jvm and hardware codesign\",\"categories\":[\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\"],\"tags\":[\"java\",\"garbage collection\",\"azul\",\"multicore\",\"jit compilers\"],\"quote\":\"Anything from Cliff Click is quite good.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"6orsmFndx_o\",\"title\":\"Ivan Sutherland Sketchpad Demo 1963\",\"channel\":\"Interface Studies\",\"summary\":\"A TV show about Ivan Sutherland's 1963 MIT thesis software Sketchpad, which ran on the TX-2 computer. It shows a light pen drawing, constraints, snapping, scaling and object-style graphics, foundations of GUIs and CAD.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it as a striking demonstration that core GUI, CAD and constraint-programming ideas existed in the 1960s.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of graphical interfaces\",\"categories\":[\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"History of computing\",\"Computer graphics & rendering\"],\"tags\":[\"sketchpad\",\"ivan sutherland\",\"cad\",\"constraints\",\"light pen\",\"hci history\"],\"quote\":\"It blows me away that this kind of UI was even conceived in the 1960s.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"79fvDDPaIoY\",\"title\":\"Bryan Cantrill from Joyent on Manta: internet-facing object storage facility that features compute\",\"channel\":\"AI Council\",\"summary\":\"Bryan Cantrill of Joyent presents Manta, an internet-facing object store that lets you run compute directly on the data. The talk is peppered with ZFS history and Cantrill's trademark commentary on the industry.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends Cantrill talks generally for their mix of industry history, technical depth and entertaining delivery, and this one is a favorite for storage and data integrity threads.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"object storage with compute\",\"categories\":[\"Databases & storage\",\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\",\"Operating systems & kernels\"],\"tags\":[\"manta\",\"bryan cantrill\",\"zfs\",\"joyent\",\"data integrity\",\"oracle\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend any talk you can find of Bryan Cantrill. A wonderful mix of industry history and great delivery.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"9FdHq3WfJgs\",\"title\":\"DEF CON 23 - Chris Rock - I Will Kill You\",\"channel\":\"DEFCONConference\",\"summary\":\"A DEF CON 23 talk showing how weak controls in electronic death registration systems let someone virtually kill a person and obtain a real death certificate, with consequences for their identity and life.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it whenever fake deaths, identity verification or records fraud come up, praising how easy the attack seemed.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"death certificate fraud\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\"],\"tags\":[\"identity theft\",\"def con\",\"social engineering\",\"records security\",\"fraud\"],\"quote\":\"Really eye opening, and potentially devastating if it's done to you.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"9PK-netuhHA\",\"title\":\"The Lottery: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)\",\"channel\":\"LastWeekTonight\",\"summary\":\"John Oliver's Last Week Tonight segment on state lotteries. It argues that the claim that lotteries fund education is misleading, because the money is fungible and often diverted to general funds.\",\"why_hn\":\"Go-to reference in lottery threads for the point that earmarked lottery revenue does not really add to school funding.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"state lotteries\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\",\"Satire & parody\"],\"tags\":[\"lottery\",\"john oliver\",\"public funding\",\"education funding\",\"regressive tax\"],\"quote\":\"this video is worth watching.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"A9UmdY0E8hU\",\"title\":\"Where in the world is it easiest to get rich? | Harald Eia | TEDxOslo\",\"channel\":\"TEDx Talks\",\"summary\":\"Sociologist and comedian Harald Eia argues in a TEDxOslo talk that Nordic social democracies produce more rich people per capita than the US or UK. He credits high taxes, welfare and strong unions for creating a safety net that enables risk taking.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as a counterintuitive data point in debates on welfare states, social mobility and entrepreneurship.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nordic wealth and social mobility\",\"categories\":[\"Labor, work & inequality\",\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\",\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\"],\"tags\":[\"nordic model\",\"social mobility\",\"welfare state\",\"millionaires per capita\",\"economics\"],\"quote\":\"In these kinds of discussions I like to promote this interesting TEDx talk\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0}",
